    "questions": [
      {
        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is Jenkins?",
        "answer": "Jenkins is an open-source automation server primarily used to implement Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery (CI/CD) pipelines. It automates activities such as source-code checkout, compilation, unit testing, code-quality analysis, packaging, and deployment. Jenkins integrates with tools such as Git, Maven, Gradle, Docker, Kubernetes, SonarQube, and artifact repositories. For example, when a developer pushes code to GitHub, Jenkins can automatically trigger a pipeline, build the application, execute tests, create an artifact, and deploy it to a test environment."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "What is Continuous Integration (CI)?",
        "answer": "Continuous Integration is a software development practice in which developers frequently merge code changes into a shared repository. Each change is validated through automated activities such as compilation, unit testing, and static code analysis. Jenkins can automatically detect repository changes and execute these validation steps. CI helps teams identify integration problems early instead of discovering them near the end of a release cycle."
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "question": "What is Continuous Delivery (CD)?",
        "answer": "Continuous Delivery is the practice of keeping application changes in a deployable state through automated build, testing, and release preparation processes. Jenkins can implement this workflow by building application artifacts, executing automated tests, and deploying to staging environments. A production release may still require an authorized approval. The objective is to make software releases predictable, repeatable, and lower risk."
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "question": "What is the difference between Continuous Delivery and Continuous Deployment?",
        "answer": "Continuous Delivery ensures that validated software can be released whenever the organization chooses, usually with a manual approval before production deployment. Continuous Deployment automatically releases every change that passes the required pipeline checks into production. Both practices rely on automated testing and reliable deployment processes. Organizations choose between them based on business risk, regulatory requirements, and confidence in their automated validation."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins Controller?",
        "answer": "The Jenkins Controller is the central component responsible for managing the Jenkins environment. It provides the web interface, stores job and pipeline configuration, schedules builds, manages agents, and coordinates execution. In a distributed Jenkins architecture, the controller assigns work to connected agents rather than performing every build itself. Production environments should protect controller availability, credentials, configuration backups, and administrative access."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins Agent?",
        "answer": "A Jenkins Agent is a machine or execution environment that runs build tasks assigned by the Jenkins Controller. Agents can run on virtual machines, physical servers, containers, or Kubernetes Pods. They provide the operating-system tools, runtime dependencies, and resources required by builds. Organizations use multiple agents to distribute workloads and isolate different build environments."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "What is the difference between a Jenkins Controller and an Agent?",
        "answer": "The Controller coordinates Jenkins operations, manages job configuration, schedules work, and communicates with agents. An Agent executes the actual build steps assigned to it, such as checking out code, compiling applications, running tests, and creating packages. Separating these responsibilities improves scalability and reduces resource pressure on the controller. In production, agents are commonly selected according to workload requirements and security boundaries."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins Executor?",
        "answer": "An executor is a logical execution slot on a Jenkins controller or agent that allows Jenkins to run a build task. The number of executors determines how many tasks can run concurrently on that node. Increasing executor count without sufficient CPU, memory, or disk capacity can cause build performance problems. Administrators should configure executor capacity based on workload characteristics rather than simply maximizing concurrency."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins Job?",
        "answer": "A Jenkins Job is a configured automation task that Jenkins can execute. A job may perform activities such as checking out source code, compiling an application, running tests, creating artifacts, or deploying software. Jenkins supports different job types, including Freestyle projects, Pipeline jobs, and Multibranch Pipeline jobs. Teams configure jobs according to the complexity and automation requirements of their projects."
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "question": "What is a Freestyle Project in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "A Freestyle Project is a traditional Jenkins job type configured through the Jenkins web interface. It allows administrators to define source-code management, build triggers, build steps, environment settings, and post-build actions. It is suitable for straightforward automation tasks. For complex pipelines, Jenkins Pipeline is generally more maintainable because its workflow can be stored as code in a version-controlled Jenkinsfile."
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins Pipeline?",
        "answer": "A Jenkins Pipeline is a collection of automated steps that define the software delivery workflow. It can include source checkout, compilation, testing, security scanning, packaging, artifact publishing, and deployment. Pipeline execution can be controlled through stages, conditions, parallel branches, and post-build actions. Storing the pipeline definition in source control helps teams review, version, and maintain their CI/CD process."
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "question": "What is a Jenkinsfile?",
        "answer": "A Jenkinsfile is a text file that defines a Jenkins Pipeline using the Pipeline syntax. It is commonly stored in the root directory of an application's source-code repository. When Jenkins checks out the repository, it can read the Jenkinsfile and execute the defined workflow. Keeping pipeline definitions alongside application code makes pipeline changes traceable and allows teams to review them through normal code-review practices."
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "question": "What is Declarative Pipeline in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "Declarative Pipeline is a structured Jenkins Pipeline syntax that uses predefined sections such as pipeline, agent, stages, steps, environment, options, parameters, and post. It provides a consistent format that is easier for teams to read and maintain. Declarative syntax supports common delivery workflows without requiring every pipeline to be written as unrestricted Groovy code. It is often preferred when a pipeline can be expressed through its structured model."
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "question": "What is Scripted Pipeline in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "Scripted Pipeline is a flexible Jenkins Pipeline approach written using Groovy-based syntax. It provides programming constructs such as variables, loops, conditionals, and exception handling for complex automation logic. This flexibility can be useful when a workflow requires dynamic behavior. However, teams should keep scripts understandable and maintainable because excessive custom logic can make pipeline behavior difficult to troubleshoot."
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "question": "What is the difference between Declarative and Scripted Pipeline?",
        "answer": "Declarative Pipeline uses a structured syntax with predefined sections and validation rules, making common CI/CD workflows easier to read and maintain. Scripted Pipeline provides more direct Groovy programming flexibility for advanced or dynamic workflows. Declarative pipelines are generally a good starting point for standard delivery processes, while Scripted pipelines may be useful for specialized logic. Both execute through Jenkins Pipeline infrastructure and can use shared pipeline capabilities."
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "question": "What is a Stage in Jenkins Pipeline?",
        "answer": "A Stage is a logical section of a Jenkins Pipeline that represents a major phase of the delivery process. Common stages include Checkout, Build, Unit Test, Code Quality, Docker Build, and Deployment. Stages make pipeline execution easier to understand because Jenkins displays their status and duration. Separating work into meaningful stages also helps teams identify where a build failed."
      },
      {
        "number": 17,
        "question": "What is a Step in Jenkins Pipeline?",
        "answer": "A Step is an individual operation executed inside a Pipeline stage. Examples include sh for running shell commands, bat for Windows batch commands, echo for printing messages, checkout for retrieving source code, and junit for publishing test results. Steps are the building blocks of a pipeline's execution logic. A stage normally contains one or more steps that perform a related activity."
      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "question": "What is the post section in a Jenkins Declarative Pipeline?",
        "answer": "The post section defines actions that Jenkins performs after pipeline execution or after a particular stage completes. Conditions include always, success, failure, unstable, and changed, depending on the desired behavior. Teams commonly use post actions to publish test reports, send notifications, clean temporary resources, or record deployment status. Cleanup should be designed carefully so it does not remove evidence required for incident investigation."
      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "What is SCM in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "Source Code Management is the process of managing application source code through a version-control system. Jenkins integrates with systems such as Git to retrieve the source code required for a build. A pipeline can specify the repository URL, branch, credentials, and checkout behavior. Using SCM integration ensures that each build can be associated with a particular source revision."
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "question": "How does Jenkins integrate with Git?",
        "answer": "Jenkins integrates with Git through its Git-related plugins and source-control configuration. A job or pipeline specifies the repository URL and the branch or revision to retrieve. Jenkins can use configured credentials for private repositories and can trigger builds through webhooks or polling. The checked-out commit should be recorded so that build failures and deployed artifacts can be traced to the exact source revision."
      },
      {
        "number": 21,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins Build Trigger?",
        "answer": "A build trigger defines the event or schedule that starts a Jenkins job or pipeline. Common triggers include repository webhooks, scheduled execution, SCM polling, upstream job completion, and manual user initiation. For example, a GitHub webhook can notify Jenkins when a developer pushes a commit. Choosing an appropriate trigger helps avoid unnecessary builds while ensuring important source changes are validated promptly."
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "question": "What is a Webhook in Jenkins integration?",
        "answer": "A webhook is an HTTP callback sent by an external system when a specific event occurs. In CI/CD, a source-control platform can send a webhook to Jenkins after a push or pull-request event. Jenkins processes the notification and triggers the configured job when the event matches its configuration. Webhooks reduce the delay associated with periodic repository polling and require appropriate endpoint security and network connectivity."
      },
      {
        "number": 23,
        "question": "What is the Jenkins Workspace?",
        "answer": "A Jenkins workspace is a directory on the controller or agent where a job performs its operations. It commonly contains checked-out source code, temporary build files, test output, and generated artifacts during execution. Each job uses a workspace associated with the node where its build runs. Workspace cleanup and storage management are important because accumulated files can consume disk space and affect future builds."
      },
      {
        "number": 24,
        "question": "What is an Artifact in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "A build artifact is an output generated by the software build process, such as a JAR file, WAR file, ZIP package, binary, or container image. Jenkins can archive selected files so they can be accessed after a build completes. In mature CI/CD workflows, artifacts are often published to repositories such as Nexus, Artifactory, or container registries. The same tested artifact should be promoted between environments rather than rebuilt differently for each deployment."
      },
      {
        "number": 25,
        "question": "What is Artifact Archiving in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "Artifact archiving stores selected build outputs as part of a Jenkins build record. The archive step identifies files using workspace-relative patterns and makes matching files available through the Jenkins interface. This is useful for retaining packages, reports, and other outputs associated with a specific build. Jenkins artifact archiving is not necessarily a replacement for a dedicated artifact repository, especially when long-term retention and distribution are required."
      },
      {
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What are Jenkins Credentials?",
        "answer": "Jenkins Credentials provide a managed way to store sensitive information required by automation jobs. Examples include usernames, passwords, SSH private keys, API tokens, and secret text. Pipelines can retrieve credentials through supported credential bindings instead of hardcoding secrets in source files. Access should be restricted through appropriate permissions, and logs must be prevented from exposing sensitive values."
      },
      {
        "number": 27,
        "question": "What is the Jenkins Credentials Store?",
        "answer": "The Jenkins Credentials Store manages credentials that jobs and pipeline steps can use when accessing external systems. Credentials can be organized into scopes and domains depending on the Jenkins configuration and plugins. Administrators control who can create, update, and use stored credentials. Production teams should periodically review credential ownership, rotate secrets, and remove credentials that are no longer required."
      },
      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "What are Jenkins Plugins?",
        "answer": "Jenkins Plugins extend the platform with integrations and additional capabilities. Plugins provide functionality for source control, build tools, notifications, credentials, cloud agents, test reporting, and deployment platforms. For example, Git integration supports repository checkout, while Kubernetes integration can provision build agents dynamically. Plugin versions and dependencies should be managed carefully because incompatible or vulnerable plugins can affect Jenkins stability and security."
      },
      {
        "number": 29,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins Parameterized Build?",
        "answer": "A parameterized build allows users or automation systems to provide input values when starting a job. Parameters can represent values such as environment names, application versions, deployment regions, or feature selections. Pipeline logic can use these values to control execution. Parameters should be validated before use, especially when they influence deployment targets or shell commands, to avoid accidental production changes and command-injection risks."
      },
      {
        "number": 30,
        "question": "What are Environment Variables in Jenkins Pipeline?",
        "answer": "Environment variables provide configuration values that pipeline steps and application commands can access during execution. Jenkins supports built-in environment variables and pipeline-defined values through environment configuration or supported scripting features. Examples include build identifiers, workspace paths, and deployment environment names. Sensitive values should be supplied through the Credentials system rather than stored as ordinary environment variables in source-controlled pipeline files."
      },
      {
        "number": 31,
        "question": "What is a Multibranch Pipeline in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "A Multibranch Pipeline automatically discovers and manages pipeline jobs for branches and supported change requests in a source-control repository. Jenkins checks each relevant branch for a Jenkinsfile and creates a corresponding branch-specific pipeline. This supports workflows where feature branches, release branches, and pull requests require independent validation. Branch discovery and build behavior depend on the configured source-control provider and branch-source plugin."
      },
      {
        "number": 32,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins Shared Library?",
        "answer": "A Jenkins Shared Library is reusable pipeline code that can be maintained separately and imported by multiple Jenkinsfiles. It helps organizations standardize common operations such as building applications, running tests, publishing artifacts, and deploying services. Shared libraries reduce duplicated pipeline logic and make centralized improvements possible. They should be version-controlled, reviewed, and tested because a change to shared code can affect many pipelines."
      },
      {
        "number": 33,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins Build Queue?",
        "answer": "The Jenkins Build Queue contains tasks waiting for an available executor or for other scheduling requirements to be satisfied. A task may remain queued because all suitable executors are busy, no agent matches its label, or required resources are unavailable. Administrators investigate queue details and node availability to identify scheduling bottlenecks. Increasing executor capacity is not always the correct solution if the underlying agents lack sufficient resources."
      },
      {
        "number": 34,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins Label?",
        "answer": "A Jenkins label identifies a controller or agent node, or a group of nodes, for scheduling purposes. A pipeline can request an agent using a label that represents a capability such as Linux, Windows, Docker, or a particular toolchain. Jenkins schedules the workload on an eligible node matching the label expression. Accurate labels help route builds to the correct operating system and execution environment."
      },
      {
        "number": 35,
        "question": "What is Jenkins JUnit test reporting?",
        "answer": "Jenkins can publish automated test results in JUnit XML format using the junit pipeline step or related job configuration. The test report records information such as passed tests, failed tests, skipped tests, and execution details. Jenkins displays test trends and identifies failures across builds, helping teams detect quality regressions. The test command must generate valid report files, and the pipeline must publish the correct workspace paths."
      }
