        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is Git?",
        "answer": "Git is a distributed version control system used to track changes in source code and other project files. It helps developers maintain a history of their work, identify who made a change, and return to an earlier version when something goes wrong.\n\nGit is commonly used in software development projects where multiple developers work on the same application. Each developer can maintain a local copy of the repository, make changes, and share those changes with the team through a remote repository such as GitHub, GitLab, or Bitbucket.\n\nFor example, consider a team developing an online shopping application. One developer works on the login functionality while another works on the payment module. Git helps both developers manage their changes separately and combine their work without manually copying files between systems."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "Why is Git used in software development?",
        "answer": "Git is used to manage changes in project files and maintain a reliable history of development work. Without version control, developers may overwrite one another's code, lose important changes, or find it difficult to identify when a problem was introduced.\n\nGit allows developers to create branches, commit changes, compare versions, and merge work from different team members. It also supports collaboration through remote repositories, where developers can share their code and review changes before they become part of the main project.\n\nFor example, if a developer introduces a bug in a new application feature, the team can use Git history to identify the commit that introduced the issue. They can then correct the change or restore a previous working version."
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "question": "What is a Git repository?",
        "answer": "A Git repository is a location where Git tracks project files and stores their version history. It contains the current project content along with the information required to identify previous commits, branches, and other Git objects.\n\nA repository can be created locally on a developer's computer or hosted remotely on platforms such as GitHub and GitLab. Developers use the local repository to make changes and commits, then share their work with the remote repository.\n\nFor example, when a developer creates a repository for a Java application, Git can track source files, configuration files, and documentation. Whenever the developer commits changes, Git records a new point in the project's history."
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "question": "What is the difference between Git and GitHub?",
        "answer": "Git is a version control system that tracks changes in files and manages development history. It is software that developers can install and use on their computers without depending on a hosting website.\n\nGitHub is a web-based platform that hosts Git repositories and provides collaboration features such as pull requests, code reviews, issue tracking, and project automation. GitHub uses Git to manage repository history, but it provides additional services around team collaboration.\n\nFor example, a developer can use Git commands on a laptop to commit application changes. The developer can then push those commits to a GitHub repository so other team members can access the code and review the changes."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "question": "What is a distributed version control system?",
        "answer": "A distributed version control system stores a complete repository history in each developer's local repository. Developers can work with commits and branches locally without continuously connecting to a central server.\n\nGit follows this distributed model. A developer can create commits, inspect previous versions, compare changes, and create branches even when the internet connection is unavailable. A remote repository is used when the developer needs to share changes with other team members.\n\nFor example, a developer travelling without internet access can continue working on a feature and commit the changes locally. When internet access becomes available, the developer can push those commits to the team's shared repository."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git init command?",
        "answer": "The git init command creates a new Git repository in a directory. It initializes the repository metadata required for Git to track changes in the project. After initialization, developers can add files, create commits, and manage branches.\n\nFor example, when starting a new application project, a developer can open the project directory in a terminal and run git init. Git creates a hidden .git directory that stores repository information.\n\nThe command initializes version control locally. It does not automatically upload the project to GitHub or any other remote hosting platform. A remote repository must be configured separately if the developer wants to share the project."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git clone command?",
        "answer": "The git clone command creates a local copy of an existing remote Git repository. It downloads the project files and repository history so the developer can work on the project from their own computer.\n\nTeams commonly use git clone when a new developer joins a project or when an engineer needs to work with an existing application repository. The cloned repository normally includes the remote repository configuration, allowing the developer to pull and push changes when permissions allow.\n\nFor example, a developer can run git clone followed by the repository URL to download the team's application source code. The developer can then enter the downloaded directory, create a branch, and start making changes."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "What is the difference between git init and git clone?",
        "answer": "The git init command creates a new empty Git repository in an existing project directory. It is generally used when a developer is starting version control for a project that does not already have Git repository history.\n\nThe git clone command copies an existing repository, including its tracked files and commit history, from a local or remote source. It is normally used when developers need to obtain an existing project.\n\nFor example, if a developer creates a new application from scratch, git init can initialize its repository. If the developer joins an existing company project hosted on GitHub, git clone can download that repository and its history."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git status command?",
        "answer": "The git status command displays the current state of a Git working directory and staging area. It helps developers understand which files are modified, which files are new, which changes are staged, and whether the local branch has commits that are not yet synchronized with its upstream branch.\n\nDevelopers commonly run git status before adding files or creating a commit. This helps them avoid committing unwanted files and confirms whether all intended changes have been included.\n\nFor example, after editing an application configuration file and creating a new script, a developer can run git status to see both changes. Git will show whether those files are untracked, modified, or already staged."
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git add command?",
        "answer": "The git add command places changes into the staging area so they can be included in the next commit. Git separates the working directory from the staging area, allowing developers to choose exactly which changes should be recorded.\n\nFor example, suppose a developer modifies three files but wants to commit only the application source file. The developer can run git add on that specific file instead of staging every change. The staged version becomes the content Git will include when the next commit is created.\n\nA common command is git add filename. Developers can also use git add . to stage changes under the current directory, but they should review the staged content before committing."
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "question": "What is the Git staging area?",
        "answer": "The staging area is an intermediate area where developers prepare changes before creating a commit. It allows developers to select which file changes or portions of files should become part of the next recorded version.\n\nThis is useful when multiple changes are made during one development session but should be organized into separate commits. Developers can stage related changes together and leave unrelated work out of the commit.\n\nFor example, a developer may update a login page and also modify a database configuration file. If these changes represent separate tasks, the developer can stage and commit them separately. This creates a cleaner project history and makes future reviews easier."
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "question": "What is a Git commit?",
        "answer": "A Git commit is a recorded snapshot of the staged changes in a repository. Each commit has a unique identifier and includes information such as the author, timestamp, commit message, and reference to its parent commit or commits.\n\nDevelopers create commits to save meaningful units of work, such as adding a login feature, correcting a production bug, or updating deployment configuration. A commit does not automatically upload changes to a remote repository; it records them in the local repository.\n\nFor example, after updating an application's authentication logic, a developer can stage the required files and create a commit with a message explaining the change. The team can later inspect that commit to understand what was modified."
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "question": "Why are meaningful Git commit messages important?",
        "answer": "A commit message explains the purpose of a recorded change. Clear messages help developers, reviewers, and operations teams understand why a modification was made without opening every changed file.\n\nIn a professional project, commit messages should describe the actual work, such as fixing a login timeout or updating a deployment configuration. Messages such as update, test, or changes are less useful because they do not explain the reason for the modification.\n\nFor example, a message like Fix database connection timeout is more informative than simply writing changes. During a production incident, meaningful commit history can help engineers quickly identify the change associated with a new issue."
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "question": "What is a Git branch?",
        "answer": "A Git branch is a movable reference to a commit that provides a separate line of development. Developers use branches to work on features, bug fixes, and experiments without immediately changing the main development branch.\n\nFor example, a team may maintain a main branch for stable application code and create a feature branch for a new payment feature. The developer can make multiple commits on the feature branch and later merge the completed work into the target branch after review.\n\nBranches make parallel development easier and help teams organize changes. They are lightweight in Git, so creating a branch does not require copying the entire project directory."
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "question": "What is the difference between the main branch and a feature branch?",
        "answer": "The main branch usually represents the primary development or release line of a repository. Teams often protect it with review requirements and automated checks so that unverified changes are not merged directly.\n\nA feature branch is created for a particular development task, such as adding a new API or fixing a defect. Developers make changes and commits on that branch, then request a review before merging the work into the main branch.\n\nFor example, a developer working on a customer registration feature can create a branch named feature/customer-registration. The feature can be developed and tested separately while other developers continue working on different branches."
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git branch command?",
        "answer": "The git branch command is used to list, create, or manage local branches. Running git branch without additional options displays the local branches and identifies the currently checked-out branch.\n\nDevelopers create branches to separate different tasks and keep unfinished changes away from the main development line. For example, git branch feature/login creates a new branch named feature/login from the current commit.\n\nThe command does not automatically switch the working directory to the new branch. Developers must switch to it separately, for example by using git switch feature/login."
      },
      {
        "number": 17,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git switch command?",
        "answer": "The git switch command changes the currently checked-out branch. It updates the working directory to reflect the files and commit associated with the selected branch, subject to Git's rules for protecting local changes.\n\nFor example, a developer working on a bug fix may need to move to another feature branch. The developer can use git switch followed by the target branch name to change branches.\n\nThe command can also create and switch to a new branch using git switch -c branch-name. Before switching, developers should understand the state of their uncommitted changes to avoid confusion or conflicts."
      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "question": "What is the difference between git switch and git checkout?",
        "answer": "Both git switch and git checkout can be used to change branches. However, git switch was introduced to provide a more focused command for branch switching and branch creation.\n\nThe older git checkout command supports several different operations, including switching branches and restoring files. Because it performs multiple types of tasks, its behavior can be less obvious to beginners. Git provides git restore for many file-restoration operations.\n\nFor example, git switch main changes to the main branch, while git switch -c feature/payment creates and switches to a new branch. Using the focused commands makes everyday Git operations easier to understand."
      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "What is a remote repository in Git?",
        "answer": "A remote repository is a version of a Git repository hosted at another location, commonly on a platform such as GitHub, GitLab, or Bitbucket. It allows multiple developers and automation systems to share commits and branches.\n\nDevelopers usually maintain a local repository on their computers and connect it to a shared remote repository. They can download remote updates with fetch or pull and publish their local commits with push, depending on their access permissions.\n\nFor example, a company may host its application source code in a private GitHub repository. Developers clone the repository, work locally, and push approved changes so the rest of the team can access them."
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git remote command?",
        "answer": "The git remote command manages the remote repository references configured in a local Git repository. A remote reference gives a local repository a convenient name for an external repository URL.\n\nDevelopers commonly use git remote -v to display the configured remote names and their fetch and push URLs. The command git remote add origin followed by a repository URL adds a remote named origin.\n\nFor example, after initializing a local project, a developer can connect it to a newly created GitHub repository by adding the remote URL. The developer can then use origin when pushing or fetching changes."
      },
      {
        "number": 21,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git push command?",
        "answer": "The git push command sends local commits to a remote repository. It allows developers to publish their work so other team members and automation systems can access the new commits.\n\nA typical command is git push origin feature/login, which pushes the local feature/login branch to the remote named origin. The push can be rejected if the remote branch contains changes that the local branch does not include or if the user does not have permission.\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, pushing code to a hosted repository can trigger automated activities such as testing, building, and deployment. The exact pipeline behavior depends on the repository's configured automation rules."
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git pull command?",
        "answer": "The git pull command retrieves changes from a remote repository and integrates them into the current local branch. In the common configuration, it performs a fetch followed by a merge, although pull can also be configured to use rebase.\n\nDevelopers use git pull to update their local branch with changes made by other team members. For example, before starting work on a shared development branch, a developer may pull the latest changes to reduce the chance of working with outdated code.\n\nIf local changes conflict with incoming changes, Git may stop the integration and ask the developer to resolve the conflicts. Developers should review the current branch before pulling in important work."
      },
      {
        "number": 23,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git fetch command?",
        "answer": "The git fetch command downloads new commits and references from a remote repository without automatically merging those changes into the current local branch. It updates remote-tracking references so developers can inspect what has changed.\n\nFor example, a developer can run git fetch origin and then compare the local branch with origin/main. This makes it possible to review incoming commits before deciding whether to merge or rebase them.\n\nFetch is useful when developers want to understand remote changes without immediately modifying their working branch. It is a safer first step when reviewing updates in a shared project."
      },
      {
        "number": 24,
        "question": "What is the difference between git fetch and git pull?",
        "answer": "git fetch downloads updates from a remote repository and updates remote-tracking references, but it does not automatically integrate those changes into the current local branch.\n\ngit pull normally performs a fetch and then integrates the fetched changes into the current branch through merge or a configured rebase operation. Therefore, pull can change the current branch and may produce merge conflicts.\n\nFor example, an engineer investigating changes in origin/main can first run git fetch origin and review the incoming commits. Once the changes are understood, the engineer can choose to merge or rebase them instead of immediately integrating them through git pull."
      },
      {
        "number": 25,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git merge command?",
        "answer": "The git merge command combines changes from one branch into the currently checked-out branch. It is commonly used when a feature or bug-fix branch has completed development and its changes need to be integrated into another branch.\n\nFor example, after a feature branch has been reviewed and tested, a developer can switch to the target branch and merge the feature branch. Git may create a merge commit to record the integration, or it may perform a fast-forward update when the branch history allows it.\n\nIf the same lines were changed differently in both branches, Git may report merge conflicts. Those conflicts must be resolved before the merge can be completed."
      },
      {
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What is a merge conflict in Git?",
        "answer": "A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically combine changes from different branches or commits. This commonly happens when two developers modify the same lines of a file in different ways, or when one change deletes a file that another change modifies.\n\nGit marks the conflicting sections in the affected files and pauses the integration until a developer decides which content should remain. The developer reviews the changes, edits the file to the correct final version, stages the resolved file, and completes the merge.\n\nFor example, two developers may update the same application configuration value differently. The team must understand the intended configuration before selecting or combining the changes. Simply removing conflict markers without checking the logic can introduce defects."
      },
      {
        "number": 27,
        "question": "What is a pull request?",
        "answer": "A pull request is a collaboration feature provided by repository hosting platforms such as GitHub and GitLab. It allows a developer to propose merging changes from one branch into another and ask teammates to review the work.\n\nA pull request commonly displays changed files, commit history, discussion comments, and automated status checks. Reviewers can identify defects, request improvements, and approve the change according to the team's process.\n\nFor example, after completing a feature branch, a developer opens a pull request targeting main. The CI pipeline can run tests and code-quality checks, and the team can review the changes before merging them into the main branch."
      },
      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "What is the difference between git merge and a pull request?",
        "answer": "git merge is a Git operation that combines changes from one branch into another. It can be performed directly from a command line without using a hosting platform.\n\nA pull request is a review and collaboration workflow provided by platforms such as GitHub and GitLab. It gives team members a place to inspect changes, discuss implementation details, run automated checks, and approve the proposed integration.\n\nFor example, a developer can merge a local feature branch into a local main branch using Git. In a company workflow, the developer may instead push the feature branch and open a pull request so the team can review it before the platform allows the merge."
      },
      {
        "number": 29,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git log command?",
        "answer": "The git log command displays the commit history of a repository. It can show commit identifiers, authors, dates, and commit messages, helping developers understand how the project has changed over time.\n\nEngineers use Git history when investigating bugs, reviewing earlier development work, or identifying when a particular change was introduced. Options such as --oneline provide a compact view, while other options can display branch information and detailed commit changes.\n\nFor example, during a production issue, an engineer can inspect recent commits to identify application or configuration changes made shortly before the problem started. This helps narrow the investigation to relevant modifications."
      },
      {
        "number": 30,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git diff command?",
        "answer": "The git diff command displays differences between versions of files. It helps developers understand exactly what has changed before staging, committing, or reviewing code.\n\nFor example, running git diff shows unstaged changes in the working directory compared with the staging area. Running git diff --staged displays changes that have already been staged for the next commit. Developers can also compare branches or commits to understand differences between versions.\n\nReviewing diffs is an important habit because it helps identify accidental edits, debug statements, unrelated modifications, or configuration changes that should not be included in a commit."
      },
      {
        "number": 31,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git restore command?",
        "answer": "The git restore command is used to restore file content in the working directory or staging area. It helps developers discard unwanted file changes or remove a file from the staging area without changing the branch history.\n\nFor example, git restore filename restores an unstaged file to the version recorded in the index. Running git restore --staged filename removes the file's changes from the staging area while keeping the working-directory changes.\n\nThis command can discard work depending on how it is used. Developers should inspect the file and understand which changes they want to keep before running a restore operation."
      },
      {
        "number": 32,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git reset command?",
        "answer": "The git reset command moves the current branch reference to another commit and can also update the staging area or working directory depending on the selected mode. It is commonly used to undo local commits or unstage changes.\n\nThe --soft option moves the branch reference while keeping changes staged. The --mixed option, which is the default, keeps file changes in the working directory but unstages them. The --hard option also resets tracked working files and can permanently discard uncommitted work.\n\nFor example, a developer who created a local commit too early may use a suitable reset mode to move the commit back while keeping the file changes for further editing. Reset operations should be used carefully, especially on commits already shared with teammates."
      },
      {
        "number": 33,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the git revert command?",
        "answer": "The git revert command creates a new commit that reverses the changes introduced by an earlier commit. It does not normally remove the earlier commit from history, so the repository retains a record of both the original change and its reversal.\n\nThis is especially useful when a faulty change has already been pushed to a shared branch. Instead of rewriting shared history, a developer can create a revert commit that safely records the corrective action.\n\nFor example, if a deployment-related change causes a production problem after being merged, the team can revert that commit, review the resulting change, and push the new revert commit through its normal approval process."
      },
      {
        "number": 34,
        "question": "What is the difference between git reset and git revert?",
        "answer": "git reset moves the current branch reference to another commit and may change the staging area or working directory depending on the selected option. It is commonly used to adjust local history before changes are shared.\n\ngit revert creates a new commit that reverses the effect of an earlier commit. It preserves the existing commit history and is generally safer for changes that have already been shared with a team.\n\nFor example, if a developer has made an incorrect local commit that has not been pushed, reset may be suitable. If an incorrect commit has already reached a shared production branch, revert is usually preferred because it records the correction without rewriting shared history."
      },
      {
        "number": 35,
        "question": "What is a .gitignore file?",
        "answer": "A .gitignore file tells Git which untracked files and directories should be ignored. It is commonly used for generated files, local environment settings, build output, temporary files, and dependency directories that should not be committed to the repository.\n\nFor example, a Java project may ignore target build output, while a Node.js project may ignore node_modules and local environment files. This keeps the repository cleaner and reduces the risk of accidentally sharing machine-specific or sensitive files.\n\nA .gitignore rule does not automatically stop tracking a file that Git already tracks. If a sensitive file has already been committed, developers must remove it from tracking and address any exposed credentials separately."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "tool": "Terraform",
    "category": "Basic Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is Terraform?",
        "answer": "Terraform is an Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool developed by HashiCorp. It allows engineers to create, configure, and manage infrastructure using configuration files instead of manually creating resources through cloud-provider consoles.\n\nTerraform supports many infrastructure platforms, including AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, Kubernetes, and other services. Engineers describe the required infrastructure in files, and Terraform compares that configuration with the existing environment before applying changes.\n\nFor example, an organization may need an AWS VPC, subnets, EC2 instances, security groups, and an RDS database. Instead of creating each resource manually, a DevOps engineer can define them in Terraform files and use Terraform to provision the complete environment consistently."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "What is Infrastructure as Code (IaC)?",
        "answer": "Infrastructure as Code is the practice of managing infrastructure through machine-readable configuration files rather than performing every task manually. It allows teams to define servers, networks, databases, storage, and other infrastructure as code.\n\nIaC makes infrastructure changes repeatable, reviewable, and easier to automate. Configuration files can be stored in Git, reviewed through pull requests, and executed through CI/CD pipelines. This reduces manual errors and helps different environments follow the same configuration.\n\nFor example, a DevOps team can maintain Terraform files for development, testing, and production environments. When a new environment is required, the team can use the approved configuration to create the required infrastructure instead of repeating manual console operations."
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "question": "Why is Terraform used in DevOps?",
        "answer": "Terraform is used in DevOps to automate infrastructure provisioning and management. It allows infrastructure changes to be handled through version-controlled configuration files and integrated into automated delivery pipelines.\n\nWithout infrastructure automation, engineers may need to create cloud resources manually. This process can take time and may produce differences between environments. Terraform helps teams use a consistent definition for infrastructure and review changes before applying them.\n\nFor example, when a development team needs a new AWS environment, a Terraform pipeline can validate the configuration, show the planned changes, and provision the required resources after approval. This makes infrastructure delivery more predictable and repeatable."
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "question": "What is a Terraform configuration file?",
        "answer": "A Terraform configuration file contains instructions describing the infrastructure that Terraform should manage. Terraform configuration files commonly use the HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL), which is designed to be readable by both humans and machines.\n\nThese files can define providers, resources, variables, outputs, data sources, and modules. Engineers usually organize related infrastructure definitions into files with the .tf extension.\n\nFor example, an engineer can create a main.tf file containing an AWS provider configuration and an EC2 resource definition. Terraform reads the configuration and uses the provider to create or manage the requested AWS instance."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "question": "What is HCL in Terraform?",
        "answer": "HCL stands for HashiCorp Configuration Language. It is the configuration language commonly used to describe infrastructure in Terraform. HCL uses readable blocks, arguments, expressions, and values to define what infrastructure should exist.\n\nFor example, a resource block identifies the type of infrastructure and gives it a local name. Arguments inside the block describe its configuration, such as an instance type, network settings, or storage size.\n\nHCL is easier to read than writing infrastructure automation entirely in a general-purpose programming language. It allows DevOps engineers to understand the intended infrastructure by reviewing the configuration files."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "question": "What is a Terraform provider?",
        "answer": "A Terraform provider is a plugin that allows Terraform to communicate with an external platform or service. Providers contain the functionality required to create, read, update, and delete resources through that platform's API.\n\nFor example, the AWS provider allows Terraform to manage AWS resources such as EC2 instances, VPCs, S3 buckets, and security groups. The Kubernetes provider allows Terraform to manage supported Kubernetes resources.\n\nA provider is configured in Terraform files, and Terraform installs the required provider plugin during initialization. Provider versions should be controlled so that infrastructure automation behaves consistently across developer machines and CI/CD systems."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the terraform init command?",
        "answer": "The terraform init command initializes a Terraform working directory. It prepares the directory for Terraform operations by downloading the required provider plugins and configuring the backend used to store Terraform state.\n\nThis command is normally executed when starting a new Terraform project or when its provider or backend configuration changes. It also prepares module dependencies when the configuration uses modules.\n\nFor example, if a project uses the AWS provider, running terraform init downloads the required provider version and prepares the working directory. After initialization, engineers can validate the configuration and generate an execution plan."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the terraform plan command?",
        "answer": "The terraform plan command compares the desired infrastructure configuration with the current state information and refreshed resource information where applicable. It displays the changes Terraform intends to make if the configuration is applied.\n\nThe plan helps engineers understand whether Terraform will create new resources, update existing resources, or destroy resources. It is an important review step before making infrastructure changes.\n\nFor example, if an engineer changes an EC2 instance type from one supported size to another, terraform plan shows the proposed change and indicates whether the provider requires an in-place update or resource replacement. Teams commonly review the plan before approving production changes."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the terraform apply command?",
        "answer": "The terraform apply command executes the changes required to make the actual infrastructure match the Terraform configuration. It normally creates an execution plan and asks for confirmation before applying the proposed changes, unless automatic approval or a saved plan is used.\n\nDuring execution, Terraform communicates with the configured provider APIs to create, update, or delete infrastructure resources. After successful operations, Terraform updates its state information to record the managed resources.\n\nFor example, after reviewing a plan that creates an AWS VPC and EC2 instance, an engineer can run terraform apply to provision those resources. In production, teams commonly apply a previously reviewed saved plan through an approved pipeline."
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the terraform destroy command?",
        "answer": "The terraform destroy command removes infrastructure resources managed by the current Terraform configuration and state. It is mainly used when an environment or temporary infrastructure is no longer required.\n\nTerraform identifies the managed resources and calculates the operations required to destroy them. Resources may have dependencies, so Terraform generally removes dependent resources in a suitable order.\n\nFor example, a team may create a temporary testing environment containing EC2 instances, security groups, and networking resources. After testing is completed, terraform destroy can remove those managed resources. Engineers must review the destruction plan carefully because deleting production infrastructure can cause service outages and data loss."
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "question": "What is Terraform state?",
        "answer": "Terraform state is information maintained by Terraform to remember the infrastructure resources it manages and their relationship with the configuration. It maps Terraform resource addresses to real infrastructure objects and stores information needed for future planning and updates.\n\nWhen Terraform creates an EC2 instance, it records the resource information in the state. During a later plan, Terraform uses this information to understand which real resource corresponds to the resource declared in the configuration.\n\nState is important because Terraform uses it to determine what needs to change. It can contain sensitive information, so access must be controlled and state storage must be protected."
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "question": "What is the terraform.tfstate file?",
        "answer": "The terraform.tfstate file is the default local state file created by Terraform when using local state storage. It contains the recorded information about resources managed by the Terraform configuration.\n\nFor example, after creating an AWS EC2 instance, Terraform records information about that instance in the state file. During later operations, Terraform uses the state to compare the recorded infrastructure with the desired configuration.\n\nThe state file should not be edited manually or casually deleted. In team environments, local state is usually replaced with a protected remote backend so that multiple engineers and automation pipelines can work with shared infrastructure safely."
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "question": "What is a Terraform backend?",
        "answer": "A Terraform backend defines where Terraform stores its state and how state operations are handled. Terraform supports local state storage as well as remote backends that store state in an external system.\n\nFor example, an organization can configure an S3-based backend to store Terraform state centrally. This allows team members and CI/CD pipelines to work with the same infrastructure state instead of maintaining separate local copies.\n\nA backend is important for collaboration and operational safety. Teams should configure appropriate access permissions, encryption, backup or recovery arrangements, and state locking where supported by the selected backend."
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "question": "What is remote state in Terraform?",
        "answer": "Remote state means storing Terraform state outside the local working directory, usually in a shared storage service. It allows multiple authorized engineers and automation systems to access the same state information.\n\nFor example, a DevOps team managing AWS infrastructure can store its state in an S3 bucket configured as a Terraform backend. Engineers working from different computers can then use the same state rather than maintaining separate local state files.\n\nRemote state improves team collaboration, but it must be protected with suitable access permissions and encryption. Teams should also understand the locking capabilities of their chosen backend to reduce the risk of conflicting state operations."
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "question": "What is state locking in Terraform?",
        "answer": "State locking prevents multiple Terraform operations from modifying the same state at the same time when the configured backend supports locking. It helps avoid conflicting changes that could result in inconsistent state information.\n\nFor example, suppose two engineers start applying infrastructure changes against the same environment simultaneously. Without effective locking, both operations could attempt to update the same resources or state data. Locking helps ensure that one operation has control while another waits or fails safely.\n\nThe locking mechanism depends on the selected backend and its supported features. Teams should use a backend configuration that provides appropriate concurrency protection for their Terraform version and environment."
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "question": "What is a Terraform resource?",
        "answer": "A Terraform resource represents an infrastructure object that Terraform can manage through a provider. Resources are the main building blocks used to create and maintain cloud infrastructure.\n\nA resource block normally contains a resource type, a local resource name, and configuration arguments. For example, an AWS EC2 instance resource can define the selected machine image, instance type, and other supported settings.\n\nWhen Terraform applies the configuration, the provider communicates with AWS to create or update the actual instance. Terraform then records the resource in its state so that future operations can manage it."
      },
      {
        "number": 17,
        "question": "What is a Terraform data source?",
        "answer": "A Terraform data source allows Terraform to read information about an existing object or external system without managing that object as a resource. It is useful when infrastructure configuration depends on information that already exists.\n\nFor example, a Terraform project may need to find an existing AWS VPC or retrieve information about an approved AMI. An AWS data source can query the provider for that information and expose the result to other configuration blocks.\n\nA data source reads information rather than creating the object it describes. This helps teams connect newly provisioned infrastructure with existing shared resources."
      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "question": "What is the difference between a Terraform resource and a data source?",
        "answer": "A Terraform resource is used to create and manage an infrastructure object. Terraform records the resource in its state and can plan changes or deletion based on the configuration.\n\nA data source is used to read information about an existing object or external system. It does not normally create or take ownership of the object it reads.\n\nFor example, an AWS EC2 resource can create a new virtual machine, while an AWS AMI data source can look up an approved machine image that already exists. The resource can then use the image information returned by the data source when creating the instance."
      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "What are input variables in Terraform?",
        "answer": "Input variables allow engineers to pass values into Terraform configuration instead of hardcoding every value. They make configuration reusable across different environments and deployments.\n\nA variable can represent values such as an AWS Region, EC2 instance type, environment name, or VPC CIDR range. The configuration declares the variable and can use its value through the var reference.\n\nFor example, the same Terraform configuration can create a smaller EC2 instance in a development environment and a larger instance in production by supplying different variable values. This reduces duplicated configuration and makes environment-specific settings easier to manage."
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "question": "What is a Terraform output value?",
        "answer": "An output value allows Terraform to display selected information after infrastructure operations. Outputs can expose useful details such as an EC2 instance ID, public IP address, load balancer DNS name, or database endpoint.\n\nFor example, after creating an AWS application environment, a Terraform output can display the load balancer DNS name. An engineer can use that value to access the application or pass it to another automation step.\n\nOutputs improve visibility and can help connect different Terraform configurations. Sensitive information should be marked and handled carefully because output values may be visible in command results or stored in state."
      },
      {
        "number": 21,
        "question": "What is a Terraform module?",
        "answer": "A Terraform module is a collection of Terraform configuration files organized together to manage related infrastructure. A module can contain resources, variables, outputs, and other module references.\n\nModules help teams reuse common infrastructure patterns instead of copying the same configuration into many projects. For example, an organization can create a reusable VPC module that defines subnets, route tables, and gateways according to its approved network design.\n\nA root module is the main working directory from which Terraform runs. It can call child modules to organize larger infrastructure projects into smaller and easier-to-maintain components."
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "question": "What is the difference between a root module and a child module?",
        "answer": "The root module is the main Terraform configuration directory where an engineer runs Terraform commands. It contains the top-level configuration and can call other modules to create parts of the infrastructure.\n\nA child module is a module called by another module. It usually contains reusable configuration for a specific infrastructure component, such as networking, compute, or database resources.\n\nFor example, a root module for an application environment can call a VPC child module and an EC2 child module. This structure keeps the main configuration organized and allows the same child modules to be reused by different environments."
      },
      {
        "number": 23,
        "question": "What is a Terraform variable file?",
        "answer": "A Terraform variable file provides values for input variables declared in Terraform configuration. Files commonly use the .tfvars or .auto.tfvars extension.\n\nFor example, a development variable file may specify a development environment name and a smaller EC2 instance type, while a production variable file provides production-specific values. The same Terraform configuration can use these values without duplicating the resource definitions.\n\nVariable files help separate reusable infrastructure logic from environment-specific settings. Teams should avoid storing passwords, API keys, or other secrets in ordinary variable files committed to source control."
      },
      {
        "number": 24,
        "question": "What is the purpose of terraform validate?",
        "answer": "The terraform validate command checks whether the Terraform configuration is syntactically valid and internally consistent. It can identify problems such as invalid arguments, incorrect block structures, and references that cannot be resolved within the configuration.\n\nIt is commonly used before generating a plan or running infrastructure changes. In CI/CD pipelines, validation helps detect configuration mistakes early, before the pipeline attempts to modify cloud resources.\n\nFor example, if an engineer uses an unsupported argument inside an AWS resource block, terraform validate may report the configuration problem. Validation does not prove that the infrastructure can be created successfully; provider permissions, remote conditions, and actual resource constraints may still cause failures later."
      },
      {
        "number": 25,
        "question": "What is the purpose of terraform fmt?",
        "answer": "The terraform fmt command automatically formats Terraform configuration files using the standard Terraform formatting rules. It improves consistency and readability across files maintained by different engineers.\n\nFor example, developers may write resource blocks with different spacing or alignment. Running terraform fmt adjusts the formatting so the configuration follows a consistent style.\n\nTeams commonly run terraform fmt -check in CI pipelines to verify that committed Terraform files are correctly formatted. Formatting does not change the intended infrastructure design; it standardizes how the configuration is written."
      },
      {
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What is the purpose of terraform output?",
        "answer": "The terraform output command displays the output values defined by the current Terraform configuration and recorded in state. These values can provide useful information about infrastructure created by Terraform.\n\nFor example, after deploying an AWS environment, an output may contain the EC2 instance ID or the DNS name of an application load balancer. An engineer can run terraform output to retrieve those values without manually searching through the cloud console.\n\nOutput values should be designed carefully because they may expose infrastructure details. Sensitive outputs require appropriate handling, especially when commands run inside shared CI/CD environments."
      },
      {
        "number": 27,
        "question": "What is the purpose of terraform show?",
        "answer": "The terraform show command displays a human-readable representation of Terraform state or a saved execution plan. It helps engineers inspect what Terraform currently knows about managed infrastructure or what a saved plan intends to change.\n\nFor example, after generating a saved plan for an infrastructure update, an engineer can use terraform show to inspect the planned resource changes. This can help reviewers understand the proposed update before it is applied.\n\nTerraform show is useful for investigation and review, but the displayed information may include sensitive values. Access to its output should be controlled in the same way as access to Terraform state and plans."
      },
      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "What is a Terraform workspace?",
        "answer": "A Terraform workspace is a named working state associated with a Terraform configuration. Workspaces allow the same configuration directory to maintain separate state instances for different deployments.\n\nFor example, a team may use separate workspaces named development and testing to manage separate instances of the same infrastructure configuration. Terraform selects the active workspace when determining which state to use.\n\nWorkspaces are useful for certain environment-separation needs, but they do not automatically create complete security or access boundaries. Production environments may require separate configurations, credentials, accounts, or backend arrangements depending on organizational requirements."
      },
      {
        "number": 29,
        "question": "What is the difference between Terraform workspaces and separate environment directories?",
        "answer": "Terraform workspaces use the same configuration directory with different named state instances. They can be useful when environments share the same infrastructure structure but need separate state.\n\nSeparate environment directories maintain different configuration directories for environments such as development, testing, and production. This approach allows each environment to have its own configuration files and potentially different infrastructure definitions.\n\nFor example, a small project may use workspaces for similar test environments. A large organization may prefer separate directories or repositories when production requires different network rules, resource sizes, approval controls, or account boundaries."
      },
      {
        "number": 30,
        "question": "What is Terraform dependency management?",
        "answer": "Terraform dependency management determines the order in which infrastructure resources should be created, updated, or destroyed. Terraform builds a dependency graph from references between resources and uses it to determine suitable execution ordering.\n\nFor example, an EC2 instance may depend on a subnet and security group. When the configuration references those resources, Terraform understands that the network resources must be available before the instance can be created.\n\nTerraform can also use an explicit depends_on argument when a dependency is not clear from normal references. Explicit dependencies should be added only when necessary because unnecessary dependencies can reduce parallel execution."
      },
      {
        "number": 31,
        "question": "What is the Terraform dependency graph?",
        "answer": "The Terraform dependency graph is an internal representation of relationships between resources and operations. Terraform uses this graph to determine which actions must happen before others and which independent operations can run in parallel.\n\nFor example, a VPC must exist before Terraform can create subnets inside it. A resource that references the VPC creates a dependency relationship, allowing Terraform to determine the correct order automatically.\n\nThe dependency graph helps Terraform manage complex infrastructure safely and efficiently. Engineers can inspect the graph using Terraform's graph command when investigating unexpected ordering or dependencies."
      },
      {
        "number": 32,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the terraform import command?",
        "answer": "The terraform import command connects an existing real-world infrastructure object to a Terraform resource address in state. It is useful when an organization already has infrastructure created manually or through another system and wants Terraform to manage it.\n\nFor example, an AWS EC2 instance may already be running in production without being managed by Terraform. An engineer can define the corresponding resource configuration and import the existing instance into Terraform state using the required resource identifier.\n\nImporting an object does not automatically generate a complete configuration that matches every existing setting. Engineers must review and update the Terraform configuration so that it accurately represents the imported infrastructure before applying changes."
      },
      {
        "number": 33,
        "question": "What is infrastructure drift in Terraform?",
        "answer": "Infrastructure drift occurs when the actual infrastructure becomes different from the configuration Terraform expects to manage. Drift can happen when an engineer changes a resource manually through a cloud console or when another automation system modifies it.\n\nFor example, Terraform may define an EC2 instance with one security group configuration, but an administrator may manually add another inbound rule in AWS. The real environment now differs from the intended configuration.\n\nTerraform plan can help identify differences between the configuration, state, and refreshed remote resource information. Teams should investigate drift before applying changes because Terraform may propose updating or replacing resources to restore the intended configuration."
      },
      {
        "number": 34,
        "question": "What is a saved Terraform plan?",
        "answer": "A saved Terraform plan is an execution plan written to a file after Terraform calculates the proposed infrastructure changes. It allows teams to separate the review stage from the later apply stage.\n\nFor example, a CI pipeline can run terraform plan and save the result as a plan file. An authorized reviewer can inspect the proposed changes, and a later pipeline stage can apply that saved plan after approval.\n\nSaved plans help improve consistency between review and execution, but they must be protected because they can contain sensitive information. Teams should also ensure that the plan is applied against the intended configuration, state, credentials, and environment."
      },
      {
        "number": 35,
        "question": "How is Terraform used in a CI/CD pipeline?",
        "answer": "Terraform is commonly integrated into CI/CD pipelines to automate infrastructure validation, planning, approval, and deployment. This allows infrastructure changes to follow a controlled process similar to application code changes.\n\nFor example, when a developer creates a pull request containing Terraform changes, the pipeline can run terraform fmt -check, terraform init, terraform validate, and terraform plan. Reviewers inspect the proposed infrastructure changes before approving the pull request.\n\nAfter approval, a controlled deployment stage can apply the reviewed plan using an authorized identity. Production pipelines should protect credentials, restrict permissions, secure state, and require approval for destructive or high-impact changes."
      }
    ]
