    "questions": [
      {
        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is Ansible?",
        "answer": "Ansible is an open-source IT automation and configuration management tool. It helps DevOps engineers automate tasks such as software installation, server configuration, application deployment, user management, and infrastructure operations across multiple machines.\n\nAnsible uses a simple automation language called YAML to define instructions in files known as playbooks. It commonly connects to Linux servers through SSH and executes the required tasks without installing a permanent Ansible agent on every managed server.\n\nFor example, an organization may have 50 Linux servers that need the same Nginx installation and configuration. Instead of connecting to each server manually, an administrator can create one Ansible playbook and execute it against all required servers."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "Why is Ansible used in DevOps?",
        "answer": "Ansible is used in DevOps to automate repetitive infrastructure and application management tasks. It helps teams perform server configuration, software installation, deployment, patching, and operational activities in a consistent way.\n\nWhen the same task is performed manually on many servers, there is a risk of human error and configuration differences. Ansible allows engineers to define the required steps once and execute them across multiple managed systems.\n\nFor example, when a company launches a new application environment, Ansible can install Java, configure application directories, update environment settings, and restart required services on multiple servers. This saves time and makes server configuration more consistent."
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "question": "What is configuration management?",
        "answer": "Configuration management is the process of maintaining servers and infrastructure in a defined and consistent configuration. It includes managing software packages, system settings, users, permissions, services, and application configuration.\n\nWithout configuration management, different servers may gradually develop different settings because administrators make manual changes over time. This can cause applications to behave differently between development, testing, and production environments.\n\nAnsible helps automate configuration management through playbooks. For example, an organization can define the required Nginx version, configuration file, service state, and firewall settings. The same configuration can then be applied to all appropriate servers."
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "question": "What is agentless architecture in Ansible?",
        "answer": "Ansible follows an agentless architecture for many of its common managed-node operations. This means that administrators generally do not need to install and maintain a permanent Ansible agent on every managed Linux server.\n\nThe Ansible control node connects to managed Linux machines using SSH and executes the required modules or commands. For Windows systems, Ansible commonly uses WinRM or other supported connection methods.\n\nFor example, if an administrator needs to configure 20 Linux servers, Ansible can connect to those servers through SSH using an authorized account. This reduces the additional software and maintenance required on managed systems."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "question": "What is an Ansible control node?",
        "answer": "The Ansible control node is the machine from which Ansible commands and automation are executed. It contains the Ansible installation, project files, inventory, playbooks, and the credentials or connection configuration needed to reach managed systems.\n\nA control node can be a Linux server, a developer workstation, or an automation environment such as a CI/CD runner. It communicates with managed machines and coordinates the execution of automation tasks.\n\nFor example, a DevOps team may maintain a dedicated automation server that runs Ansible playbooks against development, testing, and production servers. The control node must have the required network access and permissions to perform those operations."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "question": "What is a managed node in Ansible?",
        "answer": "A managed node is a system that Ansible controls through automation. It may be a Linux server, Windows server, network device, or another supported system.\n\nManaged nodes are defined through an inventory, which provides information such as hostnames, IP addresses, groups, and connection variables. Ansible uses this information to determine which systems should receive the automation tasks.\n\nFor example, an organization may have separate application and database servers. Ansible inventory can group them as app_servers and db_servers so that administrators can apply the correct configuration to each group."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "What is an Ansible inventory?",
        "answer": "An Ansible inventory is a file or inventory source that identifies the managed systems Ansible should connect to. It can contain server IP addresses, hostnames, groups, and connection-related variables.\n\nAn inventory can be written in INI or YAML format. It can also come from dynamic inventory plugins that retrieve infrastructure information from external platforms such as cloud providers.\n\nFor example, a company may maintain 10 application servers and 5 database servers. The inventory can group these machines separately, allowing an administrator to run an application deployment only against application servers instead of targeting every machine."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "What is the difference between static inventory and dynamic inventory?",
        "answer": "Static inventory is an inventory in which administrators manually define the managed hosts and groups. It is suitable for environments where server addresses and infrastructure membership do not change frequently.\n\nDynamic inventory retrieves host information from an external source, such as AWS, Azure, or another infrastructure platform. It is useful when servers are frequently created, deleted, or replaced through cloud automation.\n\nFor example, if an organization maintains a small fixed group of Linux servers, a static inventory may be sufficient. If an Auto Scaling Group continuously adds and removes EC2 instances, dynamic inventory can help Ansible discover the currently available instances automatically."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "What is an Ansible playbook?",
        "answer": "An Ansible playbook is a YAML file that describes automation tasks to be executed against selected managed systems. It defines the target hosts, variables, tasks, and other execution instructions.\n\nPlaybooks are commonly used for server configuration, application deployment, package installation, service management, and infrastructure operations. They allow administrators to maintain automation instructions in readable files and store them in Git.\n\nFor example, an Ansible playbook can install Nginx, copy an application configuration file, and ensure that the Nginx service is running. The same playbook can be executed against multiple web servers."
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "question": "What is YAML in Ansible?",
        "answer": "YAML stands for YAML Ain't Markup Language. It is a human-readable data format used by Ansible to define playbooks, variables, and other automation configuration.\n\nYAML uses indentation to represent relationships between data elements. This means correct spacing is important. Incorrect indentation can cause parsing errors or change how Ansible interprets a configuration.\n\nFor example, an Ansible playbook uses YAML to define a list of tasks under a play. Each task can specify a module and its parameters. Because YAML is readable, engineers can understand the automation steps without writing a large amount of programming code."
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "question": "What is an Ansible module?",
        "answer": "An Ansible module is a unit of automation that performs a specific operation on a managed system or supported service. Modules provide reusable functionality for tasks such as installing packages, managing files, creating users, and controlling services.\n\nFor example, the package module can install software, the copy module can transfer files, and the service module can manage system services. Ansible executes the selected module as part of a playbook task.\n\nModules help engineers avoid writing separate shell commands for every operation. They also provide structured behavior and often support idempotency, making automation easier to maintain."
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "question": "What is the difference between an Ansible module and a playbook?",
        "answer": "An Ansible module performs an individual automation operation, while a playbook organizes multiple tasks into a complete automation workflow.\n\nFor example, the service module can start or stop an application service. A playbook can combine package installation, configuration file deployment, permission management, and service startup into one ordered process.\n\nModules provide the individual actions, and playbooks describe when and where those actions should be executed. This separation allows automation tasks to be reused in different playbooks."
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "question": "What is an Ansible task?",
        "answer": "An Ansible task is an individual instruction inside a playbook. It normally calls an Ansible module with the required parameters to perform one specific operation.\n\nTasks are executed in the order in which they are defined, unless special execution behavior changes that order. A task can install a package, create a directory, copy a file, or restart a service.\n\nFor example, an application deployment playbook may contain one task to install Java, another task to copy the application configuration, and a third task to start the application service. Each task represents one manageable step in the deployment process."
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "question": "What is an Ansible play?",
        "answer": "An Ansible play is a section of a playbook that maps a group of target hosts to a set of tasks and execution settings. A play defines where automation should run and what operations should be performed.\n\nA play commonly includes hosts, variables, tasks, handlers, and privilege-escalation settings. A playbook can contain multiple plays for different groups of systems.\n\nFor example, one play can configure web servers while another play configures database servers. Both plays can exist in the same playbook but target different inventory groups."
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the ansible command?",
        "answer": "The ansible command is used to execute ad-hoc automation tasks directly from the command line. It is useful when administrators need to perform a quick operation without creating a complete playbook.\n\nFor example, an administrator can use an ad-hoc command to check the connectivity of a group of Linux servers or retrieve their uptime. Ansible selects the target hosts from the inventory and executes the requested module.\n\nAd-hoc commands are useful for quick administration and investigation. For repeatable workflows involving multiple steps, playbooks are generally easier to maintain and review."
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the ansible-playbook command?",
        "answer": "The ansible-playbook command executes an Ansible playbook. It reads the YAML instructions, identifies the target hosts, loads the required variables, and runs the tasks using the configured connection method.\n\nFor example, an administrator can execute ansible-playbook deploy.yml to run an application deployment workflow defined in deploy.yml. The playbook may install dependencies, copy application files, update configuration, and restart services.\n\nThe command supports useful options for selecting inventory, limiting target hosts, checking syntax, and controlling execution. In production, engineers should review the target environment and playbook changes before running deployment operations."
      },
      {
        "number": 17,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the ansible-inventory command?",
        "answer": "The ansible-inventory command displays and inspects inventory information understood by Ansible. It helps administrators verify which hosts and groups Ansible has discovered from the configured inventory sources.\n\nFor example, ansible-inventory --list displays inventory information in a structured format, while ansible-inventory --graph provides a visual tree-like representation of groups and hosts.\n\nThis command is useful when troubleshooting incorrect host grouping, missing servers, or inventory configuration problems. Before executing a production playbook, engineers can inspect the inventory to confirm that the intended machines are included."
      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the ansible.cfg file?",
        "answer": "The ansible.cfg file is the main configuration file used to control Ansible behavior for a project or execution environment. It can define settings such as the default inventory path, remote user, connection behavior, privilege escalation, and other execution options.\n\nAnsible can load configuration from different locations, including environment-specific configuration paths. The configuration file selected by Ansible affects how commands and playbooks behave.\n\nFor example, a project can define its inventory file and default SSH settings in ansible.cfg. This allows team members to run automation with consistent project settings instead of repeatedly specifying every option on the command line."
      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "What is SSH in Ansible?",
        "answer": "SSH stands for Secure Shell. It is a network protocol that provides secure remote access to systems through an encrypted connection. Ansible commonly uses SSH to connect from its control node to managed Linux machines.\n\nThe control node authenticates to the remote system using an approved SSH key or another supported authentication method. After connecting, Ansible executes the required automation modules or commands.\n\nFor example, when configuring an Ubuntu server, Ansible may connect using an authorized deployment account and execute tasks that install packages or update configuration files. SSH access, network connectivity, and remote permissions must all be correct for automation to succeed."
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "question": "What is privilege escalation in Ansible?",
        "answer": "Privilege escalation allows Ansible to execute tasks with higher operating-system permissions than the account used for the initial connection. On Linux systems, this commonly means using sudo to perform administrative operations.\n\nFor example, a deployment account may be allowed to connect through SSH but may not have direct permission to install system packages. Ansible can use become to run the required package-management task with elevated privileges.\n\nPrivilege escalation should be configured carefully. Production automation should use approved accounts, limited sudo permissions, and appropriate security controls instead of granting unnecessary administrative access."
      },
      {
        "number": 21,
        "question": "What is the purpose of become in Ansible?",
        "answer": "The become feature in Ansible enables privilege escalation when a task requires additional permissions. It is commonly used to execute operations as root or another authorized operating-system user.\n\nFor example, a normal deployment account may be able to upload application files but may require elevated privileges to install a system package or restart a protected service. Setting become for the appropriate play or task allows Ansible to perform those operations using the configured privilege-escalation method.\n\nUsing become does not automatically guarantee permission. The remote account must be authorized to escalate privileges, and any required password or sudo configuration must be available."
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "question": "What are Ansible facts?",
        "answer": "Ansible facts are system information collected from managed nodes. They can include operating-system details, IP addresses, memory information, processor details, network interfaces, and other host properties.\n\nFacts are useful when automation needs to make decisions based on the actual system environment. For example, a playbook can install different packages depending on whether a managed machine is running Ubuntu or Red Hat Enterprise Linux.\n\nAnsible commonly gathers facts at the beginning of a play unless fact gathering is disabled. These facts can then be referenced as variables inside tasks and templates."
      },
      {
        "number": 23,
        "question": "What is the setup module in Ansible?",
        "answer": "The setup module is used to collect facts from managed systems. It gathers information about the operating system, hardware, networking, and other system properties that Ansible exposes as facts.\n\nFor example, an administrator can use the setup module to inspect the distribution name and version of a Linux server. A playbook can use those facts to select the correct package installation steps.\n\nThe setup module is especially useful when automation must support different operating systems or server configurations. It helps playbooks make decisions based on the actual environment instead of relying only on manually entered information."
      },
      {
        "number": 24,
        "question": "What are Ansible handlers?",
        "answer": "Ansible handlers are special tasks that normally execute only when notified by another task. They are commonly used for operations that should happen only when a related configuration change occurs.\n\nFor example, a playbook may copy a new Nginx configuration file to a server. If the file changes, the task can notify a handler that restarts or reloads Nginx. If the configuration file remains unchanged, the handler normally does not run.\n\nHandlers help avoid unnecessary service restarts and make automation more efficient. They are generally executed at handler execution points rather than immediately after every notifying task."
      },
      {
        "number": 25,
        "question": "What is idempotency in Ansible?",
        "answer": "Idempotency means that repeating an automation operation produces the same intended final state without causing unnecessary changes each time. It is an important concept in configuration management.\n\nFor example, if a playbook ensures that Nginx is installed and running, executing the playbook again should normally report that no change is required when the server is already in the correct state.\n\nMany Ansible modules are designed to support idempotent behavior. However, shell and command tasks may perform actions every time they run unless additional conditions are used. Engineers should prefer suitable Ansible modules when available."
      },
      {
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What are Ansible variables?",
        "answer": "Ansible variables are values used to make playbooks flexible and reusable. They can represent information such as application versions, directory paths, usernames, package names, environment settings, and service configuration.\n\nFor example, instead of hardcoding an application version in several tasks, an engineer can define it as a variable and reference it wherever required. Different environments can provide different variable values while using the same playbook.\n\nVariables can be defined in playbooks, inventory files, dedicated variable files, roles, and other supported locations. Ansible has variable precedence rules that determine which value is used when the same variable is defined in multiple places."
      },
      {
        "number": 27,
        "question": "What is an Ansible template?",
        "answer": "An Ansible template is a file containing text with dynamic values that Ansible can replace during automation. Ansible commonly uses the Jinja2 templating language to generate configuration files based on variables and conditions.\n\nFor example, an organization may use one Nginx configuration template for several servers. The server name, application port, and environment-specific values can be supplied through variables when Ansible generates the final configuration file.\n\nTemplates help teams maintain reusable configuration files without creating a separate hardcoded file for every environment. The generated configuration can then be deployed to the managed system."
      },
      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "What is an Ansible role?",
        "answer": "An Ansible role is a structured way to organize reusable automation content. A role can contain tasks, handlers, variables, defaults, templates, files, and metadata in a standard directory structure.\n\nFor example, a team can create an Nginx role containing package installation tasks, configuration templates, service handlers, and default variables. The same role can be reused by multiple playbooks and projects.\n\nRoles improve maintainability by separating large automation workflows into smaller components. They also make it easier for teams to share and maintain standard server configuration procedures."
      },
      {
        "number": 29,
        "question": "What is the difference between an Ansible playbook and a role?",
        "answer": "A playbook defines the automation workflow, including the target hosts and the plays or tasks that should be executed. It acts as the main entry point for running an automation process.\n\nA role is a reusable structure that organizes related automation content, such as tasks, handlers, templates, and variables. A playbook can include one or more roles to perform specific configuration activities.\n\nFor example, a server deployment playbook may target the web server inventory group and include an Nginx role and an application role. The playbook controls the overall workflow while the roles provide reusable implementation details."
      },
      {
        "number": 30,
        "question": "What is Ansible Galaxy?",
        "answer": "Ansible Galaxy is a platform and command-line ecosystem used to discover, share, and install reusable Ansible roles and collections. It helps automation teams reuse existing community or organization-maintained automation content.\n\nFor example, instead of creating every task for a common configuration from the beginning, an engineer can search for a suitable role or collection and evaluate whether it meets the organization's requirements.\n\nDownloaded content should be reviewed for maintenance quality, security, compatibility, and trustworthiness before it is used in production. Teams may also publish and share their own reusable automation content."
      },
      {
        "number": 31,
        "question": "What is the difference between Ansible ad-hoc commands and playbooks?",
        "answer": "Ansible ad-hoc commands are used to execute individual tasks directly from the command line. They are useful for quick checks and simple administrative operations.\n\nPlaybooks are YAML files that define multiple tasks and their execution workflow. They are more suitable for repeatable automation such as application deployment, server configuration, and multi-step operational procedures.\n\nFor example, an administrator may use an ad-hoc command to check whether several servers are reachable. For a complete application deployment involving package installation, file copying, configuration changes, and service startup, a playbook is easier to maintain and reuse."
      },
      {
        "number": 32,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the --check option in Ansible?",
        "answer": "The --check option enables Ansible check mode, which attempts to show what changes would be made without applying most of those changes to the managed systems.\n\nIt is useful when administrators want to review a playbook before running it against important infrastructure. For example, an engineer can run a server configuration playbook in check mode to inspect which packages, files, or services would be changed.\n\nCheck mode is not a perfect simulation for every task. Some modules do not fully support it, and tasks depending on earlier changes may not behave exactly as they would during a normal execution. It should be treated as a useful review aid rather than a guarantee."
      },
      {
        "number": 33,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the --syntax-check option in Ansible?",
        "answer": "The --syntax-check option checks whether an Ansible playbook can be parsed successfully. It helps identify YAML structure problems and other playbook syntax issues before the automation is executed.\n\nFor example, if a playbook has incorrect indentation or an invalid task structure, a syntax check can report the problem before Ansible attempts to connect to managed servers.\n\nSyntax checking does not verify that every remote operation will succeed. Network connectivity, permissions, package availability, and application behavior must still be tested during appropriate execution stages."
      },
      {
        "number": 34,
        "question": "What is the purpose of the --limit option in Ansible?",
        "answer": "The --limit option restricts a playbook or ad-hoc command to a selected host or group from the inventory. It is useful when an administrator wants to execute automation against only a specific part of the infrastructure.\n\nFor example, an inventory may contain 100 production servers, but an engineer may want to test a configuration change on one server first. The --limit option can restrict execution to that selected host.\n\nLimiting the target hosts reduces the scope of an operation, but engineers must still verify the inventory and target selection before running sensitive production automation."
      },
      {
        "number": 35,
        "question": "How is Ansible used in a CI/CD pipeline?",
        "answer": "Ansible is commonly integrated into CI/CD pipelines to automate application deployment and server configuration. It allows delivery pipelines to execute repeatable operational tasks after an application has been built and tested.\n\nFor example, a Jenkins pipeline can retrieve an Ansible playbook from Git, validate its syntax, and execute it against approved application servers. The playbook can copy the application package, update configuration, restart the service, and perform basic validation.\n\nIn production environments, the pipeline should use secure credentials, approved inventories, controlled permissions, and deployment approvals. Teams should also verify that the correct environment and target servers are selected before running Ansible automation."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "tool": "Linux",
    "category": "Basic Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is Linux?",
        "answer": "Linux is an open-source operating system kernel used to manage computer hardware and software resources. Operating systems built around the Linux kernel are commonly called Linux distributions, such as Ubuntu, Red Hat Enterprise Linux, Debian, and Amazon Linux.\n\nLinux is widely used in DevOps because it provides a stable environment for running applications, databases, containers, automation tools, and cloud infrastructure. Most production servers and many cloud workloads run on Linux-based systems.\n\nFor example, a company may host its Java application on an Ubuntu or Red Hat server. DevOps engineers use Linux commands to inspect system resources, manage files, configure services, troubleshoot application failures, and maintain server availability."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "Why is Linux important in DevOps?",
        "answer": "Linux is important in DevOps because many production environments, cloud servers, containers, and automation platforms run on Linux. DevOps engineers frequently interact with Linux systems while building, deploying, and supporting applications.\n\nLinux provides command-line tools for managing processes, networking, storage, permissions, logs, and services. These capabilities make it suitable for automation and large-scale infrastructure operations.\n\nFor example, when a Jenkins pipeline deploys an application to a Linux server, engineers may use Linux commands to verify the deployment directory, check application logs, inspect running processes, and restart services. Understanding Linux is therefore an essential skill for daily DevOps troubleshooting."
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "question": "What is the Linux kernel?",
        "answer": "The Linux kernel is the core component of the Linux operating system. It manages communication between software and hardware and controls important resources such as CPU time, memory, devices, filesystems, and networking.\n\nWhen an application needs to access hardware or request an operating-system service, it interacts with the kernel through system calls. The kernel manages these requests and provides controlled access to system resources.\n\nFor example, when a Java application requests memory or reads a file, the Linux kernel handles the underlying resource operations. Understanding the kernel is useful when troubleshooting system-level problems such as high CPU usage, memory pressure, device errors, or kernel crashes."
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "question": "What is a Linux distribution?",
        "answer": "A Linux distribution is a complete operating-system package built around the Linux kernel. It combines the kernel with system utilities, package management tools, libraries, configuration files, and software repositories.\n\nDifferent distributions are designed for different requirements. Ubuntu is widely used for cloud servers and development environments, while Red Hat Enterprise Linux is commonly used in enterprise environments. Debian, Amazon Linux, and SUSE are also used in production.\n\nFor example, a DevOps engineer may manage Ubuntu servers in one environment and Red Hat servers in another. Package installation commands and some configuration paths can differ between distributions, so engineers should identify the operating system before executing administration commands."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "question": "What is the Linux command line?",
        "answer": "The Linux command line is a text-based interface used to interact with the operating system. Users enter commands into a shell to manage files, processes, users, services, networking, and other system resources.\n\nThe command line is especially useful for remote server administration because engineers can connect to Linux machines through SSH without requiring a graphical desktop.\n\nFor example, a DevOps engineer can connect to a production server and use commands such as pwd, ls, ps, df, and systemctl to investigate a deployment issue. Command-line operations can also be included in shell scripts and automation pipelines."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "question": "What is a Linux shell?",
        "answer": "A Linux shell is a command interpreter that accepts commands from users or scripts and requests the operating system to execute them. It provides features such as command execution, variables, loops, conditions, input and output redirection, and pipelines.\n\nCommon Linux shells include Bash, Zsh, and Dash. Bash is widely used for system administration and automation scripts.\n\nFor example, an engineer can write a Bash script that checks disk usage, verifies whether a service is running, and records the results in a log file. The shell acts as the interface between the script or user and the operating system."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "What is the difference between the Linux kernel and the shell?",
        "answer": "The Linux kernel is the central part of the operating system that manages hardware, memory, CPU scheduling, processes, devices, and system-level operations. It works behind the scenes to provide essential operating-system functionality.\n\nThe shell is a command interpreter that allows users and scripts to interact with the operating system. It accepts commands and requests the kernel and other system utilities to perform operations.\n\nFor example, when an administrator runs a command to list files, the shell interprets the command and starts the required program. That program requests filesystem information through operating-system interfaces, which are managed by the kernel."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "What is the Linux filesystem?",
        "answer": "The Linux filesystem is the structure used to organize and store files and directories. Linux uses a hierarchical directory structure that starts from the root directory, represented by a forward slash (/).\n\nImportant directories include /etc for system configuration, /var for changing data such as logs, /home for user directories, /tmp for temporary files, /usr for many user-space programs and libraries, and /opt for optional application software.\n\nFor example, when troubleshooting an application, an engineer may inspect its configuration under /etc, application logs under /var/log, and deployment files under an application-specific directory. Knowing filesystem locations makes server investigation faster."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "What is the root directory in Linux?",
        "answer": "The root directory is the top-level directory of the Linux filesystem. It is represented by a single forward slash (/), and every other file and directory exists somewhere beneath it in the directory hierarchy.\n\nIt is different from the root user account. The root directory is a filesystem location, while the root user is an administrative account with extensive system privileges.\n\nFor example, paths such as /etc/ssh, /var/log, and /home/ubuntu all begin from the root directory. Administrators use absolute paths starting with / when they need to refer to a specific location regardless of the current working directory."
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "question": "What is the difference between an absolute path and a relative path?",
        "answer": "An absolute path identifies a file or directory from the root of the filesystem. It always begins with a forward slash (/), such as /var/log/application.log.\n\nA relative path identifies a location based on the current working directory. It does not begin from the filesystem root and can use directory references such as ., .., or a directory name.\n\nFor example, if an engineer is working inside /opt/app, the relative path logs/app.log refers to /opt/app/logs/app.log. The absolute path /opt/app/logs/app.log identifies the same file independently of the current directory."
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "question": "What is the pwd command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The pwd command stands for print working directory. It displays the absolute path of the directory in which the current shell session is working.\n\nThis command is useful when an engineer connects to a server or moves between directories and wants to confirm the current location before running commands.\n\nFor example, before deleting or modifying application files, an administrator can run pwd to confirm that the shell is inside the intended deployment directory. This simple verification helps prevent accidental changes to the wrong location."
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "question": "What is the ls command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The ls command lists the files and directories available in a specified location. Without an argument, it normally displays the contents of the current working directory.\n\nCommon options include -l for detailed information, -a for hidden files, -h for human-readable sizes when used with suitable size-display options, and -R for recursive listing.\n\nFor example, an engineer can use ls -lah to inspect files, permissions, ownership, and file sizes in an application directory. This is useful when checking whether deployment artifacts or configuration files were created successfully."
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "question": "What is the cd command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The cd command stands for change directory. It is used to move from one directory to another within the Linux filesystem.\n\nAn engineer can use cd with an absolute path, a relative path, or special directory references. For example, cd /var/log moves to the system log directory, while cd .. moves to the parent directory.\n\nThis command is commonly used during server administration and troubleshooting. Before inspecting application files or executing maintenance commands, engineers should navigate to the correct directory and verify their location."
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "question": "What is the mkdir command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The mkdir command is used to create directories in the Linux filesystem. It can create one directory or multiple directories depending on the arguments provided.\n\nThe -p option creates missing parent directories as needed and does not report an error merely because the requested directory already exists.\n\nFor example, a deployment script may use mkdir -p /opt/company/app/logs to create the required application directory structure. Directory ownership and permissions should also be configured correctly so that the application account can access the required locations."
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "question": "What is the touch command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The touch command is commonly used to create an empty file when the specified file does not exist. When the file already exists, it normally updates its access and modification timestamps.\n\nFor example, an engineer can use touch deployment.log to create a log file before running a script that writes deployment information into it.\n\nThe command is also useful in automation when a workflow expects a marker file or placeholder file to exist. Administrators should understand that touching an existing file changes timestamps and does not erase its contents."
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "question": "What is the cp command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The cp command is used to copy files and directories from one location to another. It supports options for recursive copying, preserving selected file attributes, and controlling overwrite behavior.\n\nFor example, during an application deployment, an engineer may copy a new configuration file into the application's configuration directory. The -r option is commonly used when copying directories.\n\nBefore copying production configuration files, administrators should verify the source and destination paths. They should also consider ownership, permissions, backups, and whether overwriting an existing file could affect a running application."
      },
      {
        "number": 17,
        "question": "What is the mv command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The mv command is used to move files or directories from one location to another. It can also rename files and directories when the source and destination are within the same filesystem location.\n\nFor example, an engineer may rename an old application configuration file to application.conf.backup before deploying a replacement configuration.\n\nThe mv command can overwrite an existing destination depending on the options and environment. In production, engineers should verify the destination and preserve important configuration files before performing file replacement operations."
      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "question": "What is the rm command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The rm command is used to remove files and directories. It can permanently delete filesystem entries, so administrators must use it carefully, especially on production servers.\n\nThe -r option removes directories recursively, while -f forces removal without interactive confirmation in many situations. Combining powerful options without checking the target path can cause serious data loss.\n\nFor example, an engineer may remove an obsolete temporary deployment file after verifying that it is no longer required. Before using rm in automation, scripts should validate variables and paths, avoid unsafe wildcards, and protect critical directories."
      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "What is the cat command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The cat command is used to display, combine, or read file contents through standard output. It is commonly used to inspect small configuration files and text files.\n\nFor example, an engineer can run cat /etc/os-release to view operating-system identification information or cat application.conf to inspect an application configuration file.\n\nFor large log files, cat may produce too much output and make investigation difficult. Commands such as less, head, tail, and grep are often more suitable for reviewing large files or searching for specific information."
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "question": "What is the grep command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The grep command searches text for lines matching a specified pattern. It is commonly used to find error messages, application events, configuration values, and other important information in log files.\n\nFor example, an engineer can search an application log for the word ERROR to identify possible failures. Options such as -i perform case-insensitive matching, while -n displays matching line numbers.\n\nGrep is especially useful when combined with other Linux commands through pipelines. During production troubleshooting, engineers can narrow down large log files and focus on the messages related to a specific incident."
      },
      {
        "number": 21,
        "question": "What is the find command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The find command searches for files and directories based on conditions such as name, type, size, ownership, and modification time. It can search through directory trees recursively.\n\nFor example, an engineer can use find to locate application log files that were modified recently or identify large files consuming storage space.\n\nThe find command can also execute actions on matching results, but destructive actions should be reviewed carefully. In production, administrators should first run a search to verify the exact matching files before deleting or changing them."
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "question": "What is the chmod command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The chmod command changes the permission settings of files and directories. Linux permissions control whether the owner, group, and other users can read, write, or execute an item.\n\nPermissions can be represented symbolically, such as u+x, or numerically, such as 755 and 644. For example, chmod 755 script.sh grants the owner full permissions and grants read and execute permissions to the group and others.\n\nThis command is important during application deployments because scripts must have appropriate execution permissions. Administrators should avoid granting unnecessary write or execute permissions, especially on sensitive production files."
      },
      {
        "number": 23,
        "question": "What is the chown command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The chown command changes the ownership of files and directories. Ownership normally includes a user and optionally a group, which influence how filesystem permissions are applied.\n\nFor example, an application directory may need to be owned by a dedicated application account so that the service can read configuration files and write to permitted directories.\n\nAn engineer can use chown with appropriate user and group values to correct ownership problems. Recursive ownership changes should be performed carefully because changing an entire directory tree may affect system files or other applications."
      },
      {
        "number": 24,
        "question": "What is the difference between chmod and chown?",
        "answer": "chmod changes the permission bits of a file or directory, while chown changes its ownership. Permissions determine which operations the owner, group, and other users are allowed to perform.\n\nFor example, an application deployment file may be owned by the deployment account but may not have execute permission. In that case, chmod can correct the execution permission. If the file belongs to the wrong account, chown may be required instead.\n\nDuring production troubleshooting, engineers should inspect both ownership and permissions before changing them. Correct ownership with incorrect permissions, or correct permissions with incorrect ownership, can still prevent an application from accessing a file."
      },
      {
        "number": 25,
        "question": "What is the ps command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The ps command displays information about running processes. It can show process identifiers, users, CPU and memory information, execution time, and command details depending on the selected options.\n\nFor example, an engineer can use ps aux to inspect processes running on a Linux server. This helps identify whether an application process is running and which user started it.\n\nDuring application troubleshooting, the process list can reveal duplicate processes, unexpected commands, or processes consuming resources. It is often used together with grep, top, and other process-management commands."
      },
      {
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What is the top command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The top command provides a live view of running processes and system resource usage. It displays information such as CPU utilization, memory usage, process identifiers, and process ownership.\n\nFor example, when a production application becomes slow, an engineer can use top to identify whether a process is consuming unusually high CPU or memory resources.\n\nThe command refreshes its display periodically and helps administrators observe changing system activity. It is useful for initial investigation, but detailed analysis may require additional tools and historical monitoring data."
      },
      {
        "number": 27,
        "question": "What is the kill command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The kill command sends a signal to a process identified by its process ID. It is commonly used to request that a process terminate or to send another supported signal.\n\nFor example, an administrator may send the TERM signal to request graceful shutdown of an application process. If a process does not respond, a stronger signal such as KILL may be considered as a last resort.\n\nBefore terminating a production process, engineers should identify what it does, understand its impact, and check whether a service manager or application-specific shutdown method should be used instead."
      },
      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "What is the df command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The df command displays filesystem disk-space usage. It reports information such as total capacity, used space, available space, and mount points.\n\nFor example, an engineer can run df -h to view disk usage in human-readable units. If a production server reports that it cannot write log files, df can help determine whether a filesystem is full.\n\nA filesystem reaching 100 percent usage may affect applications, databases, logging, and system operations. Engineers should identify which filesystem is full and investigate the files consuming space before deleting or expanding storage."
      },
      {
        "number": 29,
        "question": "What is the du command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The du command estimates the disk space used by files and directories. It is useful for identifying which directories are consuming significant storage on a server.\n\nFor example, an engineer can use du -sh /var/log to check the total size of the log directory. This helps investigate situations where disk usage is increasing unexpectedly.\n\nThe df command reports filesystem-level usage, while du estimates usage from directory contents. Using both commands together helps distinguish between a full filesystem and a particular directory that is consuming a large amount of space."
      },
      {
        "number": 30,
        "question": "What is the free command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The free command displays information about system memory and swap usage. It helps administrators understand how much memory is available, used, and allocated to buffers and caches.\n\nFor example, an engineer can run free -h to inspect memory information in human-readable units. This is useful when an application becomes slow or the server experiences memory pressure.\n\nLinux uses available memory for caching to improve performance, so a low free-memory value does not automatically mean that the system is out of memory. Engineers should examine available memory, swap activity, and process-level usage before diagnosing a memory problem."
      },
      {
        "number": 31,
        "question": "What is the systemctl command in Linux?",
        "answer": "The systemctl command is used to manage and inspect services and other units controlled by systemd. It supports operations such as starting, stopping, restarting, enabling, disabling, and checking the status of services.\n\nFor example, an engineer can use systemctl status nginx to inspect the current status of the Nginx service. The command can also help determine whether a service is configured to start automatically during system boot.\n\nWhen a service fails, systemctl status provides useful initial information. Engineers can then inspect the service logs and configuration to identify the underlying cause instead of repeatedly restarting the service without investigation."
      },
      {
        "number": 32,
        "question": "What is the difference between systemctl start and systemctl enable?",
        "answer": "systemctl start starts a service immediately for the current system session. It does not necessarily configure the service to start automatically after the machine reboots.\n\nsystemctl enable configures a service to start automatically during the appropriate system boot process. It does not necessarily start the service immediately.\n\nFor example, an engineer may use systemctl enable --now nginx when both immediate startup and automatic startup at boot are required. Understanding this difference is important when preparing application servers and ensuring required services return after a reboot."
      },
      {
        "number": 33,
        "question": "What is SSH in Linux?",
        "answer": "SSH stands for Secure Shell. It is a protocol used to establish encrypted remote connections to another computer. Linux administrators use SSH to manage servers securely through a command-line interface.\n\nSSH commonly supports password-based authentication and public-key authentication. In production environments, organizations often use approved SSH keys, restricted accounts, network controls, and centralized access policies.\n\nFor example, a DevOps engineer can connect to a cloud-based Linux server using an authorized SSH key and inspect application services or logs. SSH connectivity depends on network access, the SSH server, authentication, and account permissions."
      },
      {
        "number": 34,
        "question": "What is a Linux process?",
        "answer": "A Linux process is an instance of a running program. Each process is normally assigned a process identifier, called a PID, which allows the operating system and administrators to identify and manage it.\n\nProcesses use system resources such as CPU time, memory, file descriptors, and network connections. A single application may create multiple processes or threads depending on its architecture.\n\nFor example, a Java application server runs as a process on a Linux machine. Engineers can inspect it using commands such as ps and top, investigate its resource usage, and check whether it has stopped unexpectedly."
      },
      {
        "number": 35,
        "question": "What is a Linux log file and why is it important in DevOps?",
        "answer": "A Linux log file records events generated by the operating system, services, applications, and other components. Logs help administrators understand what happened before, during, and after an incident.\n\nCommon log locations include /var/log, although applications may maintain their own log directories. On systemd-based systems, journalctl can be used to inspect logs collected by the system journal.\n\nFor example, if an application fails to start after deployment, an engineer can inspect the application logs and system service logs to identify errors such as missing configuration, permission problems, unavailable dependencies, or port conflicts. Logs are essential evidence during production troubleshooting and incident investigation."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "tool": "ArgoCD",
    "category": "Basic Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is Argo CD?",
        "answer": "Argo CD is a declarative GitOps continuous delivery tool for Kubernetes. It continuously monitors application configuration stored in Git repositories and compares that desired configuration with the actual state of applications running in Kubernetes clusters.\n\nWhen differences are detected, Argo CD reports the synchronization status and can synchronize the cluster with the desired configuration. It supports automated and manual synchronization.\n\nFor example, when a developer updates a Kubernetes Deployment image in Git, Argo CD can detect the change and deploy the updated configuration to the target cluster."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "What is GitOps?",
        "answer": "GitOps is an operational approach in which Git repositories are used as the source of truth for infrastructure and application configuration. Changes are reviewed, version-controlled, and delivered through automated reconciliation.\n\nInstead of manually changing Kubernetes resources, engineers update configuration in Git. A GitOps controller such as Argo CD observes the repository and works to make the cluster match the declared configuration.\n\nFor example, a team can store Deployment, Service, and ConfigMap manifests in Git. When an approved change is merged, Argo CD detects it and synchronizes the Kubernetes environment."
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "question": "Why is Argo CD used in DevOps?",
        "answer": "Argo CD is used to automate and standardize application deployments to Kubernetes through GitOps. It provides visibility into application health, synchronization status, deployment history, and configuration differences.\n\nIt reduces the need for engineers to manually apply Kubernetes manifests and helps maintain consistency between Git and running clusters.\n\nFor example, a DevOps team can use Jenkins to build and push a Docker image, update the image tag in a Git repository, and allow Argo CD to deploy that version to Kubernetes."
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "question": "What is a declarative deployment?",
        "answer": "A declarative deployment describes the desired final state of an application rather than listing every manual command required to reach that state.\n\nKubernetes manifests declare resources such as Deployments, Services, and ConfigMaps. Argo CD compares those declarations with the live cluster resources and identifies differences.\n\nFor example, a manifest may declare that an application should run three replicas. If the live Deployment has only two replicas, the desired configuration indicates that the cluster should return to three."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "question": "What is an Argo CD Application?",
        "answer": "An Argo CD Application is a custom resource that connects a source repository or configuration source with a destination Kubernetes cluster and namespace.\n\nIt defines where the desired configuration is stored, which path or chart should be used, and where the resources should be deployed. It can also define synchronization policies.\n\nFor example, an Application can point to a Git repository path containing production manifests and deploy those resources into the production namespace of a specific Kubernetes cluster."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "question": "What is the Argo CD architecture?",
        "answer": "Argo CD commonly includes components such as the API server, repository server, application controller, and Redis. These components work together to provide the user interface, repository access, application comparison, and synchronization functionality.\n\nThe API server provides the interface for users and automation. The repository server retrieves and renders configuration from Git or supported sources. The application controller compares desired and live states and performs synchronization when requested or configured.\n\nFor example, when Git changes, the repository server retrieves the new manifests and the controller evaluates whether the cluster needs an update."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "What is the Argo CD API server?",
        "answer": "The Argo CD API server exposes the Argo CD API and user interface. It handles user requests, authentication and authorization integration, and operations such as inspecting applications and requesting synchronization.\n\nAdministrators use the API server through the web interface, CLI, or API clients. Access should be protected using appropriate authentication, TLS, and network controls.\n\nFor example, an engineer can use the Argo CD UI to inspect an application's health and synchronization status or use the CLI to request a deployment."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "What is the Argo CD repository server?",
        "answer": "The repository server is responsible for retrieving application configuration from supported sources and generating the Kubernetes manifests that Argo CD needs to compare with the live cluster.\n\nIt can work with Git repositories, Helm charts, Kustomize configurations, and other supported configuration sources. Repository access credentials and rendering tools must be configured correctly.\n\nFor example, when an Application points to a Helm chart in Git, the repository server retrieves the chart and renders the Kubernetes manifests using the configured values."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "What is the Argo CD application controller?",
        "answer": "The application controller continuously monitors Argo CD Applications and compares the desired resources with the live resources in Kubernetes.\n\nIt determines whether an application is Synced or OutOfSync and evaluates resource health. Depending on the configured synchronization policy, it can also apply changes to the destination cluster.\n\nFor example, if someone manually changes a Deployment replica count in Kubernetes while Git still declares another value, the controller detects the difference and reports the application as OutOfSync."
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "question": "What is the difference between desired state and live state?",
        "answer": "Desired state is the configuration declared in the Git repository or another configured source. It describes how the application and its Kubernetes resources are expected to look.\n\nLive state is the actual configuration currently stored in the Kubernetes cluster. Argo CD compares these two states to identify differences.\n\nFor example, Git may declare five application replicas while the live Deployment has three. This difference indicates that the live state does not currently match the desired state."
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "question": "What is synchronization in Argo CD?",
        "answer": "Synchronization is the process of applying the desired configuration from the configured source to the destination Kubernetes cluster.\n\nArgo CD calculates the required resource changes and applies them during synchronization. Synchronization can be started manually or automatically when automated sync is enabled.\n\nFor example, after a developer updates a Deployment image tag in Git, an engineer can trigger synchronization so the cluster receives the new Deployment specification."
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "question": "What is the difference between Synced and OutOfSync?",
        "answer": "Synced means the live application resources match the desired configuration according to Argo CD's comparison rules. OutOfSync means Argo CD has detected differences between the desired and live states.\n\nAn application may become OutOfSync because of a Git change that has not been deployed or because someone manually changed a Kubernetes resource.\n\nFor example, if Git declares two replicas but an administrator manually changes the Deployment to four replicas, Argo CD may report OutOfSync. Engineers should inspect the difference before deciding whether to synchronize."
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "question": "What is application health in Argo CD?",
        "answer": "Application health describes the operational condition of the Kubernetes resources managed by an Argo CD Application. It provides an indication of whether resources appear healthy, progressing, degraded, or in another supported state.\n\nHealth is evaluated using resource-specific health assessments. For example, a Deployment may be considered healthy when its required replicas are available.\n\nAn application can be Synced but Degraded. This means the configuration matches Git, but one or more resources are not operating as expected. Engineers should inspect Kubernetes events, pod status, and application logs."
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "question": "What is automated sync in Argo CD?",
        "answer": "Automated sync allows Argo CD to apply eligible changes from the configured source without requiring an engineer to manually start synchronization each time.\n\nWhen a new desired configuration is detected, Argo CD can automatically synchronize the application according to its configured policy.\n\nFor example, a development environment may automatically deploy every approved Git change. Production environments may use manual approvals or additional controls before synchronization."
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "question": "What is self-healing in Argo CD?",
        "answer": "Self-healing is an automated synchronization behavior that helps restore live resources when they are changed manually and no longer match the desired configuration.\n\nWhen self-healing is enabled as part of automated synchronization, Argo CD can detect certain live changes and apply the desired configuration again.\n\nFor example, if someone manually scales a Deployment from three replicas to one while Git declares three, self-healing can restore the declared replica count. Teams should understand the impact before enabling it in production."
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "question": "What is prune in Argo CD?",
        "answer": "Pruning is the process of deleting managed Kubernetes resources that are no longer present in the desired configuration.\n\nWhen pruning is enabled for synchronization, Argo CD can remove resources that were previously managed by the Application but have been removed from its source configuration.\n\nFor example, if an obsolete Service manifest is removed from Git, pruning can delete the corresponding managed Service from the cluster. Production teams should review deletion impact before enabling automatic pruning."
