      },
      {
        "number": 27,
        "question": "What is a sync wave in Argo CD?",
        "answer": "Sync waves allow Kubernetes resources to be applied in a controlled order during synchronization. Resources can be assigned wave numbers so that some resources are created before others.\n\nThis is useful when application components depend on resources being available first. Argo CD processes lower-numbered waves before higher-numbered waves.\n\nFor example, a team may create a required configuration resource before deploying an application that depends on it. Sync waves help express this deployment ordering in the resource configuration."
      },
      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "What is a sync hook in Argo CD?",
        "answer": "A sync hook is a specially annotated Kubernetes resource that runs at a particular stage of an Argo CD synchronization operation.\n\nHooks are commonly used for operations such as database migrations, pre-deployment checks, and post-deployment validation. Their execution phase is controlled through supported hook annotations.\n\nFor example, a migration Job can run before a new application version is deployed. Engineers must design hooks carefully because failures, retries, and cleanup behavior can affect deployment results."
      },
      {
        "number": 29,
        "question": "What is a rollback in Argo CD?",
        "answer": "A rollback is the process of returning an application to a previously known configuration or release state. In GitOps environments, a common approach is to revert the Git change or select an appropriate previous revision through supported Argo CD operations.\n\nThis helps recover from deployments that introduce application failures or configuration problems.\n\nFor example, if a newly deployed image causes errors, the team can revert the image tag change in Git and allow Argo CD to synchronize the previous version. The team should also consider database compatibility and other changes that may not be automatically reversible."
      },
      {
        "number": 30,
        "question": "What is Argo CD application history?",
        "answer": "Argo CD application history records information about previous application synchronization operations and deployed revisions.\n\nIt helps engineers understand which source revision was deployed, when synchronization occurred, and which release may have introduced a problem.\n\nFor example, if an application starts failing after a deployment, an engineer can inspect its history and compare the current revision with the previous successful revision. This information supports incident investigation and rollback planning."
      },
      {
        "number": 31,
        "question": "What is repository authentication in Argo CD?",
        "answer": "Repository authentication allows Argo CD to access private source repositories using approved credentials or authentication methods.\n\nDepending on the repository provider and configuration, authentication may use SSH keys, HTTPS credentials, tokens, or supported integrations. Credentials must be protected and have only the permissions required for repository access.\n\nFor example, if Argo CD cannot retrieve a private Git repository, engineers should verify the repository URL, credential validity, permissions, and network access before investigating application synchronization."
      },
      {
        "number": 32,
        "question": "What is the difference between Argo CD and Jenkins?",
        "answer": "Jenkins is a general-purpose automation server commonly used to build, test, package, and orchestrate software delivery pipelines. Argo CD is a GitOps continuous delivery tool focused on reconciling Kubernetes applications with configuration stored in Git.\n\nThey can work together rather than replacing one another. Jenkins can build a Docker image and update the image reference in Git, while Argo CD detects the repository change and deploys the desired Kubernetes configuration.\n\nThis separation allows CI and CD responsibilities to be handled by tools suited to their respective tasks."
