    "tool": "EKS",
    "category": "Basic Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "question": "What is Amazon EKS?",
        "answer": "Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) is a managed Kubernetes service provided by AWS. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while customers configure worker capacity, networking, security, and workloads. DevOps teams use EKS to deploy and operate containerized applications with Kubernetes integrated into AWS services."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is an EKS cluster?",
        "answer": "An EKS cluster is a Kubernetes environment running on AWS. It includes a managed control plane and connects to compute resources such as EC2 worker nodes or Fargate. Organizations commonly create separate clusters for development, staging, and production environments."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the EKS control plane?",
        "answer": "The EKS control plane contains Kubernetes components such as the API server, scheduler, and controller manager. AWS manages the control-plane infrastructure and availability. Worker nodes communicate with the API server to register, receive workloads, and report their status."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are worker nodes in EKS?",
        "answer": "Worker nodes provide compute resources for Kubernetes pods. They are commonly EC2 instances registered with an EKS cluster. Engineers manage node capacity, operating-system updates, kubelet health, CPU, memory, and network connectivity to keep application workloads healthy."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is an EKS managed node group?",
        "answer": "A managed node group is an AWS-managed way to provision and maintain a group of EC2 worker nodes. It integrates with EKS for node lifecycle operations and supported updates. Engineers configure instance types, subnets, scaling limits, labels, and update settings."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is an EKS Fargate profile?",
        "answer": "A Fargate profile specifies which Kubernetes pods should run on AWS Fargate instead of customer-managed EC2 worker nodes. It uses namespace and optional label selectors to match pods. Fargate reduces worker-node administration for supported workloads but has specific storage, networking, and workload limitations."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the difference between EKS managed nodes and Fargate?",
        "answer": "Managed node groups run pods on EC2 instances, giving teams more control over node configuration and capacity. Fargate runs selected pods on serverless compute without requiring customers to manage worker EC2 instances. The decision depends on workload requirements, operating-system access, storage, cost, and operational control."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is eksctl?",
        "answer": "eksctl is a command-line utility designed to create and manage Amazon EKS clusters. It simplifies cluster creation, node-group management, and other common operations. Engineers can use declarative configuration files to create repeatable cluster environments."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the AWS VPC CNI plugin in EKS?",
        "answer": "The Amazon VPC CNI plugin provides pod networking by assigning VPC IP addresses to pods on supported worker nodes. It integrates Kubernetes pod networking with AWS VPC networking. Insufficient subnet IP addresses or network-interface capacity can prevent new pods from obtaining IP addresses."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is CoreDNS in EKS?",
        "answer": "CoreDNS is the DNS service used by Kubernetes workloads to resolve service names and other cluster DNS records. It runs as pods inside the cluster and is commonly managed as an EKS add-on. If CoreDNS becomes unhealthy, applications may experience service-discovery failures even when their pods are running."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is kube-proxy in EKS?",
        "answer": "kube-proxy implements Kubernetes Service networking on worker nodes by maintaining network rules that direct traffic to service backends. It helps route traffic to the correct pods. If kube-proxy is unhealthy or misconfigured, communication through Kubernetes Services may fail."
      },
      {
        "question": "How does IAM authentication work with EKS?",
        "answer": "IAM authentication allows AWS identities to authenticate to an EKS Kubernetes API server. The IAM identity is associated with cluster access permissions through supported EKS access-management mechanisms. Authentication establishes identity, while Kubernetes authorization determines which operations that identity can perform."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are EKS access entries?",
        "answer": "EKS access entries associate IAM principals with access to an EKS cluster. They can grant access through EKS access policies or Kubernetes groups, depending on the configuration. They provide a supported access-management mechanism and can reduce dependence on the older aws-auth ConfigMap workflow."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the aws-auth ConfigMap?",
        "answer": "The aws-auth ConfigMap is a traditional EKS mechanism for mapping IAM users and roles to Kubernetes users and groups. It has historically been used to grant worker-node and administrator access. Administrators should understand the cluster authentication mode before changing it or migrating to EKS access entries."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is IRSA in EKS?",
        "answer": "IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA) allows Kubernetes service accounts to assume specific AWS IAM roles through an OIDC identity provider. It gives application pods AWS permissions without distributing long-lived access keys. For example, an application can receive permission to read a particular S3 bucket through its service account."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the EKS OIDC provider?",
        "answer": "The EKS OIDC provider establishes an identity trust relationship between Kubernetes service-account tokens and AWS IAM. It is commonly configured to enable IRSA. The IAM role trust policy must reference the correct cluster OIDC provider and restrict access to the intended service account."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the EBS CSI driver in EKS?",
        "answer": "The Amazon EBS Container Storage Interface driver allows Kubernetes workloads to provision and attach Amazon EBS volumes through persistent volume claims. It connects Kubernetes storage operations with AWS EBS. The driver requires suitable IAM permissions and compatible node placement and volume availability-zone settings."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is an EKS add-on?",
        "answer": "An EKS add-on is an integrated Kubernetes component that AWS supports for use with EKS. Examples include VPC CNI, CoreDNS, and kube-proxy. Administrators can manage compatible add-on versions through EKS and should check version compatibility before upgrades."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is an EKS cluster endpoint?",
        "answer": "The EKS cluster endpoint is the Kubernetes API server endpoint used by kubectl and cluster components to communicate with the control plane. It can be configured for public access, private access, or both. Endpoint access settings and network restrictions determine which clients can reach the API server."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the difference between public and private EKS endpoints?",
        "answer": "A public endpoint allows access to the Kubernetes API through a publicly reachable endpoint, subject to configured access restrictions. A private endpoint allows API access through the cluster VPC networking path. Production environments often restrict public access and use private connectivity for administrative operations."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you connect kubectl to an EKS cluster?",
        "answer": "The AWS CLI can update the local kubeconfig using aws eks update-kubeconfig with the cluster name and region. kubectl then uses the configured context and AWS authentication process to communicate with the cluster API server. The IAM identity must have the required cluster access permissions."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is an EKS node group scaling configuration?",
        "answer": "A node group's scaling configuration defines the desired, minimum, and maximum number of worker nodes. The minimum controls the lower capacity boundary, the maximum limits scaling, and the desired value represents target capacity. These values should reflect workload demand, availability requirements, and budget constraints."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is Cluster Autoscaler in EKS?",
        "answer": "Cluster Autoscaler adjusts the size of compatible node groups when pods cannot be scheduled because of insufficient node capacity or when nodes are underutilized. It works with configured node groups and AWS permissions. It changes worker-node capacity, while the Kubernetes scheduler determines where pods run."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is Karpenter in EKS?",
        "answer": "Karpenter is a Kubernetes node-provisioning system that can launch and terminate compute capacity based on pending pod requirements and configured constraints. It can select suitable instance types and capacity options. It differs from traditional Cluster Autoscaler workflows and requires appropriate AWS permissions and scheduling configuration."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the AWS Load Balancer Controller?",
        "answer": "The AWS Load Balancer Controller manages AWS load balancers for Kubernetes Services and Ingress resources. It can provision Application Load Balancers or Network Load Balancers according to resource configuration. It requires suitable IAM permissions, network configuration, and compatible Kubernetes resources."
      },
      {
        "question": "How does EKS integrate with Amazon ECR?",
        "answer": "Amazon Elastic Container Registry stores container images that EKS workloads can use. Worker nodes or supported compute environments need appropriate permissions and network access to pull images. Image-pull failures can result from incorrect image references, missing repositories, authentication problems, or unavailable network paths."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are Kubernetes namespaces in EKS?",
        "answer": "Namespaces logically separate Kubernetes resources within a cluster. Teams can organize workloads by environment, application, or ownership and apply namespace-scoped access controls and resource quotas. Namespaces provide organization but do not automatically create complete security isolation between workloads."
      },
      {
        "question": "How does EKS support high availability?",
        "answer": "EKS provides a managed control plane designed to operate across multiple availability zones. Customers improve workload availability by distributing worker nodes and application replicas across suitable availability zones, configuring health checks, and using disruption-aware deployment strategies. Applications also need resilient storage and dependency designs."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you upgrade an EKS cluster?",
        "answer": "An EKS upgrade involves planning a supported Kubernetes version transition, checking deprecated APIs and add-on compatibility, and upgrading the control plane before dependent node components according to AWS guidance. Managed node groups and add-ons must also be updated appropriately. Testing workloads and monitoring rollout health reduce upgrade risk."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are EKS control plane logs?",
        "answer": "EKS control plane logging can publish selected Kubernetes API server, audit, authenticator, controller manager, and scheduler logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. These logs help investigate API access, authentication, scheduling, and control-plane-related problems. Logging categories should be enabled according to operational and security requirements."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you monitor an EKS cluster?",
        "answer": "An EKS cluster can be monitored using CloudWatch, Prometheus, Grafana, Kubernetes metrics, and application telemetry. Engineers monitor node capacity, pod restarts, pending pods, API errors, network health, and application latency. Alerts should connect meaningful symptoms to operational response procedures."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is EKS Pod Identity?",
        "answer": "EKS Pod Identity is an AWS capability that associates Kubernetes service accounts with IAM roles so applications can obtain AWS credentials. It provides an alternative to IRSA for supported configurations. Administrators configure the association and required agent and IAM permissions according to AWS guidance."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the difference between EKS and self-managed Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "EKS is a managed Kubernetes service in which AWS operates the control plane and integrates it with AWS services. With self-managed Kubernetes, the organization is responsible for operating more of the control-plane infrastructure and lifecycle. EKS reduces some operational overhead but customers still manage workloads, access, networking, and worker capacity."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a pod disruption budget in EKS?",
        "answer": "A Pod Disruption Budget (PDB) specifies the minimum number or percentage of application pods that should remain available during voluntary disruptions. It helps protect workloads during operations such as node maintenance and draining. A PDB does not guarantee availability during involuntary failures or replace proper replica and availability-zone design."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the purpose of EKS security groups?",
        "answer": "Security groups act as virtual firewalls for supported AWS network interfaces used by EKS control-plane communication and worker nodes. Their inbound and outbound rules determine which network connections are allowed. Incorrect rules can prevent nodes from registering, pods from communicating, or administrators from reaching the Kubernetes API."
      }
    ]
