        "question": "What is the difference between a Pod and a Deployment in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "A Pod is the smallest deployable Kubernetes unit and contains one or more tightly coupled containers. A Deployment manages a ReplicaSet and maintains the desired number of interchangeable Pod replicas while supporting declarative updates. In production, teams normally create Deployments for stateless applications instead of manually creating individual Pods."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "What is a ReplicaSet in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "A ReplicaSet ensures that a specified number of matching Pods are running at a given time. It creates replacement Pods when existing ones are deleted or become unavailable, according to its controller behavior. Deployments normally manage ReplicaSets, allowing application teams to perform controlled updates and rollbacks."
      },
      {
