      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "How do you recover work created in a detached HEAD state?",
        "answer": "First identify the commit using `git log` or `git reflog`, then create a branch at that commit using `git switch -c recovery-branch`. The new branch preserves the commit in normal branch history. Verify the branch and push it if the work needs to be shared with the team."
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "question": "What is a Git submodule?",
        "answer": "A Git submodule allows one repository to reference another repository at a specific commit. It is useful when a project depends on an independently maintained codebase. Developers must initialize and update submodules during checkout, and CI pipelines must explicitly handle them when cloning the main repository."
      },
      {
        "number": 21,
        "question": "What is a Git submodule update --init --recursive command?",
        "answer": "This command initializes configured submodules, checks out the commits recorded by the parent repository, and recursively initializes nested submodules. It is commonly used in CI pipelines when the application depends on external repositories. Missing submodule initialization can cause builds to fail because expected source directories are empty."
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "question": "What is Git LFS?",
        "answer": "Git Large File Storage replaces large files in the Git history with small pointer files and stores the actual file content through an LFS storage service. It is useful for binary assets such as design files, large datasets, and media files. Teams must install and configure Git LFS on developer machines and CI agents so the actual content is downloaded."
      },
      {
        "number": 23,
        "question": "What is a .gitignore file?",
        "answer": "A .gitignore file defines untracked files and directories that Git should normally ignore. It is used to exclude generated output, local configuration, caches, and temporary files from source control. It does not automatically stop tracking a file that was already committed; that requires removing it from the index while retaining it locally when appropriate."
      },
      {
        "number": 24,
        "question": "How do you stop tracking a file without deleting it locally?",
        "answer": "Use `git rm --cached filename` to remove a file from Git's index while leaving the local working copy in place. Add the file pattern to `.gitignore` if it should remain untracked in future. Commit the change so other contributors also stop tracking that file."
      },
      {
        "number": 25,
        "question": "What is the difference between git diff and git diff --staged?",
        "answer": "git diff shows changes between the working tree and the staging area, while `git diff --staged` shows changes staged for the next commit compared with HEAD. Reviewing both helps developers understand exactly what will be committed and what remains unstaged. This is especially useful when a working directory contains several unrelated changes."
      },
      {
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What is the Git index or staging area?",
        "answer": "The Git index is the staging area that records the content selected for the next commit. Developers use `git add` to place changes into the index and can inspect them using `git diff --staged`. It allows a commit to contain selected changes even when other modifications remain in the working directory."
      },
      {
        "number": 27,
        "question": "What is an interactive rebase?",
        "answer": "Interactive rebase allows developers to review and modify a sequence of commits, including reordering, combining, editing, or dropping commits. It is useful for cleaning up local feature-branch history before submitting a pull request. Rewriting commits that others already depend on can disrupt shared branches, so coordination is important."
      },
      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "What is squashing commits in Git?",
        "answer": "Squashing combines multiple commits into a smaller number of commits, often one logical change. It can make a feature branch easier to review and maintain. Developers can squash commits through interactive rebase or a pull-request merge strategy, depending on the team's repository policy."
      },
      {
        "number": 29,
        "question": "What is a fast-forward merge?",
        "answer": "A fast-forward merge occurs when the target branch has not diverged from the source branch, so Git can simply move the target branch reference forward. No merge commit is required for this operation. It keeps history linear but does not preserve an explicit merge commit for the branch integration."
      },
      {
        "number": 30,
        "question": "What is a Git merge commit?",
        "answer": "A merge commit is a commit with multiple parents that records the integration of separate lines of development. It preserves the history of the branches that were combined. Teams may prefer merge commits when they want to retain the context of feature-branch integration."
      },
      {
        "number": 31,
        "question": "What is a protected branch in Git hosting platforms?",
        "answer": "A protected branch is a branch governed by repository rules that restrict operations such as direct pushes, force pushes, or deletion. It can require pull-request reviews, passing CI checks, or authorized approvals before changes are merged. Protecting main or release branches reduces accidental changes and enforces team review practices."
      },
      {
        "number": 32,
        "question": "What is a pull request or merge request?",
        "answer": "A pull request or merge request proposes changes from one branch to another and provides a place for code review, discussion, and automated checks. Reviewers can inspect the diff and request modifications before integration. CI pipelines commonly run tests and security checks against the proposed changes."
      },
      {
        "number": 33,
        "question": "What is the difference between fork and clone in Git?",
        "answer": "A clone creates a local copy of a repository on a developer's machine. A fork creates a separate server-side copy under another account or organization on platforms that support forking. Forks are commonly used in open-source contribution workflows, where developers push changes to their fork and submit a pull request to the original repository."
      },
      {
        "number": 34,
        "question": "What is a Git commit hash?",
        "answer": "A commit hash is a content-derived identifier for a Git commit object, which records information such as its tree, parent commits, author, committer, and message. It allows developers to refer to a specific point in repository history. The hash changes when the commit object content changes, such as after amending or rebasing a commit."
      },
      {
        "number": 35,
        "question": "How do you compare two Git branches?",
        "answer": "Use commands such as `git diff branchA..branchB` to compare file changes and `git log branchA..branchB` to inspect commits reachable from one branch but not the other. The exact range direction matters, so engineers should confirm which branch is the base and which is the comparison target. Pull-request tools provide a convenient review of the same differences."
      },
      {
        "number": 36,
        "question": "What is git blame?",
        "answer": "git blame shows the last commit that modified each line of a file, along with the author and commit information. It helps identify the history behind a particular implementation or configuration line. It should be used as an investigation aid rather than as a way to assign blame to an individual."
      },
      {
        "number": 37,
        "question": "What is git clean?",
        "answer": "git clean removes untracked files and directories from the working tree according to the selected options. Use `git clean -n` first to preview what would be removed, and use the appropriate force option only after confirming the targets. It can delete local files that are not committed, so careful review is essential."
      },
      {
        "number": 38,
        "question": "What is the difference between git switch and git checkout?",
        "answer": "git switch is a command focused on switching branches and creating branches, while git checkout is an older multi-purpose command used for branch switching and restoring files. Using switch for branch operations and restore for file restoration makes commands clearer. Both remain useful in modern Git workflows."
      },
      {
        "number": 39,
        "question": "How do you sign Git commits?",
        "answer": "Git commits can be signed using configured GPG or SSH signing keys, allowing others to verify that a commit was signed by a trusted key. Developers configure signing in Git and ensure the public key is registered with the repository hosting platform when required. Signed commits help strengthen source-code provenance and release verification."
      },
      {
        "number": 40,
