  {
    "tool": "Grafana",
    "category": "Intermediate Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is Grafana and how is it used in a production DevOps environment?",
        "answer": "Grafana is an observability and visualization platform used to query, visualize, explore, and alert on operational data from sources such as Prometheus, Loki, Elasticsearch, InfluxDB, and cloud monitoring services. In production, DevOps teams build dashboards for application availability, request rate, error rate, latency, infrastructure utilization, Kubernetes workloads, and deployment health. A dashboard can combine metrics and logs from different data sources to help engineers investigate an incident. For example, when API latency increases, an engineer can correlate request duration, pod CPU, memory, restarts, and application logs. Grafana does not normally collect metrics itself; it queries configured data sources. Production readiness also requires access controls, dashboard provisioning, alert routing, data-source security, and tested queries."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "How do you configure a Prometheus data source in Grafana?",
        "answer": "A Prometheus data source connects Grafana to a Prometheus-compatible query endpoint. In Grafana, navigate to Connections or Data Sources, add Prometheus, configure the server URL, select the appropriate access mode, and save and test the connection. In Kubernetes, the URL is commonly an internal Service DNS name such as http://prometheus-operated.monitoring.svc:9090, depending on the installation. Check that Grafana can resolve the Service name, reach the port, and authenticate if a proxy or gateway is used. A successful connection test proves basic connectivity but not that every dashboard query is correct. Validate with a simple query such as up, then test the actual application metrics and labels required by dashboards."
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot a Grafana dashboard showing No Data?",
        "answer": "Start by checking the dashboard time range, refresh interval, selected data source, and template variables. Open the affected panel, inspect its query, and use Query Inspector to review the request, response, errors, and returned series. Run the metric query directly in Prometheus to determine whether the metric exists independently of Grafana. Check label filters, metric names, time range, datasource selection, and transformations that may remove returned data. A dashboard may also reference a renamed metric after an application deployment. Test a simple query such as up to distinguish a datasource problem from a panel-specific problem. Correct the query or datasource and verify that the panel returns expected values over the incident time window."
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "question": "What are Grafana variables and how are they used in reusable dashboards?",
        "answer": "Grafana variables provide dynamic values that can be selected by dashboard users and reused in panel queries, titles, links, and other dashboard settings. Common variables represent cluster, namespace, application, environment, instance, or region. For example, a namespace variable can populate a PromQL selector such as sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{namespace=\"$namespace\"}[5m])). Query variables can obtain their values from a data source, while custom variables provide a predefined list. In production, variables help maintain one dashboard for multiple clusters or environments instead of duplicating dashboards. Troubleshoot empty variables by checking the variable query, datasource, refresh setting, label availability, and variable dependencies. Avoid overly broad variable queries that create excessive dashboard load."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot a Grafana panel that displays incorrect metric values?",
        "answer": "Inspect the panel query, selected data source, query time range, legend format, transformations, units, and aggregation. Use Query Inspector to examine the raw response returned by the data source. Compare the query result with the same expression in Prometheus or the underlying source. Common causes include incorrect rate windows, wrong units, duplicate series, inappropriate aggregation, label mismatches, and a transformation that sums or filters data incorrectly. For example, displaying bytes as megabytes without conversion can make a value appear incorrect. Verify whether the metric is a counter or gauge and use suitable PromQL functions. Correct the expression and test it against known traffic or infrastructure behavior before publishing the dashboard."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "question": "What is the difference between Grafana dashboards, panels, and folders?",
        "answer": "A dashboard is a collection of visualizations and related settings for a monitoring purpose. A panel is an individual visualization, such as a time series, table, stat, gauge, or log view. Folders organize dashboards by team, application, environment, or operational responsibility. In production, a platform team may maintain Kubernetes infrastructure dashboards while application teams own service-level dashboards. Folder permissions can control who can view or edit dashboards, but datasource permissions and organizational roles must also be considered. Use consistent naming, ownership, descriptions, and tags so on-call engineers can locate the right dashboard quickly. Provision important dashboards through version-controlled configuration to avoid losing operational content."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "How do you configure Grafana alert rules and troubleshoot alerts that do not fire?",
        "answer": "Grafana alert rules evaluate queries or expressions against configured data sources and generate alert instances when defined conditions are met. Configure the query, reduce or threshold expression, evaluation interval, pending period, labels, annotations, and notification policy. If an alert does not fire, inspect the rule evaluation state, query result, data-source health, evaluation errors, threshold, pending duration, and rule labels. Check whether the query returns No Data or Error and whether the configured No Data behavior is appropriate. Verify that the notification policy routes the alert to the expected contact point. Test with a controlled condition in a non-production environment and confirm both firing and resolved notifications reach the intended destination."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "What are Grafana contact points and notification policies?",
        "answer": "Contact points define where Grafana sends alert notifications, such as email, Slack-compatible endpoints, PagerDuty, or other supported integrations. Notification policies determine how alerts are grouped, matched, routed, and delivered to contact points. In production, routing often uses labels such as team, service, environment, and severity. If an alert reaches the wrong team, inspect the alert labels, policy tree, matching conditions, grouping configuration, and contact-point status. Check credentials and endpoint connectivity without exposing tokens in logs. Use a test notification to verify delivery. Maintain a documented ownership model so alerts have a clear destination and do not silently fall through to an unintended default route."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot Grafana datasource connection failures?",
        "answer": "A datasource failure can result from an incorrect URL, DNS resolution problem, network restriction, TLS certificate error, authentication failure, expired credentials, or unavailable backend. Use Grafana's Save and Test operation and inspect Grafana server logs for the detailed error. In Kubernetes, test connectivity from the Grafana pod or its network environment rather than from a developer workstation. Verify Service DNS, namespace, port, NetworkPolicy, proxy settings, certificates, and authentication configuration. Confirm that the backend is healthy independently. Avoid placing passwords or tokens in dashboard JSON or troubleshooting output. After correcting the connection, test a basic query and then verify representative production panels."
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "question": "How do you provision Grafana dashboards and data sources through configuration files?",
        "answer": "Grafana provisioning allows teams to define data sources, dashboards, alerting resources, and other settings as files managed by automation. This makes monitoring configuration repeatable across environments. Data-source provisioning commonly uses YAML files, while dashboard provisioning defines providers that load dashboard JSON from a directory or mounted volume. In Kubernetes, these files can be supplied through ConfigMaps, Secrets, Helm chart values, or a controlled provisioning mechanism. Validate file syntax, mount paths, permissions, datasource URLs, and dashboard JSON before deployment. Provisioning can overwrite UI changes depending on configuration, so establish Git as the source of truth and document how engineers propose dashboard changes. Verify provisioned resources after Grafana restarts."
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "question": "What is Grafana Query Inspector and how does it help in production troubleshooting?",
        "answer": "Query Inspector displays details about a panel's data request and response, including the query sent to the datasource, execution timing, returned data, and errors. It helps distinguish a slow datasource query from a Grafana transformation or visualization problem. During an incident, inspect the query and compare it with a direct backend query. Check the time range, interval, variables, request parameters, response size, and query duration. If the backend query itself is slow, investigate Prometheus or the selected datasource. If the backend returns correct data but the panel is wrong, inspect transformations, field configuration, and units. Query Inspector is especially useful for diagnosing dashboards that load slowly or show unexpected values."
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot slow-loading Grafana dashboards?",
        "answer": "Slow dashboards can result from expensive datasource queries, large time ranges, excessive panels, high-cardinality metrics, frequent refreshes, large responses, transformations, or insufficient Grafana resources. Use Query Inspector to identify slow panels and compare backend query duration with total panel rendering time. Test queries directly against the datasource and inspect Prometheus query performance where applicable. Reduce unnecessary series, use appropriate aggregation, apply recording rules for repeated expensive expressions, and avoid refreshing every panel too frequently. Check Grafana CPU, memory, database latency, and plugin behavior. Optimize one panel at a time and compare load time before and after the change. Ensure performance improvements do not remove important incident investigation detail."
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "question": "How do Grafana users, teams, roles, and permissions work?",
        "answer": "Grafana access control determines which users and teams can view, edit, or administer dashboards, folders, data sources, and alerting resources. Organizations can use built-in roles, team membership, folder permissions, and more granular permissions depending on the Grafana edition and configuration. In production, application teams should generally receive access to their own dashboards while platform administrators manage shared infrastructure resources. If a user cannot see a dashboard, verify organization membership, team membership, folder permissions, dashboard permissions, and datasource access. Authentication through an identity provider may also affect group synchronization. Apply least privilege, periodically review access, and test permissions using a representative non-administrator account."
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "question": "How do you configure dashboard time ranges and refresh intervals for production monitoring?",
        "answer": "Dashboard time ranges define the period of data shown, while refresh intervals control how frequently panels request updated data. A short refresh interval can be useful for live incident response but may increase query load substantially when many users and dashboards are active. Historical investigation often requires a wider time range and does not need rapid refresh. Configure sensible defaults based on the monitoring use case and datasource scrape interval. If a dashboard appears stale, verify its time range, refresh setting, timezone, and latest sample timestamp. If it becomes slow after reducing the refresh interval, inspect query concurrency and backend load. Provide clear dashboard defaults so on-call engineers can quickly switch between current and historical views."
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot Grafana dashboards after a Prometheus metric or label rename?",
        "answer": "A metric or label rename can break dashboards, variables, alerts, and links that depend on the old name. Search the dashboard JSON and panel queries for the old metric or label, then inspect the current metric endpoint and Prometheus query results. Use Query Inspector to confirm the actual query Grafana sends. Check whether the application deployment introduced a new metric name, changed label values, or removed a series. Update the dashboard and alert definitions together, and test with representative data in a non-production environment. During a production migration, consider temporary metric compatibility if dashboards must continue working while old application versions are running. Document the metric change so downstream monitoring owners can update their queries."
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "question": "How do you use Grafana transformations and what problems can they introduce?",
        "answer": "Grafana transformations modify query results before visualization. Examples include joining data frames, filtering fields, renaming columns, calculating fields, reducing values, and organizing table columns. They are useful when a dashboard needs to combine or reshape results from one or more queries. However, transformations can hide data, create incorrect joins, change field types, or produce unexpected aggregation. If a panel shows incorrect values, temporarily disable transformations and inspect the raw query response. Verify join keys, field names, null handling, and transformation order. Keep complex data calculations in a well-tested datasource query or recording rule where practical. Document transformations because their behavior may not be obvious to another engineer during an incident."
      },
      {
        "number": 17,
        "question": "How do you manage Grafana dashboards as code using Git?",
        "answer": "Dashboard-as-code stores dashboard JSON or provisioning definitions in a version-controlled repository. Engineers review dashboard changes through pull requests, validate JSON and queries, and deploy approved dashboards through CI/CD or provisioning. This provides history, repeatability, peer review, and recovery when an incorrect dashboard change is introduced. Define folder ownership, datasource references, dashboard identifiers, and environment-specific settings carefully. Avoid committing credentials or environment-specific secrets in dashboard files. Test dashboard imports against the target Grafana version because schema and plugin differences can affect compatibility. After deployment, verify that dashboards load, variables populate, and critical panels return data. Keep production dashboards traceable to a Git commit."
      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot Grafana when dashboards disappear after a restart?",
        "answer": "Dashboards may disappear if Grafana uses ephemeral storage, provisioning files are missing, the database is unavailable, or the deployment points to a different organization or instance. Check Grafana logs, database connectivity, persistent volume mounts, provisioning configuration, and the dashboard provider path. In Kubernetes, verify that the expected ConfigMaps or volumes are mounted in the Grafana pod. Confirm that the dashboard exists in the expected organization and folder. If the deployment uses an external database, check database health and backup recovery. Restore dashboards from version-controlled JSON or a verified backup rather than recreating them manually without preserving history. Test persistence through a controlled restart."
      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "How do you monitor Grafana itself in a production environment?",
        "answer": "Grafana should be monitored as a production service because a monitoring outage can reduce incident visibility. Monitor process or pod availability, CPU, memory, restart count, HTTP request rate, request errors, response latency, database connectivity, and datasource query failures. Use Grafana's supported internal metrics or application health endpoints where configured. Alert on sustained unavailability, resource saturation, repeated datasource errors, and database failures. For Kubernetes deployments, inspect pod readiness, events, volume mounts, and service endpoints. Maintain an independent monitoring path so Grafana failure does not prevent alert delivery. Periodically test dashboard access, alert evaluation, and notification delivery from an operational user account."
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "question": "Describe an end-to-end production Grafana dashboard or alerting incident investigation.",
        "answer": "When a production dashboard or alerting workflow fails, first determine whether Grafana is unavailable, the datasource is failing, a query returns no data, a panel is incorrect, or notifications are not delivered. Record the affected dashboard, panel, datasource, time range, user impact, and recent changes. Inspect Grafana health and logs, datasource tests, Query Inspector output, dashboard variables, transformations, alert evaluation state, and notification policies. Compare the same metric directly in the backend to isolate Grafana from Prometheus or another datasource. Correct the datasource, query, permission, provisioning, or alert-routing problem using a reviewed change. Validate the dashboard with real data and send a controlled notification test. Document the root cause and add provisioning, query, and alert-delivery checks to CI or operational monitoring."
      },
      {
        "number": 21,
        "question": "How do you configure Grafana alerting for CPU and memory utilization?",
        "answer": "Create separate alert rules for CPU and memory using metrics from the configured datasource. With Prometheus, a CPU utilization query can use rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total[5m]) and aggregate by namespace, pod, or container. Memory usage can be compared with the relevant container memory limit or node capacity. Define a threshold based on workload behavior, an evaluation interval, and a pending period to avoid alerting on brief spikes. Add labels such as team, service, namespace, and severity, and include annotations explaining the impact and investigation steps. Verify that the query handles missing limits and terminated containers correctly. Test the rule using known high-utilization behavior and confirm that the notification reaches the correct on-call team."
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "question": "What is the difference between Grafana-managed alert rules and data source-managed alert rules?",
        "answer": "Grafana-managed alert rules are evaluated by Grafana's alerting engine and can combine supported queries and expressions across data sources. Data source-managed rules are created and evaluated by the underlying datasource, such as Prometheus or a compatible metrics backend. The choice affects where evaluation occurs, which query features are available, how rules are stored, and how they are managed. In production, teams should decide which system owns evaluation and avoid maintaining conflicting copies of the same alert. When troubleshooting, identify the rule type first, then inspect the correct evaluation engine, rule status, query errors, labels, and notification routing. Document ownership and provisioning so engineers know where a rule must be changed."
      },
      {
        "number": 23,
        "question": "How do you use Grafana Loki to investigate application logs?",
        "answer": "Loki is a log aggregation system that Grafana can query using LogQL. In production, applications and infrastructure agents send logs to Loki, where engineers filter them by labels such as namespace, pod, container, and application. A query such as {namespace=\"payments\", container=\"api\"} selects matching streams, while filters can narrow results to error messages or request identifiers. Start with a narrow time range and known labels, then expand the investigation using trace IDs, correlation IDs, and timestamps. If logs are missing, check the log collector, ingestion errors, Loki readiness, label configuration, and retention. Avoid using highly variable values as indexed labels because excessive label cardinality can increase operational cost and query complexity."
      },
      {
        "number": 24,
        "question": "How do you correlate Grafana metrics, logs, and traces during a production incident?",
        "answer": "Correlation connects different observability signals so engineers can move from a symptom to its cause. Begin with a metric anomaly such as increased request latency or HTTP 5xx responses. Identify the affected service, namespace, pod, and time window, then inspect related logs for matching timestamps and request identifiers. If distributed tracing is configured, use trace IDs to inspect the request path across services and locate the slow or failing dependency. Grafana can link panels and data sources through variables, derived fields, and trace-to-logs or metrics-to-traces configuration where supported. Ensure timestamps, labels, service names, and trace identifiers are consistent. Validate the links using a real test request before relying on them during an incident."
      },
      {
        "number": 25,
        "question": "What is metric cardinality and why can it affect Grafana dashboard performance?",
        "answer": "Metric cardinality is the number of distinct time series produced by combinations of metric names and label values. Labels such as user ID, request ID, session ID, or unrestricted URL paths can create a very large number of series. This increases storage, ingestion, query processing, and dashboard rendering costs in systems such as Prometheus. Grafana may appear slow because its queries retrieve or aggregate too many series. Inspect the query result size and identify labels that create excessive combinations. Prefer bounded labels such as service, environment, method, and status class. Avoid simply increasing Grafana resources when the actual issue is high-cardinality instrumentation. Work with application teams to correct metric design and validate query performance afterward."
      },
      {
        "number": 26,
        "question": "How do you configure Grafana dashboard links and data links for incident investigation?",
        "answer": "Dashboard links navigate to related dashboards, while data links use a selected field or value to open a related destination. In production, an engineer may select a Kubernetes pod from a table and navigate to its logs, node metrics, or workload dashboard. Configure links with stable identifiers and variables such as cluster, namespace, pod, and time range. Verify URL encoding and ensure the destination dashboard defines the expected variables. Avoid exposing sensitive query parameters or credentials in links. Test links using realistic values containing special characters and confirm that the destination displays the same incident time window. Good links reduce investigation time by preserving context between operational views."
      },
      {
        "number": 27,
        "question": "How do you back up and restore Grafana dashboards and configuration?",
        "answer": "A production backup strategy should cover dashboards, datasource definitions, alert rules, provisioning files, plugins, and Grafana's database or persistent storage as applicable. Store dashboard JSON and provisioning configuration in Git so they can be recreated consistently. Back up the Grafana database using a method appropriate for the configured database engine and verify that backups are recoverable. Do not include passwords or tokens in ordinary dashboard exports. During recovery, restore the database and required configuration in a controlled order, then verify organizations, folders, permissions, datasources, dashboards, and alerting resources. Perform periodic restore tests rather than assuming that a successful backup job guarantees recoverability."
      },
      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot Grafana plugin installation or plugin compatibility problems?",
        "answer": "Plugins extend Grafana with additional visualizations, datasource integrations, or application features. A plugin may fail because it is incompatible with the Grafana version, unsigned under the configured policy, missing from the installation, or unable to initialize due to dependency or permission problems. Inspect Grafana startup logs and plugin-related errors, then verify the installed plugin version and its compatibility requirements. In containerized deployments, check whether plugin installation persists across pod replacement and whether outbound network access is required. Test upgrades in a non-production environment and pin approved versions in deployment configuration. Avoid disabling plugin security checks as a quick production workaround. Validate dashboards and queries that depend on the plugin after remediation."
      },
      {
        "number": 29,
        "question": "How do you use Grafana annotations during incident management?",
        "answer": "Annotations add time-based events to dashboards, making it easier to correlate metric changes with deployments, configuration updates, maintenance, or incidents. They can be created manually or supplied from external systems through supported integrations and APIs. In production, deployment annotations help engineers determine whether a latency increase began immediately after a release. Include a useful event title, timestamp, environment, service, and reference to the change or incident record. Verify timezone handling and ensure annotation queries return events for the dashboard's selected time range. Avoid creating noisy annotations for every minor event. A consistent annotation strategy improves historical investigations and post-incident analysis."
      },
      {
        "number": 30,
        "question": "How do you monitor SLOs and error budgets using Grafana?",
        "answer": "Service-level objectives define measurable reliability targets, such as the percentage of successful requests over a rolling period or a latency threshold. Grafana can visualize the underlying service-level indicators and calculate error-budget consumption using metrics from Prometheus or another datasource. For an availability SLI, compare successful requests with total eligible requests while excluding traffic that the SLO definition explicitly excludes. Display short-window and long-window behavior so teams can identify rapid error-budget burn as well as gradual degradation. Alert rules should reflect the agreed SLO policy rather than arbitrary infrastructure thresholds. Validate query boundaries, traffic volume, and missing data behavior with the service owner before using the dashboard for release decisions."
      },
      {
        "number": 31,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot Grafana when users receive HTTP 502 or 504 errors?",
        "answer": "A 502 or 504 can originate from a reverse proxy, ingress controller, load balancer, Grafana process, or an upstream dependency. Identify which component generated the response by checking browser network details, proxy logs, ingress events, and Grafana server logs. Verify Grafana pod readiness, service endpoints, resource pressure, database connectivity, and datasource query duration. A 504 may occur when a dashboard query exceeds a proxy or application timeout. A 502 may indicate that the proxy cannot establish a valid upstream connection. Check recent deployment, TLS, routing, and timeout changes. Fix the failing layer rather than increasing every timeout blindly, then validate login, dashboard loading, datasource queries, and alert evaluation."
      },
      {
        "number": 32,
        "question": "How do you manage Grafana secrets and datasource credentials securely?",
        "answer": "Datasource passwords, API tokens, and integration credentials should be managed through an approved secrets-management mechanism rather than committed to Git or embedded in dashboard JSON. Use Grafana's secure datasource configuration and supported secret references, or integrate with an external secret manager through the deployment platform. In Kubernetes, protect Secret access with RBAC and encryption controls and restrict who can read pod environment variables or mounted files. Rotate credentials through a documented process and verify that dependent integrations continue working. Review logs and exported configuration for accidental secret exposure. Apply least privilege to datasource accounts and use separate credentials for different environments. Test secret rotation before applying it to critical production integrations."
      },
      {
        "number": 33,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot Grafana when alert notifications are delayed?",
        "answer": "Delayed notifications may result from long evaluation intervals, pending periods, notification grouping timers, contact-point retries, network failures, or a slow external notification provider. Inspect the alert rule's evaluation interval and pending duration, then review alert state transition timestamps. Check notification policy grouping and timing settings, contact-point health, Grafana logs, and the external provider's delivery status. Determine whether the delay occurs before the alert fires or after Grafana attempts delivery. Avoid reducing every timer without considering alert noise and provider limits. Test with a controlled alert and measure both evaluation and delivery latency. Document the intended notification service-level target and alert on sustained delivery failures."
      },
      {
        "number": 34,
        "question": "How do you design Grafana dashboards for multiple Kubernetes clusters?",
        "answer": "A multi-cluster dashboard should use consistent metric labels and a cluster variable so users can switch between clusters without maintaining separate copies of every dashboard. Include cluster, namespace, workload, and environment dimensions where those labels are reliably available. Queries should filter on the selected cluster and avoid accidentally combining unrelated clusters. Provide overview panels for node capacity, pod health, API errors, networking, and workload availability, then link to more detailed dashboards. Validate that datasource selection and label conventions are consistent across clusters. If clusters use separate Prometheus instances, use an appropriate datasource strategy and test failover behavior. Clearly display the selected cluster to prevent engineers from investigating or changing the wrong environment."
      },
      {
        "number": 35,
        "question": "What is the role of Grafana's unified alerting and how does it improve operations?",
        "answer": "Unified alerting provides a centralized model for managing alert rules, evaluation, labels, notification policies, and contact points in Grafana. It helps teams manage alerts across supported data sources through a consistent interface and routing model. In production, labels can route service alerts to application teams while infrastructure alerts go to platform operations. Grouping can reduce duplicate notifications when many related instances fail together. However, incorrect labels or policy matching can send alerts to the wrong destination. Review rule ownership, evaluation behavior, grouping keys, and notification routes during implementation. Keep alert definitions version-controlled where possible and periodically test firing, resolution, and escalation behavior."
      },
      {
        "number": 36,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot Grafana after migrating from one instance or environment to another?",
        "answer": "A Grafana migration can fail because datasource URLs, dashboard identifiers, plugin versions, folder permissions, organizations, alerting configuration, or database settings differ between environments. Before migration, inventory dashboards, datasources, plugins, alert rules, users, teams, and provisioning files. Export or provision the required resources without exposing secrets. After migration, verify datasource connectivity and inspect dashboard queries for hard-coded hostnames or environment-specific labels. Confirm that variables populate and notification policies route to the correct test destinations. Compare dashboard counts and critical panel results with the source instance. Keep the old environment available for rollback until the new instance has passed operational validation."
      },
      {
        "number": 37,
        "question": "How do you reduce noisy Grafana alerts without hiding real production incidents?",
        "answer": "Start by identifying alerts that repeatedly fire without requiring action, then determine whether the cause is an unstable threshold, short-lived spike, missing-data behavior, duplicate rule, or incorrect grouping. Use suitable evaluation windows, pending periods, rate calculations, and meaningful thresholds based on service behavior. Group related alert instances and route them according to service ownership and severity. Add inhibition or suppression logic only when it is supported and the dependency relationship is understood. Preserve alerts for user-impacting symptoms such as availability, latency, and error-budget burn. Review alert history with the on-call team and test the revised rules against historical incident data before deploying them."
      },
      {
        "number": 38,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot a Grafana dashboard that works for administrators but not for ordinary users?",
        "answer": "When a dashboard works for administrators but fails for other users, investigate organization membership, folder and dashboard permissions, datasource permissions, team synchronization, and authentication-provider group mappings. Check whether the user can access the dashboard itself and whether the datasource allows that user's query. Review variables or links that may expose resources the user cannot access. Test using a representative non-admin account rather than assuming administrator access reflects normal behavior. If role synchronization is enabled, verify that the identity provider sends the expected groups. Grant only the minimum required permissions and retest both dashboard viewing and datasource queries."
      },
      {
        "number": 39,
        "question": "How do you plan Grafana capacity for a production monitoring platform?",
        "answer": "Grafana capacity planning should consider concurrent users, dashboard count, panel count, query complexity, refresh frequency, response sizes, alert evaluation load, plugins, and database usage. Measure CPU, memory, request latency, datasource query duration, and database performance under realistic load. Identify expensive panels and optimize their queries before scaling Grafana horizontally. For larger deployments, review the supported high-availability architecture, shared database requirements, session handling, and externalized configuration. Ensure dashboards do not create unnecessary query storms during incidents when many engineers open the same views. Load-test important dashboards and alert rules, establish resource thresholds, and periodically review capacity as teams and monitored workloads grow."
      },
      {
        "number": 40,
        "question": "Describe a production Grafana observability platform improvement project.",
        "answer": "A practical improvement project begins by identifying operational gaps such as missing service dashboards, unreliable alerts, slow queries, inconsistent labels, or unclear ownership. Work with application and platform teams to define standard service metrics, dashboard templates, alert labels, and SLO indicators. Configure datasources securely and provision dashboards and alert rules through Git and CI/CD. Add deployment annotations and links between metrics, logs, and traces to accelerate incident investigation. Establish alert routing by team and severity, then test notifications and recovery behavior. Measure dashboard load time, alert delivery latency, and incident investigation time before and after rollout. Document standards, review access permissions, and assign owners so the observability platform remains maintainable."
      }
