      {
        "number": 14,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot EKS Cluster Autoscaler when it does not add worker nodes?",
        "answer": "Cluster Autoscaler observes unschedulable pods and adjusts supported Auto Scaling Groups when configured capacity can satisfy their requirements. If it does not scale, inspect the Cluster Autoscaler deployment logs and events, IAM permissions, node group discovery tags, Auto Scaling Group configuration, and pod scheduling messages. Check whether the pending pods can fit any configured instance type after accounting for resource requests, taints, labels, and volume topology. Review minimum and maximum node group sizes and AWS service quotas. A pod requiring an unavailable zone or instance capability may remain pending even when autoscaling is healthy. Correct the scheduling or scaling configuration and verify that a new node joins and the pod becomes Ready."
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot EKS application connectivity to AWS services using IAM Roles for Service Accounts?",
        "answer": "IAM Roles for Service Accounts, or IRSA, allows a Kubernetes service account to assume an IAM role through the cluster's OIDC identity provider. If an application receives AccessDenied or credential errors, verify that the cluster OIDC provider exists, the service account annotation references the intended role, and the IAM trust policy has the correct issuer, audience, and subject conditions. Check the pod's service account, environment, token projection, and application credential chain. Confirm that the IAM policy grants the required action on the intended resource. Inspect AWS identity from a controlled diagnostic process without printing secret credentials. Correct the trust or permission policy and verify the application can access only the required AWS resources."
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
