        "answer": "EKS Pod Identity provides a mechanism for associating Kubernetes service accounts with IAM roles so workloads can obtain AWS credentials without embedding long-lived keys. It requires the relevant EKS Pod Identity Agent and an association between the cluster, namespace, service account, and IAM role. If credentials are unavailable, inspect the association, agent pod health, service account name, IAM role trust configuration, and application credential-provider behavior. Check whether the pod is actually using the expected service account. Distinguish credential retrieval failures from IAM authorization failures such as AccessDenied. Correct the association or policy and validate the required AWS API operation using the workload identity."
      },
      {
        "number": 17,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot persistent volume mounting failures in EKS?",
        "answer": "Persistent volume failures can result from missing storage classes, CSI driver problems, IAM permissions, volume attachment limits, availability-zone mismatch, filesystem errors, or access mode conflicts. Inspect kubectl describe pod, kubectl get pvc,pv -A, storage class configuration, and CSI controller or node plugin logs. For EBS-backed storage, confirm that the volume and node are in compatible availability zones and that the EBS CSI driver has the required permissions. Check volume attachment objects and AWS volume state. Do not delete a production PVC or volume to force recovery without a data-preservation plan. Resolve the driver, topology, or permission issue and verify that the workload mounts the expected data safely."
