        "answer": "Start by identifying the affected cluster, region, namespace, workload, user impact, and incident start time. Check EKS control plane status and endpoint access, then inspect nodes, pods, events, add-ons, networking, DNS, storage, and ingress. Use kubectl describe and logs to isolate workload failures, and AWS CLI commands to inspect node groups, EC2 instances, load balancers, IAM, and cluster configuration. Correlate the incident with deployments, cluster upgrades, node replacements, IAM changes, subnet capacity, and security-group modifications. Preserve evidence before changing resources. Apply the safest approved recovery, such as restoring a compatible node group, correcting IAM or networking, or rolling back an application release. Validate node readiness, pod health, service endpoints, external access, and application SLOs. Document root cause and preventive actions."
