      {
        "number": 25,
        "question": "How do you configure and troubleshoot EKS Horizontal Pod Autoscaler?",
        "answer": "Horizontal Pod Autoscaler adjusts the number of workload replicas based on observed metrics such as CPU, memory, or custom metrics. It requires appropriate metrics availability and meaningful resource requests for resource-based scaling. Inspect the HPA with kubectl describe hpa NAME -n NAMESPACE and check current metrics, target values, desired replicas, and events. If metrics are unavailable, inspect metrics-server or the configured custom metrics adapter and its permissions and connectivity. If scaling does not occur, review minimum and maximum replicas, stabilization behavior, scaling policies, and workload resource requests. Verify that the cluster has enough node capacity for the additional pods. Test scaling under controlled load and confirm that replicas become Ready."
      },
      {
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What is the difference between Cluster Autoscaler and Karpenter in EKS?",
        "answer": "Cluster Autoscaler primarily adjusts the size of configured node groups or Auto Scaling Groups when pods cannot be scheduled or nodes are underutilized. Karpenter provisions and consolidates compute capacity based on pending pod requirements and configured NodePools or related provisioning resources. Their configuration and operational models differ, so teams should choose a supported architecture and avoid overlapping controllers managing the same capacity unintentionally. If capacity does not appear, inspect pending pod events, controller logs, IAM permissions, subnet and security-group discovery, instance availability, quotas, and scheduling constraints. Verify architecture, zones, taints, and resource requirements. Monitor node creation and workload placement after changing provisioning configuration."
