      {
        "number": 38,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot EKS deployments that fail because of missing ConfigMaps or Secrets?",
        "answer": "A workload may fail to start when a referenced ConfigMap or Secret does not exist in the same namespace, has an incorrect key, or is mounted using an invalid path. Inspect pod events and the Deployment or StatefulSet manifest for env, envFrom, and volume references. Run kubectl get configmap,secret -n NAMESPACE and verify resource names and required keys without printing secret values. Check whether the deployment pipeline creates configuration resources before updating the workload. Also consider whether a Secret was renamed, deleted, or not synchronized from an external secret manager. Correct the resource reference or restore the required object through the approved process, then confirm that new pods start and load the expected configuration."
