        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is a Jira workflow and how does it support a production software delivery process?",
        "answer": "A Jira workflow defines the sequence of statuses and transitions that an issue follows during its lifecycle. A typical software workflow may include Open, Selected for Development, In Progress, Code Review, Testing, Ready for Release, and Done. Transitions can have conditions, validators, and post-functions that control who can move an issue and what information is required. In a production DevOps team, workflows help coordinate developers, QA engineers, operations, and release managers. For example, a deployment task should not move to Done until the release is deployed and validated. When issues become stuck, inspect the current status, available transitions, permissions, validators, and workflow conditions instead of changing statuses directly without understanding the process."
