        "answer": "Inventory existing fields before creating new ones and identify whether a shared field already meets the requirement. Review field contexts, project and issue-type associations, screens, field configurations, automation rules, filters, and integrations. Limit contexts to the projects that need the field to avoid clutter and unintended reporting changes. Before modifying a widely used field, test the impact in a controlled project, communicate the change to project owners, and validate create, edit, search, and reporting behavior after rollout.",
        "number": 25,
        "question": "How would you manage Jira custom fields across multiple projects?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "A field configuration scheme maps field configurations to issue types in a project. A field configuration controls whether fields are required, hidden, or rendered in a particular way. For example, a Bug may require reproduction steps while a Story requires acceptance criteria. When a field is unexpectedly hidden or mandatory, inspect the project's field configuration scheme and the configuration mapped to the issue type. Also verify the relevant screens because a field can be configured but absent from the screen used for that operation.",
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What is the purpose of Jira field configuration schemes?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Begin with operational questions such as how many production incidents remain open, which critical issues are aging, and where SLA breaches are occurring. Create saved filters with clear project, priority, status, service, and time criteria. Add suitable gadgets such as Filter Results, Created vs Resolved, and Two Dimensional Filter Statistics. Verify filter sharing and dashboard permissions so viewers can access the underlying data. Compare dashboard counts with direct issue searches and document metric definitions before using the dashboard for operational or management decisions.",
        "number": 27,
        "question": "How do you build a useful Jira dashboard for a DevOps operations team?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Validate the project key, field names, operators, status values, and date boundaries in the JQL. Confirm that the current user has permission to browse matching issues because access restrictions can make results appear incomplete. Check custom-field context and the actual stored field values on representative issues. Simplify the query one condition at a time and compare results with a known issue. If the query is used by a dashboard or subscription, verify that the saved filter has not changed and that its sharing permissions are correct.",
        "number": 28,
        "question": "How would you troubleshoot incorrect Jira JQL search results?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Components are project-level subdivisions used to classify work by application module, service, or technical ownership. A payment project might use API, Checkout, Database, and Reporting components. Components can have leads or default assignees and can improve routing, filters, and workload reporting. Establish naming and ownership standards so components remain meaningful. If component-based assignment does not work, confirm that the component exists in the project, its lead or default assignee is configured correctly, and the issue actually has that component selected.",
        "number": 29,
        "question": "What are Jira components and how do they help technical teams?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "An issue type describes the work item, such as Bug, Story, Task, or Epic. An issue type scheme determines which issue types are available in a project and can define the default type. Different projects can use different schemes to reflect their processes. If a user cannot create a required issue type, inspect the project's scheme and verify that the type is included. Also check create screens and project permissions, because an available issue type may still be unusable if required fields or access permissions are missing.",
        "number": 30,
        "question": "How do Jira issue types and issue type schemes work together?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Translate support commitments into measurable goals such as first response and resolution time. Configure start, pause, and stop conditions based on the request lifecycle; for example, pause resolution time while waiting for customer information if the agreement allows it. Set business calendars, working hours, time zones, and priority-specific targets. Test representative status transitions and inspect the SLA panel to verify elapsed and remaining time. Review breached requests and ensure automation, escalation rules, and status changes do not unintentionally reset or stop SLA measurement.",
        "number": 31,
        "question": "How do you configure SLA tracking in Jira Service Management?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Apply least privilege by identifying the exact actions the external team requires, such as browsing selected issues, commenting, or creating requests. Map those actions to dedicated groups or project roles and review the permission scheme and issue security levels. Check whether attachments, comments, boards, dashboards, and related projects also expose information. Test with a representative external account and verify both permitted and denied operations. Record the business justification, access owner, approval, and review or expiry date so access can be removed when no longer needed.",
        "number": 32,
        "question": "How do you give an external team limited access to a Jira project?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "A group is an organization-level collection of users that can be referenced by permissions and other access controls. A project role is a project-specific logical role, such as Developers or Administrators, whose membership can vary between projects. Permission schemes can grant access to project roles, allowing each project to manage its own membership while sharing a common scheme. Use groups for centrally managed membership and roles for project-specific responsibility. When access is unexpected, inspect both group membership and the user's role membership in the affected project.",
        "number": 33,
        "question": "What is the difference between a Jira group and a project role?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Inventory the existing workflow, workflow scheme, issue types, statuses, transitions, conditions, validators, post-functions, and automation dependencies. Identify every project and issue type affected by the new workflow and map existing statuses to valid target statuses. Test migration behavior with representative issues, including resolved, reopened, and in-progress cases. Schedule broad changes through change management, communicate expected behavior, and prepare a rollback or recovery plan. After deployment, validate transitions, permissions, reporting, and automation using controlled test issues and real user roles.",
        "number": 34,
        "question": "How do you plan a safe Jira workflow migration?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Use an automation rule's outgoing web request action to call an external REST endpoint when a configured event occurs. Define the HTTP method, endpoint, authentication, content type, and request body according to the receiving API contract. Smart values can pass issue identifiers and selected fields, but secrets must be protected rather than embedded in plain text. Confirm network reachability and integration permissions. Inspect Jira's automation audit log alongside the receiving service logs to diagnose authentication failures, invalid payloads, HTTP errors, throttling, or timeouts.",
        "number": 35,
        "question": "How can Jira automation integrate with an external REST API?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Assign each external event a stable identifier, such as a monitoring alert ID, and store it in a dedicated Jira field. Before creating an issue, search for an existing issue with that identifier. Make the integration retry-safe because webhook providers may deliver the same event more than once. Use idempotency controls and record successful processing in the integration service. Test duplicate delivery, delayed retries, and concurrent requests. Monitor repeated identifiers and reconcile duplicates through an approved process while preserving audit history and incident relationships.",
        "number": 36,
        "question": "How do you prevent duplicate Jira issues created by monitoring integrations?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Determine whether the slowdown affects one user, one project, a specific operation, or the whole instance. Compare issue browsing, searches, dashboards, and editing. Check application health, CPU and memory, database response times, indexing activity, background jobs, and recent plugin or configuration changes. Review application logs and, for Data Center, inspect node health and load distribution. Collect evidence before restarting services. Reproduce the slow operation, isolate expensive filters or integrations, and coordinate infrastructure or database remediation with the platform team.",
        "number": 37,
        "question": "How would you investigate slow Jira performance reported by users?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Verify compatibility with the exact Jira version and deployment type, vendor support status, security advisories, licensing, and dependencies. Review the app's permissions and the data it can access. Test the upgrade in staging with representative workflows, custom fields, automation, and integrations. Take and verify an appropriate backup and prepare a recovery plan before production changes. After installation, validate application startup, important user journeys, logs, performance, and integration behavior. A successful installation alone does not prove that existing app functionality remains compatible.",
        "number": 38,
        "question": "What should you check before installing or upgrading a Jira app?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Define consistent meanings for priority, severity, component, environment, resolution, and ownership. Use field configurations, controlled option lists, workflow validators, and automation to reduce missing or inconsistent data. Build filters that identify incomplete records, stale issues, invalid resolutions, and unassigned critical work. Review trends with project owners and correct the process that creates poor data instead of relying only on manual cleanup. Document report definitions and validate sample records so teams interpret created, resolved, reopened, and outstanding issue counts consistently.",
        "number": 39,
        "question": "How do you maintain Jira data quality for operational reporting?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Treat configuration changes as controlled operational changes. Record the request, affected projects, dependencies, expected behavior, risk, approval, and rollback approach. Reproduce the configuration in staging and test workflows, permissions, screens, automation, filters, and integrations. Use deployment or migration methods supported by the Jira deployment type, because not every configuration object can be copied automatically. Schedule production implementation, communicate impact, verify critical user journeys, and document the final configuration and evidence for audit and future troubleshooting.",
        "number": 40,
        "question": "How do you manage Jira configuration changes across development, staging, and production?"
      }
    ]
