      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "How do you monitor Tomcat health and performance in a production environment?",
        "answer": "Monitor service availability, HTTP response codes, request latency, throughput, JVM heap and non-heap memory, garbage collection pauses, CPU, process memory, thread pool usage, connection counts, and disk capacity. Use application health endpoints and synthetic checks to verify that critical functionality works rather than only checking whether the Tomcat process is running. JMX metrics can expose JVM and container information when securely configured. Correlate Tomcat metrics with reverse-proxy, database, and downstream service metrics to identify where latency originates. Configure alerts around service-level objectives and resource saturation. Secure management interfaces and JMX access, and avoid exposing administrative endpoints publicly."
