  {
    "tool": "Docker",
    "category": "Advanced Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "answer": "Docker images are composed of read-only layers, while a running container adds a writable layer. Storage drivers such as overlay2 use layered filesystem behavior to present these layers as a unified filesystem. Large numbers of layers, excessive small-file operations, and heavy writes to the container layer can affect build time and runtime I/O. Keep images lean, order Dockerfile instructions to maximize cache reuse, and store persistent application data in volumes or external services. Inspect image history and filesystem usage, and benchmark actual workloads instead of assuming every storage operation has identical cost.",
        "number": 1,
        "question": "Explain Docker image layers, overlay filesystems, and how they affect production container performance."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Use trusted base images, pin versions or digests, minimize installed packages, and build from reviewed source in controlled CI environments. Scan operating-system packages and application dependencies, generate an SBOM, and sign images where supported. Store artifacts in a protected registry with immutable release tags and access controls. Promote the same tested image digest between environments instead of rebuilding different images for production. Verify provenance and signatures during deployment, patch vulnerable dependencies, and retain audit records linking source commit, build pipeline, scan results, and deployed digest.",
        "number": 2,
        "question": "How would you design a secure Docker image supply chain for enterprise production deployments?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "A multi-stage Dockerfile uses separate build and runtime stages so compilers, build tools, and temporary files do not need to be included in the final image. Build the Java artifact in a controlled Maven or Gradle stage, then copy only the required JAR and runtime configuration into a minimal compatible JRE image. Use dependency caching carefully and pin base image digests. Run as a non-root user and configure health checks or orchestrator probes appropriately. Compare image size, build time, startup time, and vulnerability scan results after each optimization.",
        "number": 3,
        "question": "Explain Docker multi-stage builds and how you would optimize them for large Java applications."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Namespaces isolate views of operating-system resources, including process IDs, network interfaces, mounts, users, and hostnames. Control groups account for and limit resources such as CPU and memory, depending on kernel and runtime configuration. Containers still share the host kernel, so they are not equivalent to separate virtual machines. Diagnose resource issues by inspecting container limits, host-level cgroup metrics, process behavior, and kernel messages. Set realistic CPU and memory limits, understand OOM behavior, and avoid granting unnecessary privileges or host namespace access.",
        "number": 4,
        "question": "How do Linux namespaces and cgroups provide isolation and resource control for Docker containers?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Inspect docker inspect state and exit information, container memory usage, configured memory limits, host memory pressure, and kernel logs such as dmesg or journalctl. Distinguish a container memory-limit OOM kill from a host-wide memory shortage. Review application heap settings, native memory, caches, request concurrency, and memory leaks. Increase limits only after measuring host capacity and workload demand. Add memory telemetry and load tests, and validate that the application handles memory pressure predictably rather than entering a restart loop.",
        "number": 5,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot a Docker container that is repeatedly killed by the Linux OOM killer?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Bridge networking provides isolated container networks with NAT and port publishing, while host networking shares the host network namespace and removes normal port isolation. None networking disables ordinary network connectivity, and overlay networking supports multi-host communication in compatible orchestration environments. Choose based on isolation, routing, performance, service discovery, and platform requirements. Inspect docker network inspect, routes, DNS behavior, published ports, and firewall rules when troubleshooting. Avoid exposing unnecessary ports and document how traffic enters and leaves each network boundary.",
        "number": 6,
        "question": "Explain Docker networking modes and how to select the correct mode for a production service."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Do not rely on the container writable layer for data that must survive container replacement. Use named volumes or deliberately managed bind mounts for local persistent data, and use external storage or managed databases when durability, replication, and shared access are required. Define ownership, permissions, backup, restore, encryption, and lifecycle policies. Understand that a Docker volume does not automatically provide high availability or backups. Test restoration onto a replacement host and verify application consistency, especially when databases require clean shutdown or transaction-aware backup procedures.",
        "number": 7,
        "question": "How would you design persistent storage for stateful Docker containers?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Linux capabilities split traditional root privileges into narrower permissions, allowing containers to run without broad administrative power. Seccomp filters can restrict available system calls, while AppArmor or SELinux can enforce mandatory access controls depending on the host. Run as a non-root user, drop unnecessary capabilities, retain only required permissions, and avoid privileged mode unless there is a justified exception. Validate security profiles against application behavior and monitor denied operations. Combine these controls with image scanning, patching, secrets protection, and host hardening.",
        "number": 8,
        "question": "How do Docker capabilities, seccomp, and Linux security modules improve container security?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Compare the exact image digest, architecture, environment variables, mounted files, secrets, network policies, resource limits, runtime version, and host kernel between environments. Inspect startup logs, container exit state, health status, DNS resolution, and dependency connectivity. Check whether local testing accidentally relies on files or credentials absent from the production image. Reproduce the production configuration in a controlled environment and avoid rebuilding a different image as a workaround. Capture the failing conditions and validate the fix using the same immutable artifact intended for release.",
        "number": 9,
        "question": "How do you investigate a Docker container that works locally but fails in production?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "The Docker CLI communicates with the Docker Engine API exposed by the daemon, which manages images, containers, networks, and volumes through the container runtime and host kernel. Check whether the daemon is running, inspect systemd status and journal logs, and verify socket permissions and endpoint configuration. Distinguish daemon unavailability from authorization, TLS, API-version, or resource failures. Avoid granting users unrestricted access to the Docker socket because it effectively provides host-level control. Restart the daemon only after assessing the impact on running workloads and configured live-restore behavior.",
        "number": 10,
        "question": "Explain Docker daemon architecture and how to troubleshoot Docker Engine API failures."
      },
      {
        "answer": "A process can remain alive while the application is unable to serve requests, so container health checks should test a meaningful readiness condition rather than merely checking that a PID exists. Use a lightweight endpoint or command with suitable interval, timeout, start period, and retry values. Avoid checks that create excessive load or depend on optional external services in a way that causes unnecessary restarts. Understand that Docker health status does not automatically restart every unhealthy container by itself. Coordinate health checks with the orchestrator's restart and traffic-routing behavior.",
        "number": 11,
        "question": "How would you configure reliable container health checks and distinguish them from process status?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Start with image inspection and layer history to identify unnecessary packages, build tools, caches, and duplicated dependencies. Use multi-stage builds, minimal trusted base images, package-manager cleanup, and .dockerignore to exclude irrelevant files from the build context. Do not remove libraries required by the runtime or use an unmaintained image solely because it is small. Validate native dependencies, certificates, timezone data, and application behavior. Compare vulnerability findings and runtime tests before adopting the optimized image in production.",
        "number": 12,
        "question": "How do you reduce Docker image size while preserving runtime compatibility and security?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Docker reuses a build layer when the instruction and its dependent inputs remain unchanged. Place stable dependency-installation steps before frequently changing source-copy steps, and use BuildKit cache mounts where appropriate. Pin base image digests and dependency versions, control build arguments, and avoid embedding timestamps or environment-specific values unnecessarily. Keep secrets out of build arguments and image layers; use supported secret mounts. Record the source commit and resulting digest, then rebuild in a controlled environment to confirm the process produces the expected artifact.",
        "number": 13,
        "question": "Explain Docker image caching and how to create reproducible builds in CI/CD."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Inspect the container's resolv.conf, network attachment, configured DNS servers, aliases, and the Docker network's inspection output. Test name resolution from inside the affected container and compare it with connectivity by IP address. Check whether containers share a user-defined network and whether the expected service name or alias exists. Review host DNS configuration, firewall rules, and upstream resolver availability. Avoid hardcoding changing container IP addresses. Correct the network or DNS configuration and validate name resolution after container recreation and host restart.",
        "number": 14,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot Docker DNS resolution failures between containers?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Ensure the application receives termination signals and performs cleanup before its shutdown deadline. Use exec-form ENTRYPOINT or CMD so the application becomes the container's main process, or use a suitable init process when child-process reaping and signal forwarding are needed. Handle SIGTERM by stopping new work, draining requests, completing or safely cancelling jobs, and closing connections. Configure an appropriate stop timeout and test behavior under active traffic. Confirm that the application exits cleanly before forced termination and that orchestration replacement does not cause duplicate processing.",
        "number": 15,
        "question": "How would you implement graceful shutdown and signal handling in a Dockerized application?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Docker supports multiple logging drivers, and the selected driver determines how container stdout and stderr are collected and delivered. Inspect daemon and per-container logging configuration before changing it. Configure bounded log size and file count when using the json-file driver, or use a centralized logging driver and platform appropriate retention controls. Monitor host disk usage, logging delivery failures, and application log volume. Avoid writing unbounded logs to the container filesystem. Validate log availability during daemon outages and ensure critical incident evidence is retained securely.",
        "number": 16,
        "question": "Explain Docker logging drivers and how you would prevent container logs from exhausting host disk space."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Never bake passwords, private keys, or tokens into Dockerfile instructions, build arguments, image layers, or source repositories. Use BuildKit secret mounts for credentials needed only during image construction, and inject runtime secrets through an approved orchestrator or secrets manager. Restrict access by workload identity, rotate credentials, and prevent secret values from appearing in environment dumps or logs. Inspect image history and layers if a secret may have been exposed, rotate the affected credential, and treat published images as potentially compromised until verified.",
        "number": 17,
        "question": "How do you manage secrets safely during Docker image builds and container runtime execution?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Review release notes, supported upgrade paths, runtime compatibility, kernel requirements, and deprecated configuration before upgrading. Inventory workloads, storage drivers, networks, plugins, daemon flags, and orchestration dependencies. Test the target version on representative hosts and validate image pulls, container startup, networking, logging, and volume access. Drain workloads from one host at a time where orchestration supports it, preserve rollback packages and configuration, and monitor runtime and application metrics. Avoid simultaneous fleet-wide upgrades without a tested recovery plan.",
        "number": 18,
        "question": "How would you plan Docker Engine upgrades on production hosts with minimal service disruption?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Measure time spent pulling the image, creating the container, mounting storage, initializing networking, starting the process, and completing application readiness. Inspect image size, registry latency, layer cache hit rate, host disk I/O, CPU contention, DNS, and application initialization logs. Large images and dependency downloads can dominate cold starts. Optimize layers and artifact distribution, pre-pull approved images where appropriate, and reduce expensive startup work safely. Compare startup percentiles across hosts and verify that readiness checks still prevent traffic reaching an uninitialized application.",
        "number": 19,
        "question": "How do you investigate Docker container startup latency in a high-volume deployment platform?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Define approved base images, image provenance requirements, vulnerability thresholds, non-root execution, capability restrictions, resource limits, logging standards, and secrets-handling rules. Standardize Dockerfiles and CI checks, scan images and dependencies, and publish immutable artifacts to controlled registries. Harden hosts, restrict Docker socket access, patch the engine and kernel, and monitor runtime behavior. Provide reusable templates and documented exceptions with owners and expiry dates. Audit deployed image digests regularly and measure compliance without assuming that a successful image scan alone guarantees runtime security.",
        "number": 20,
        "question": "How would you establish a production Docker security and operational baseline across an engineering organization?"
      },
      {
        "question": "Q21. Explain Docker container runtime architecture and the roles of dockerd, containerd, and runc.",
        "answer": "Docker Engine exposes an API and command-line interface that communicate with the Docker daemon, dockerd. The daemon manages images, networks, volumes, and container lifecycle operations, and commonly delegates lower-level container management to containerd. containerd handles container lifecycle tasks such as image transfer and snapshot management, while runc is a low-level OCI runtime that creates and runs containers using Linux kernel isolation features. This separation helps isolate high-level Docker management from runtime execution. When a container fails to start, inspect daemon logs, containerd service health, runtime errors, image availability, and kernel restrictions. Understanding the layers prevents misdiagnosing every container failure as an application problem."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q22. How would you investigate Docker containers that are repeatedly restarting in a production environment?",
        "answer": "Start with docker ps -a and inspect the container's restart count, exit code, health status, and timestamps. Use docker logs with timestamps and docker inspect to review the configured command, environment, mounts, resource limits, and restart policy. Determine whether the process exits normally, crashes, is killed by an out-of-memory event, or fails a health check. Compare the container behavior with host memory, CPU, disk, and daemon events. Check whether the application handles signals correctly and whether required dependencies are ready before startup. Fix the underlying application, configuration, or resource problem before changing restart policies. Validate stability through an observation period and confirm that repeated restarts no longer occur."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q23. Explain Linux namespaces and cgroups and how Docker uses them to isolate containers.",
        "answer": "Linux namespaces provide isolation for views of system resources, including process IDs, network interfaces, mount points, hostnames, and inter-process communication. Control groups, or cgroups, account for and limit resource usage such as CPU and memory, with behavior depending on the host kernel and cgroup version. Docker combines these kernel features with filesystem layers, capabilities, security profiles, and networking to create container isolation. Containers still share the host kernel, so they are not equivalent to separate virtual machines. When diagnosing isolation or resource issues, inspect namespace configuration, cgroup limits, kernel support, and container security settings. Validate that limits match the workload and that privileged access is not granted unnecessarily."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q24. How would you optimize Docker image size and build performance for enterprise CI/CD pipelines?",
        "answer": "Use multi-stage builds so compilers, package managers, and temporary build dependencies remain in the builder stage rather than the final runtime image. Choose a suitable minimal base image while checking compatibility, security updates, and required diagnostic tools. Use a .dockerignore file to exclude source-control metadata, local build output, secrets, and unrelated files from the build context. Order Dockerfile instructions to maximize safe layer-cache reuse, and pin dependencies and base-image versions for reproducibility. Combine cleanup with package installation where appropriate, but avoid obscuring the build process with fragile commands. Measure image size, build duration, vulnerability findings, and runtime behavior before adopting an optimization."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q25. Explain Docker storage drivers and how overlay filesystems affect container performance and disk consumption.",
        "answer": "Docker storage drivers manage image layers and the writable layer used by running containers. On many Linux installations, overlay2 or its kernel overlayfs implementation provides a union filesystem view that combines read-only image layers with a writable container layer. Writes to existing files may trigger copy-up behavior, which can affect I/O performance and disk usage. Persistent application data should generally be stored in volumes or explicitly managed bind mounts rather than relying on a container's writable layer. Diagnose storage problems using docker system df, host filesystem capacity, inode usage, and container mount inspection. Avoid manually deleting Docker-managed storage directories because doing so can corrupt metadata and running workloads."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q26. How would you troubleshoot Docker containers that cannot communicate across custom bridge networks?",
        "answer": "Inspect the network definitions using docker network ls and docker network inspect, then verify that both containers are attached to the intended network. Confirm that the application listens on the correct interface and port inside the container rather than only on loopback. Test name resolution using Docker's embedded DNS where applicable, and test connectivity to the target service port from the source container. Review subnet overlap, host firewall rules, published-port configuration, and any custom network driver settings. Remember that containers on the same user-defined bridge network can generally communicate by container or network alias without publishing ports to the host. Change only the failing network or application setting and retest name resolution and traffic flow."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q27. Explain Docker container security hardening using non-root users, capabilities, seccomp, AppArmor, and read-only filesystems.",
        "answer": "Container hardening reduces the impact of a compromised process by limiting its privileges and access to host resources. Run applications as non-root users, drop Linux capabilities that the workload does not require, and use supported seccomp and AppArmor or SELinux policies to restrict system calls and access. A read-only root filesystem can prevent unexpected modification, while explicitly mounted writable paths provide only the storage the application needs. Avoid privileged containers and unnecessary host PID, network, or filesystem sharing. Scan images, patch base layers, protect secrets, and use trusted registries. Validate hardening in staging because applications may legitimately require temporary files, specific capabilities, or writable directories."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q28. How would you diagnose Docker containers being killed because of memory pressure or OOM conditions?",
        "answer": "Check docker inspect for the container's memory limit and OOMKilled state, and review docker events and host kernel logs for out-of-memory messages. Compare container memory usage with its configured limit and inspect application heap, native memory, caches, and request concurrency. A container may be killed even when the host still has some free memory if its own cgroup limit is exceeded. Distinguish a kernel OOM kill from an application exit or health-check restart. Correct memory leaks, reduce unnecessary allocation, tune application concurrency, or adjust the limit after checking host capacity. Validate using load tests and monitor memory trends, restart counts, and application latency."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q29. Explain Docker image supply-chain security and how to prevent vulnerable or untrusted images from reaching production.",
        "answer": "Image supply-chain security begins with trusted base images, controlled build definitions, pinned dependencies, and authenticated access to approved registries. Scan images for operating-system and application dependency vulnerabilities during CI, and define policies for severity, exploitability, and approved exceptions. Generate and retain software bills of materials where required, and sign images or verify provenance using an established supply-chain process. Deploy immutable image digests rather than relying only on mutable tags. Restrict who can publish production images and protect registry credentials. Rebuild images when base layers receive security updates, then test the rebuilt artifact before promotion. A successful vulnerability scan is not proof of complete security, so combine scanning with runtime controls and patch management."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q30. How would you design persistent data storage for Docker workloads that must survive container replacement?",
        "answer": "Container filesystems are ephemeral by design, so important state should be stored outside the container's writable layer. Docker named volumes are managed by Docker and are useful for persistent data on a single host, while bind mounts map a specific host path into a container and require careful host-path management. Select storage based on access patterns, durability, backup, portability, and whether multiple hosts must share the data. Database workloads need application-consistent backups and a tested restore process, not merely a copy of files while writes are active. Define ownership, permissions, capacity monitoring, and retention. Test container removal and recreation to prove that the required data remains available and recoverable."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q31. Explain how Docker health checks work and how they differ from process status and orchestration readiness checks.",
        "answer": "A running container only indicates that its main process has not exited; it does not prove that the application can serve requests correctly. A Docker HEALTHCHECK executes a command at configured intervals and records healthy, unhealthy, or starting status based on the result and configured thresholds. The check should test a meaningful local application capability without depending on unrelated external systems that could cause unnecessary failures. Docker health status alone does not automatically restart every unhealthy container in all deployment configurations. Orchestrators such as Kubernetes provide separate readiness and liveness probe semantics. Design checks around startup time, dependency behavior, and failure recovery, then verify how the deployment platform responds to unhealthy status."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q32. How would you troubleshoot Docker daemon failures that prevent containers from starting across a host?",
        "answer": "First determine whether the Docker CLI cannot reach the daemon, the daemon service is stopped, or individual container operations are failing. On Linux, inspect systemctl status docker and journalctl -u docker for startup errors, storage-driver failures, configuration problems, or permission issues. Verify filesystem capacity and inodes, daemon.json syntax, containerd health, socket permissions, and kernel compatibility. Check whether a recent daemon upgrade, configuration change, or disk event preceded the outage. Preserve logs and container metadata before attempting disruptive cleanup. Restarting the daemon may affect workloads depending on configuration and runtime behavior, so follow the host recovery procedure. Validate daemon health and a controlled test container before restoring full production scheduling."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q33. Explain Docker logging drivers and how you would prevent container logs from exhausting host disk space.",
        "answer": "Docker supports logging drivers that determine how container stdout and stderr are collected and delivered, such as local, json-file, journald, or remote logging integrations depending on the environment. The default logging configuration can consume substantial disk when applications produce unbounded output and no rotation policy is configured. Set suitable maximum file sizes and retention counts for supported local drivers, or forward logs to a centralized platform with defined buffering and retention. Monitor filesystem and inode usage, and avoid logging secrets or excessively verbose payloads. Changing a logging driver may require container recreation to affect existing containers. Validate log delivery, rotation, searchability, and behavior during a central logging outage."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q34. How would you troubleshoot a Docker container that works on a developer laptop but fails in a production Linux host?",
        "answer": "Compare the exact image digest, architecture, Docker and container runtime versions, kernel features, environment variables, mounted files, permissions, and resource limits between environments. Inspect container startup logs and docker inspect output to identify differences in entrypoint, command, user, network, and mount configuration. Check whether the production host has the required CPU architecture, filesystem behavior, kernel capabilities, DNS access, and outbound connectivity. Verify that secrets and configuration are injected through the approved deployment mechanism rather than copied from a developer machine. Reproduce the production image and configuration in a staging environment, then fix the underlying portability or dependency issue. Avoid solving the problem with broad privileged access."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q35. Design a production Docker operations strategy covering image lifecycle, deployment safety, monitoring, backup, and incident recovery.",
        "answer": "A production Docker strategy should define approved base images, reproducible builds, image scanning, artifact signing where required, and immutable promotion through development, staging, and production. Deploy containers with explicit resource limits, non-root identities, minimal capabilities, health checks, and controlled network access. Store persistent data in managed volumes or external services with documented backup and restore procedures. Centralize logs and monitor container restarts, resource usage, image versions, host disk capacity, daemon health, and application-level service indicators. Use staged rollouts and retain a known-good image digest for rollback. During incidents, preserve diagnostic evidence, isolate the failing layer, restore service safely, and document preventive actions after recovery."
      }
    ]
