      {
        "question": "Q22. How would you troubleshoot Kubernetes etcd latency, quorum loss, or database corruption in a production cluster?",
        "answer": "Start by determining whether the problem is API request latency, etcd member availability, disk I/O saturation, network communication, or loss of quorum. Review control-plane and etcd logs, member health, database size, disk latency, CPU, memory, and peer connectivity. In a multi-member cluster, quorum requires a majority of members, so avoid restarting or removing multiple members simultaneously. Confirm the exact cluster topology and follow the distribution-specific recovery procedure. Take a verified snapshot when the cluster is in a recoverable state and protect snapshot credentials and files. For suspected corruption or quorum loss, use the documented etcd recovery process with the correct member identity and snapshot; do not manually delete data directories. Validate API writes, reads, controller reconciliation, and cluster object consistency after recovery."
