        "question": "Q23. Explain Kubernetes scheduling decisions involving requests, limits, taints, tolerations, affinity, and topology spread constraints.",
        "answer": "The scheduler evaluates whether a node is eligible for a Pod and then scores feasible nodes according to scheduling policies and configured constraints. CPU and memory requests influence placement and resource accounting, while limits constrain runtime consumption through the container runtime and kernel mechanisms. Taints repel Pods unless they have matching tolerations, whereas node affinity and Pod affinity or anti-affinity express placement preferences or requirements. Topology spread constraints help distribute replicas across zones, nodes, or other topology domains. When a Pod remains Pending, inspect its events and describe output for insufficient resources, unmatched selectors, untolerated taints, volume constraints, or affinity conflicts. Correct the actual constraint or capacity issue and verify that the scheduler places replicas across the intended failure domains."
