        "question": "Q25. Explain Kubernetes network packet flow from one Pod to another Pod across different worker nodes.",
        "answer": "Kubernetes networking expects each Pod to have an IP address and generally allows Pod-to-Pod communication without application-level address translation between Pods. A Container Network Interface plugin configures interfaces, routes, and network connectivity on nodes. When two Pods are on different nodes, traffic follows the networking implementation's routing or overlay mechanism, potentially involving cloud routes, encapsulation, or eBPF-based forwarding. Network policies may restrict which workloads can communicate, depending on CNI support. Troubleshoot by checking Pod IPs, node routes, CNI health, network policy rules, security groups or firewall controls, and packet flow in both directions. Test DNS separately from direct IP connectivity so name-resolution failures are not confused with routing failures. Validate service discovery and cross-node traffic after remediation."
