        "question": "Q26. How would you design Kubernetes multi-cluster disaster recovery with application availability and persistent data recovery requirements?",
        "answer": "A multi-cluster disaster recovery design begins with business-approved recovery time and recovery point objectives. Prepare a secondary cluster with compatible Kubernetes versions, networking, ingress, identity integration, secrets, policies, and deployment automation. Replicate or back up application data using storage-native or application-consistent mechanisms; copying Kubernetes manifests alone does not recover persistent application state. Manage configuration and releases through GitOps or another repeatable deployment process. Define traffic failover through DNS, global load balancing, or another tested routing mechanism, and document how to prevent conflicting writers during recovery. Test restoration of databases, object storage, secrets, and dependencies, not just Pod startup. Run periodic failover exercises and measure actual recovery time and data loss."
