      {
        "question": "Q27. Explain Kubernetes admission control and how validating and mutating admission webhooks enforce platform policies.",
        "answer": "Admission control runs after authentication and authorization but before an API object is persisted. Mutating admission webhooks can modify incoming objects, for example by adding approved defaults or injecting sidecars, while validating webhooks inspect objects and accept or reject them according to policy. Built-in admission plugins and policy frameworks can enforce requirements such as allowed registries, resource settings, security contexts, and namespace rules. A webhook outage can affect API operations depending on its failure policy and scope, so webhook availability and latency are operationally important. Troubleshoot rejected deployments by inspecting API error messages, webhook service endpoints, certificates, logs, and policy rules. Test policy changes in a non-production environment and avoid broad fail-open behavior for critical security controls."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q28. How would you troubleshoot Kubernetes PersistentVolume and PersistentVolumeClaim binding failures in a stateful production workload?",
        "answer": "Inspect the PVC and PV descriptions and events to identify whether the claim is Pending because of storage class selection, access mode, capacity, topology, or provisioner failure. Check the StorageClass parameters, volume binding mode, CSI controller and node plugin health, cloud permissions, and provider quota. With delayed binding, a volume may not be provisioned until a Pod is scheduled, so review the Pod scheduling events as well. Confirm that requested access modes and capacity are supported by the storage backend. Do not delete a PVC or PV as a first troubleshooting action because reclaim policies and backend behavior can cause data loss. Resolve the provisioning or topology mismatch, then verify attachment, mount, filesystem access, and application data integrity."
