      {
        "question": "Q31. Explain Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, Vertical Pod Autoscaler, and Cluster Autoscaler interactions and common scaling failures.",
        "answer": "Horizontal Pod Autoscaler adjusts replica counts based on observed metrics such as CPU, memory, or custom application metrics. Vertical Pod Autoscaler can recommend or adjust container resource requests depending on its configuration and operating mode. Cluster Autoscaler or a provider-specific node autoscaler adds or removes node capacity in response to unschedulable Pods and underutilized nodes. These mechanisms operate at different layers and can interact poorly if resource requests are inaccurate or controllers continuously counteract each other. Troubleshoot missing metrics, HPA conditions, VPA recommendations, pending Pods, node-group limits, quotas, and scheduling constraints. Ensure metrics-server or the custom metrics pipeline is healthy. Test scale-up and scale-down behavior, stabilization windows, PodDisruptionBudgets, and application warm-up under realistic load."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q32. How would you investigate Kubernetes DNS failures involving CoreDNS, search domains, and service discovery?",
        "answer": "Start by checking the affected Pod's resolver configuration and testing both fully qualified service names and short names. Verify that the CoreDNS Pods are Ready, their Deployment has sufficient replicas, and the CoreDNS service endpoints point to healthy Pods. Inspect CoreDNS logs and configuration for forwarding, plugin, loop, or upstream resolver problems. Check network policies, node-to-Pod connectivity, kube-proxy or the cluster service implementation, and upstream DNS reachability. Distinguish a missing Kubernetes Service or incorrect namespace from a DNS forwarding failure. Use nslookup or dig from a diagnostic Pod and compare results across nodes. Correct the failing configuration or connectivity layer, then validate service records, external name resolution, and DNS latency under load."
