        "answer": "The Jenkins controller coordinates job scheduling, stores configuration and build metadata, manages plugins, and communicates with agents that execute build steps. Agents provide isolated execution capacity and can be static machines, ephemeral cloud instances, or Kubernetes Pods. A scalable design keeps heavy compilation, testing, and packaging work off the controller, assigns agents through labels and workload-specific templates, and uses separate capacity for trusted and untrusted jobs. Protect controller storage and configuration, monitor queue length and executor utilization, and avoid running builds on the controller unless there is a deliberate reason. For resilience, maintain tested backups, configuration-as-code, plugin compatibility records, and a documented controller recovery process. Validate agent connectivity and build reproducibility during infrastructure changes."
