      {
        "question": "Q28. How would you investigate Jenkins build queue congestion and improve scheduling fairness across multiple teams?",
        "answer": "Inspect queue duration, blocked and buildable items, executor availability, agent labels, offline agents, and resource usage on the controller and build infrastructure. A job can remain queued because no agent matches its label, all matching executors are occupied, a throttle limit is reached, or a lockable resource is unavailable. Review job concurrency settings and pipeline stages that hold scarce resources longer than necessary. Separate workload classes using dedicated labels or cloud agent templates and apply quotas or throttling where appropriate. Avoid adding executors without checking CPU, memory, network, and downstream capacity. Track queue-time percentiles by team and job type, then validate that scheduling changes improve fairness without overloading shared infrastructure."
