      {
        "question": "Q29. Explain Jenkins Pipeline failure handling using timeout, retry, catchError, post conditions, and stage-level controls.",
        "answer": "Jenkins Pipeline provides mechanisms to handle expected failures and ensure cleanup and reporting are performed consistently. timeout bounds how long a block can run, while retry repeats a block for failures that are safe to retry. catchError can record a failure while allowing selected Pipeline execution to continue, but it should not hide a deployment failure that requires the build to stop. post conditions support cleanup, notifications, and artifact handling after pipeline or stage completion. Use retries only for transient operations and avoid repeating non-idempotent deployments without safeguards. Preserve the original error context, set meaningful build results, and ensure cleanup runs even when earlier steps fail. Test failure paths as deliberately as successful paths."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q30. How would you integrate Jenkins with Kubernetes dynamic agents while controlling cost, security, and build isolation?",
        "answer": "The Jenkins Kubernetes integration can provision ephemeral agent Pods for individual builds or workload groups, allowing capacity to scale with demand. Define Pod templates with appropriate labels, containers, resource requests and limits, service accounts, workspace volumes, and retention behavior. Use narrowly scoped Kubernetes permissions and avoid giving build Pods cluster-admin access. Separate trusted deployment workloads from untrusted pull-request builds, and ensure secrets are injected only into authorized jobs. Configure Pod startup timeouts and cleanup policies, and monitor scheduling delays, image-pull failures, node capacity, and orphaned Pods. Test agent creation and deletion under load, including cancellation and controller interruption, to confirm that resources are reclaimed and builds cannot interfere with one another."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q31. Explain how to implement secure artifact promotion and provenance across Jenkins build, test, staging, and production stages.",
        "answer": "Build an artifact once from a controlled source revision and promote that same immutable artifact through test, staging, and production rather than rebuilding independently in every environment. Record the source commit, build identity, dependency versions, toolchain, test results, and artifact digest. Publish artifacts to an approved repository with access controls and retention policies. Verify checksums or signatures before deployment and ensure that production deployment jobs can retrieve only approved artifacts. Separate build permissions from release permissions, require appropriate approvals, and retain deployment evidence for audit. This approach reduces environment drift and makes rollback more reliable because the previous production artifact can be identified and redeployed by its immutable version or digest."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q32. How would you troubleshoot Jenkins Pipeline failures caused by Git checkout, shallow clones, submodules, or repository authentication?",
        "answer": "Inspect the exact SCM configuration, repository URL, branch or refspec, credentials ID, checkout extensions, and Git tool version used by the failing job. Review the checkout console output and agent-side Git logs to distinguish authentication failure, missing ref, network timeout, repository permission, and workspace corruption. Shallow clones can omit history required by versioning tools, changelog generation, or merge operations. Submodules may require separate credentials or correct URL rewriting. Test repository access from the same agent identity and network path, then validate the requested commit exists. Clean or recreate the workspace only after preserving useful evidence. Correct the specific checkout configuration and confirm that the job builds the intended revision."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q33. Explain Jenkins controller performance tuning and how to identify excessive heap usage, garbage collection, or thread saturation.",
        "answer": "Controller performance analysis should correlate heap occupancy, garbage-collection pauses, CPU, thread count, queue duration, HTTP response latency, plugin activity, and build metadata growth. Capture thread dumps during slow periods and use JVM tools such as jcmd or Java Flight Recorder to identify blocked threads, expensive plugin activity, and allocation hotspots. Avoid running heavyweight builds on the controller and review plugins that create excessive background tasks or retain large objects. Size heap based on measured workload while preserving memory for native JVM structures and the operating system. Review build retention and log storage policies because large metadata and artifacts can also affect performance. Apply changes in staging and compare controller responsiveness and queue metrics under representative load."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q34. How would you design Jenkins deployment approvals and separation of duties for production releases?",
        "answer": "Production release controls should distinguish the identity that builds an artifact from the identity that authorizes and performs its deployment. Use protected branches, reviewed Pipeline changes, scoped credentials, and an approval mechanism that records the approver and decision. Limit who can modify deployment logic and prevent untrusted pull-request code from reaching production credentials. Require evidence such as successful tests, artifact digest, change reference, and target environment before approval. Use environment-specific deployment roles with least privilege and record the result of each release. Ensure emergency releases follow a documented break-glass process with retrospective review. Test that unauthorized users and untrusted jobs cannot bypass approval or invoke production deployment steps."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q35. Design a production Jenkins platform operating model covering pipeline governance, agent lifecycle, observability, security, and recovery.",
        "answer": "A production Jenkins operating model should define controller ownership, supported core and plugin versions, Pipeline standards, shared-library governance, credential controls, and agent provisioning policies. Keep controllers focused on orchestration and use isolated, appropriately sized agents for build execution. Monitor queue time, executor availability, agent connection failures, controller heap, garbage collection, disk usage, build duration, and deployment outcomes. Store critical configuration as code, back up JENKINS_HOME consistently, and test restoration with compatible plugins and credentials. Secure job configuration and shared-library repositories, separate untrusted builds from privileged deployment workloads, and audit production approvals. Use immutable artifacts and repeatable release procedures. Exercise controller recovery, agent replacement, credential rotation, and pipeline rollback scenarios regularly."
      }
    ]
