      {
        "answer": "First stop further history changes and identify the last known-good commit using team references, pull-request records, CI logs, reflogs, or another developer's clone. Use git reflog to inspect recent local reference movements, remembering that reflogs are local and expire. Confirm the intended commit with git show and compare it with the remote branch state. Restore through a reviewed branch update or a carefully coordinated push, using --force-with-lease only when rewriting history is explicitly approved. Notify collaborators because their local branches may now diverge, and investigate the process that allowed the destructive update.",
        "number": 2,
        "question": "How would you recover a Git branch after an accidental force push or branch deletion?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Merge preserves the original branch topology and creates a merge commit when necessary. Rebase replays commits onto a new base, producing a more linear history but changing commit identifiers. Squash combines a set of changes into one commit, reducing detailed branch history in the target branch. Choose based on audit requirements, review conventions, release traceability, and whether published commits may be rewritten. Avoid rebasing shared branches without coordination. Protect important branches, require pull-request reviews, and ensure the final history still provides sufficient traceability to the original work and approvals.",
        "number": 3,
        "question": "Explain Git merge, rebase, and squash strategies and how to choose between them for enterprise development."
