      {
        "answer": "Identify the common ancestor and compare both branch histories before resolving individual files. Use git merge-base, git log, git diff, and git status to understand the competing changes. Resolve conflicts in small logical groups, inspect conflict markers, and understand the intended behavior from both contributors rather than mechanically choosing one side. Run targeted tests followed by broader monorepo validation. For generated files or lockfiles, regenerate them using the project's approved tooling. Review the final staged diff and commit only after confirming that no unrelated changes or conflict markers remain.",
        "number": 4,
        "question": "How do you investigate and resolve complex Git merge conflicts in a large monorepo?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Git garbage collection optimizes repository storage by packing loose objects and removing objects that are no longer reachable and have passed applicable retention protections. Objects reachable from branches, tags, and other references are normally retained. Dangling commits may remain temporarily after branch deletion or history rewriting. Use git fsck --full --no-reflogs to inspect unreachable objects and git show to identify useful commits. Create a recovery branch pointing to the verified commit before cleanup removes it. Do not run aggressive pruning during an incident until recovery requirements and repository backups have been checked.",
        "number": 5,
        "question": "Explain Git garbage collection, object reachability, and how dangling commits can be recovered."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Choose a branching model that reflects release frequency, production support requirements, and integration complexity. Trunk-based development with short-lived branches reduces long-running merge divergence, while release branches can support controlled stabilization and patch delivery. Enforce protected branches, code review, automated tests, signed or traceable release tags, and consistent versioning. Keep feature flags separate from deployment decisions where possible. Define ownership for release branches and emergency fixes, and regularly merge supported fixes back into active development lines to prevent long-term divergence.",
        "number": 6,
        "question": "How would you design Git branching and release management for multiple teams delivering independently?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Client-side hooks run in a developer's local Git environment and can provide fast feedback before commits or pushes, but they can be bypassed or may not be installed consistently. Server-side hooks and repository-hosting protections enforce rules centrally when changes reach the server. CI policy checks validate the actual submitted change using a controlled environment and can enforce tests, security scans, and review requirements. Use local hooks for convenience, but rely on centrally enforced controls for mandatory policy. Document hook installation and ensure server-side rules cannot be bypassed through alternate push paths.",
        "number": 7,
        "question": "How do Git hooks differ from server-side branch protection and CI policy enforcement?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Measure the operation that is slow, such as clone, fetch, status, checkout, log, or diff. Inspect repository size, object count, packfiles, large binary history, filesystem performance, and working-tree file count. Use git count-objects, git gc --aggressive only when justified, git verify-pack, and Git Trace2 diagnostics to identify expensive operations. Consider shallow or partial clones, sparse checkout, and Git LFS for suitable workloads. Coordinate repository maintenance and test changes against CI and developer workflows. Do not rewrite shared history simply to reduce repository size without a migration and communication plan.",
        "number": 8,
        "question": "How would you investigate Git performance degradation in a repository containing millions of files or large history?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "A Git submodule records a specific commit from another repository rather than automatically tracking its latest branch state. The parent repository stores a gitlink entry and configuration describing the submodule. CI must initialize and update submodules recursively with credentials and network access appropriate to each repository. A missing commit, changed repository URL, or inaccessible private repository can break builds. Pin submodule revisions deliberately, validate updates through review, and ensure release builds can retrieve every referenced commit. Avoid assuming that cloning the parent repository alone retrieves all nested source.",
        "number": 9,
        "question": "Explain Git submodules and the operational challenges of managing them in CI/CD."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Use centralized identity, least-privilege repository permissions, branch protection, mandatory review, and audit logging. Enable secret scanning and push protection where available, and scan historical content when a credential exposure is suspected. If a secret is committed, revoke or rotate it immediately because deleting the visible line does not invalidate copies already cloned or fetched. Use signed commits or verified identities when required by policy. Restrict automation tokens, protect CI configuration files, and review changes to deployment scripts and repository permissions with the same care as application code.",
        "number": 10,
        "question": "How do you secure Git repositories against leaked credentials and malicious commit changes?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Git LFS stores pointer files in the Git repository while the actual large content is held in an LFS object store. A checkout may contain pointer text instead of the expected binary if LFS filters are unavailable, smudge processing is disabled, or the object cannot be downloaded. Verify Git LFS installation, repository tracking rules, authentication, bandwidth or storage limits, and object availability. Use git lfs ls-files and git lfs pull to inspect and retrieve tracked content. Validate the downloaded file against the expected pointer metadata and ensure CI agents have the required LFS credentials.",
        "number": 11,
        "question": "Explain Git LFS architecture and how you would troubleshoot missing large files in a checkout."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Immediately revoke or rotate the exposed secret because history rewriting cannot guarantee that every clone or cached copy is erased. Coordinate a maintenance window and identify all branches, tags, mirrors, pull requests, and automation references containing the file. Use a supported history-rewriting tool such as git filter-repo, verify the rewritten object graph, and force-update affected references under controlled approval. Communicate exact recovery instructions to contributors so they do not reintroduce old history. Re-run secret scanning and review hosting-provider cleanup requirements for cached objects and pull-request references.",
        "number": 12,
        "question": "How would you safely rewrite Git history to remove a sensitive file from every commit?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Git bisect performs a binary search across commit history to locate the commit that introduced a regression. Mark a known-good commit and a known-bad commit, then test the selected midpoint and classify it accordingly. Git narrows the range until it identifies the likely first bad commit. Automate repeatable tests with git bisect run when the regression can be detected reliably. Ensure the test environment and dependencies are consistent across historical commits. If commits are not independently buildable, identify the first usable range and inspect adjacent changes manually.",
        "number": 13,
        "question": "Explain Git bisect and how it helps identify regressions in complex release histories."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Define organization-wide permission groups and standard repository templates rather than assigning access independently without governance. Separate human users, CI service accounts, release bots, and administrative identities. Prefer short-lived tokens or federated identities when supported, scope automation access to required repositories and operations, and rotate credentials on a defined schedule. Enforce branch protection and review requirements centrally where possible. Audit dormant users, stale deploy keys, personal access tokens, and webhook secrets. Maintain an exception process with an owner, justification, and expiry date.",
        "number": 14,
        "question": "How would you manage Git repository permissions and automation identities across hundreds of projects?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Git worktrees allow multiple working directories to share one repository's object database while checking out different branches or commits. This lets a developer inspect a release branch, reproduce a production issue, and continue feature work without repeatedly switching a single working directory. Use git worktree list, add, remove, and prune to manage worktrees. Be aware that a branch generally cannot be checked out in multiple worktrees simultaneously. Keep build outputs and environment-specific files isolated so parallel worktrees do not interfere with one another.",
        "number": 15,
        "question": "Explain Git worktrees and how they can improve parallel development and incident response."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Configure developer and automation signing identities using an approved signing method such as GPG or SSH signing where supported by the hosting platform. Verify signatures against trusted identities and ensure the repository's verification policy is documented. Enforce signed commits or verified authorship on protected branches when required. A valid signature proves control of a signing key, not that the change is safe or reviewed, so combine verification with code review, CI checks, and access controls. Protect private signing keys and revoke compromised keys promptly.",
        "number": 16,
        "question": "How do you validate commit signing and enforce trusted source changes in a Git-based delivery process?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Fetch the latest remote references and inspect the local and remote commit graphs using git log --graph --oneline --decorate --all. Determine whether the remote contains legitimate commits that are missing locally. Integrate them using the team's approved merge or rebase strategy, resolve conflicts, run tests, and push again. Do not immediately use --force because it may overwrite another developer's work. If history rewriting is explicitly approved, prefer --force-with-lease and confirm the expected remote reference before updating it.",
        "number": 17,
        "question": "How would you troubleshoot a Git push rejected because remote history has changed?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Store environment configuration in version-controlled repositories with explicit ownership, review rules, and promotion workflows. Separate reusable templates from environment-specific values and avoid storing unencrypted secrets in Git. Use automated validation for syntax, schema, policy, and dependency references before merging. Promote reviewed commits or immutable configuration artifacts through environments rather than editing production manually. Record the deployed commit and reconcile actual system state against the declared configuration. Provide an audited emergency-change process and ensure approved manual fixes are committed back to source control.",
        "number": 18,
        "question": "How do you design Git-based configuration management to prevent environment drift?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Annotated tags create Git objects containing a tag message, tagger metadata, and optionally a cryptographic signature, making them useful for identifying releases. Lightweight tags are simple references to commits and do not carry the same metadata. Git notes attach additional information to objects without changing the commit itself, although notes require deliberate sharing and synchronization. Use immutable release tags, consistent versioning, signed release metadata where required, and artifact digests to connect source history with built and deployed software. Do not rely on movable branch names as the only release identifier.",
        "number": 19,
