  {
    "tool": "Terraform",
    "category": "Advanced Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "answer": "Terraform builds a directed dependency graph from references between resources, data sources, variables, and outputs, along with explicit depends_on declarations. It uses this graph to determine which operations can run concurrently and which must wait for dependencies. Creation generally follows dependency order, while destruction reverses the required relationships. Inspect terraform graph and plan output when ordering is unexpected. Prefer natural references over excessive explicit dependencies because references communicate both data flow and ordering. Cycles must be removed by redesigning the configuration or separating dependent operations.",
        "number": 1,
        "question": "Explain Terraform's dependency graph and how it determines resource creation and destruction order."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Use a supported remote backend with encryption, access controls, versioning or recovery capability, and locking appropriate to the backend. Separate state by environment, application, or ownership boundary to reduce blast radius and contention. Restrict write access to approved automation identities and prevent developers from casually modifying production state. Define backup and recovery procedures, audit state access, and avoid storing secrets in state because sensitive markings do not encrypt the underlying values. Test state recovery and locking behavior before depending on it for critical deployments.",
        "number": 2,
        "question": "How would you design remote Terraform state management for a large multi-team organization?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "State locking prevents concurrent operations from modifying the same state simultaneously when the backend supports locking. A lock may remain after a process crash, interrupted CI job, or backend communication failure. Inspect the lock metadata, identify the process and pipeline that acquired it, and confirm that no Terraform operation is still active. Check backend health and logs before taking action. Use terraform force-unlock only after verifying that the lock is genuinely stale and that the lock ID matches the intended state. Record the incident and investigate why the process did not release its lock.",
        "number": 3,
        "question": "Explain Terraform state locking and how to troubleshoot a stuck state lock safely."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Terraform CLI workspaces allow multiple state instances to use the same configuration and backend, with each workspace maintaining a distinct state. They can be useful for similar short-lived or isolated instances of the same configuration. However, workspaces do not provide strong organizational access boundaries by themselves. Separate root configurations, backend keys, accounts, or projects may be more appropriate when environments have different owners, permissions, policies, or lifecycles. Choose state boundaries based on blast radius and governance rather than assuming that workspace names alone isolate production.",
        "number": 4,
        "question": "How do Terraform workspaces differ from separate state files and when should each be used?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Run a reviewed Terraform plan against the correct backend, workspace, credentials, and variable set to compare declared configuration with observed infrastructure and stored state. Distinguish manual infrastructure changes from provider refresh behavior, configuration changes, and resources that are no longer tracked. Determine whether the external change was intentional before applying anything. If the desired configuration should adopt the change, update code or import the resource as appropriate. If the change is unauthorized, plan a controlled correction. Use scheduled drift detection and alerts, but do not automatically apply every detected difference.",
        "number": 5,
        "question": "How do you detect and remediate Terraform configuration drift in production infrastructure?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Import associates an existing infrastructure object with a Terraform resource address so Terraform can manage it. First write a resource configuration that accurately describes the existing object, then use the supported import workflow for the Terraform version in use. Review the resulting state and run a plan to identify differences between configuration and reality. Do not assume import automatically generates complete configuration or guarantees that the next apply is harmless. Back up state, verify the exact resource identifier and address, and avoid importing the same object into multiple state files.",
        "number": 6,
        "question": "Explain Terraform import and how to bring manually created cloud resources under state management."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Create modules around cohesive infrastructure capabilities with explicit inputs, outputs, validation, and documented assumptions. Keep modules composable rather than building one module that controls every part of an environment. Pin provider requirements and use semantic versioning for published module releases. Include examples, automated validation, security checks, and upgrade guidance. Avoid hidden dependencies on local files or ambient credentials. Test module behavior across supported provider versions and ensure that consumers can review the resulting plan before applying changes.",
        "number": 7,
        "question": "How would you design reusable Terraform modules for a large cloud platform?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "A provider configuration defines how Terraform authenticates and communicates with an infrastructure platform. Aliased provider configurations allow one root module to manage resources across multiple accounts, regions, or endpoints. Child modules should declare provider requirements and receive the intended provider configurations from their callers when multiple contexts are needed. Verify account identity and region before planning or applying. Avoid relying on implicit default providers in complex deployments because a resource can otherwise be created in the wrong environment. Use separate credentials and permissions for sensitive production boundaries.",
        "number": 8,
        "question": "Explain Terraform provider configuration, aliases, and multi-region deployment design."
      },
      {
        "answer": "A resource address change can make Terraform interpret an existing resource as removed and a new one as required. Use moved blocks where supported by the Terraform version, or carefully use terraform state mv for controlled state-address migration. Review the plan and confirm that the operation is a state mapping change rather than infrastructure destruction and recreation. Back up state before migration and coordinate changes with all pipelines using that backend. Test the refactor against a state copy or non-production environment, then verify that the production plan contains no unintended replacement.",
        "number": 9,
        "question": "How do you handle Terraform state refactoring when renaming resources or moving them into modules?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Lifecycle settings influence how Terraform handles resource changes. create_before_destroy can create a replacement before removing the old object when the provider and infrastructure permit it. prevent_destroy blocks planned destruction through Terraform while the rule remains configured. ignore_changes suppresses selected attribute differences during planning, and replace_triggered_by can trigger replacement when referenced objects or attributes change. These options do not eliminate infrastructure constraints or downtime. Use them only when the operational behavior is understood, and inspect the plan to confirm replacement order, naming constraints, capacity requirements, and rollback options.",
        "number": 10,
        "question": "Explain Terraform lifecycle meta-arguments and their effect on production resource replacement."
      },
      {
