      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. What is Ansible check mode and what are its limitations?",
        "answer": "Check mode, invoked with ansible-playbook --check, attempts to predict changes without applying them. It is useful for reviewing configuration and deployment impact before execution, but not every module fully supports check mode. Tasks that depend on earlier tasks creating files, registering actual runtime results, or executing custom commands may produce incomplete predictions. Review diff mode where supported and inspect module documentation. Never treat a successful check-mode run as proof that a production apply will succeed; validate in a representative environment."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. How do you troubleshoot Ansible performance issues across a large inventory?",
        "answer": "Measure controller CPU, memory, network, task duration, and target response time before changing concurrency. Use callback timing or verbose output to identify slow tasks, excessive fact gathering, repeated package operations, and connection delays. Tune forks, SSH pipelining, persistent connections, and fact caching where appropriate. Reduce unnecessary gathered facts and avoid expensive shell commands when a dedicated module exists. Increase concurrency gradually because excessive forks can overload managed nodes, SSH services, or package repositories."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. How do you handle partial failures during an Ansible production rollout?",
        "answer": "Use serial batches and define an acceptable failure threshold with max_fail_percentage. Identify whether failures are unreachable hosts, task failures, application health failures, or dependency problems. Stop further rollout when the service safety threshold is exceeded. Preserve logs and failed-host details, then decide whether to retry, repair, or roll back the affected batch. Use block, rescue, and always sections for controlled recovery and cleanup, but do not hide the original failure. Verify the entire service fleet before resuming deployment."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q11. How do you manage different operating systems with a single Ansible codebase?",
        "answer": "Use facts such as ansible_os_family, ansible_distribution, and ansible_distribution_major_version to select platform-specific tasks and package names. Organize operating-system differences into focused task files and templates while keeping common application behavior shared. Use conditionals only where platform behavior genuinely differs. Test supported distributions in CI or disposable virtual machines, and pin compatible collections. Avoid assuming that package names, service names, filesystem paths, or Python versions are identical across Linux distributions."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q12. What is the difference between delegate_to, local_action, and run_once?",
        "answer": "delegate_to runs a task on a different host than the current inventory host, such as calling a load-balancer API from a control node or management host. local_action is shorthand for executing a task locally on the controller. run_once executes a task once for the current batch rather than once per host, which matters when serial is configured. Understand variable context and delegation behavior before using them together. Validate that shared operations, such as database migrations, cannot execute multiple times unexpectedly."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q13. How do you build reliable Ansible handlers for service configuration changes?",
        "answer": "Handlers are notified by tasks and normally execute once at the end of the relevant play section, even when multiple tasks notify the same handler. Use them for actions such as restarting or reloading a service after a configuration template changes. Validate configuration before notifying a restart where possible, and use listen topics when several handlers share an event. Be careful with meta: flush_handlers because it changes execution timing. Confirm the service is active and healthy after the handler runs."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q14. How do you integrate Ansible with dynamic cloud inventory?",
