      },
      {
        "question": "Q20. How would you establish a production Linux server hardening and operations baseline?",
        "answer": "Define a standard image with supported packages, security updates, minimal installed services, and controlled administrative access. Enforce SSH key or approved identity authentication, disable unnecessary remote access methods, configure host firewall rules, and apply SELinux or AppArmor policy. Centralize logs, time synchronization, monitoring, vulnerability scanning, and audit events. Set resource limits and reliable log rotation, and maintain tested backups. Track configuration drift and validate the baseline continuously through automation and periodic security reviews."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q21. Explain Linux systemd dependency ordering and how to troubleshoot services that fail during boot because of unit relationships.",
        "answer": "Systemd manages services and other units through dependency and ordering relationships. Requires and Wants describe dependency relationships, while After and Before describe ordering; ordering alone does not necessarily pull another unit into the transaction. A service may fail because a required mount, network target, device, or prerequisite unit is unavailable when it starts. Inspect systemctl status, systemctl cat, systemctl list-dependencies, and journalctl -b for the affected unit. Review unit drop-ins and recent changes to ExecStart, Environment, User, WorkingDirectory, and dependency declarations. Avoid adding broad dependencies without understanding boot sequencing. Correct the missing prerequisite or unit relationship, run daemon-reload, and validate both normal startup and reboot behavior."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q22. How would you investigate Linux memory pressure, reclaim activity, swap usage, and an OOM killer event on a production server?",
        "answer": "Start with free -h, vmstat, /proc/meminfo, process-level memory information, and historical monitoring to distinguish low available memory from normal page-cache usage. Inspect kernel logs with journalctl -k or dmesg for OOM killer events and identify the process selected for termination. Review anonymous memory, page cache, swap-in and swap-out rates, memory pressure, and container or cgroup limits. High swap usage alone does not prove an active memory incident; sustained swapping and application latency are more meaningful. Identify memory growth, leaks, workload spikes, or oversized caches before changing kernel tuning. Apply capacity or application fixes, then monitor memory pressure, OOM events, and service latency under representative load."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q23. Explain Linux cgroups and namespaces and how containers use them to isolate processes and resources.",
        "answer": "Linux namespaces isolate views of system resources such as process IDs, mount points, network interfaces, hostnames, IPC, and users. Control groups organize processes and account for or limit resources such as CPU, memory, and I/O. Container runtimes combine these kernel features with filesystem isolation, capabilities, seccomp, and other security mechanisms to provide container isolation. A container is still made of host-kernel processes, so it shares the host kernel and must not be treated as a separate virtual machine. Troubleshoot resource constraints by inspecting the relevant cgroup hierarchy and namespace configuration, along with runtime and kernel logs. Understand whether the host uses cgroup v1 or v2 because file paths and controls differ. Validate limits under load and confirm that one workload cannot exhaust shared host resources."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q24. How would you troubleshoot Linux disk I/O saturation when CPU utilization is low but application response times are high?",
        "answer": "Low CPU utilization with high application latency can indicate processes waiting for storage rather than performing computation. Use iostat -xz, vmstat, pidstat -d, and tools such as iotop where available to inspect device utilization, queue depth, latency, throughput, and per-process I/O. Check filesystem health, mount options, device errors, RAID or cloud-volume metrics, and whether backups or log rotation are creating bursts. Distinguish a saturated device from an application issuing inefficient synchronous writes or excessive small operations. Review kernel logs for I/O errors and filesystem warnings. Do not run destructive filesystem repair commands on a mounted production filesystem. Resolve the workload or storage bottleneck and verify latency, queue depth, and application performance afterward."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q25. Explain Linux process states, uninterruptible sleep, zombie processes, and how to investigate a process that cannot be terminated.",
        "answer": "Linux process states provide clues about what a process is doing. Running or runnable processes are executing or waiting for CPU, sleeping processes wait for events, and processes in uninterruptible sleep commonly wait for kernel operations such as storage I/O. A zombie is a process that has exited but whose parent has not collected its exit status. Inspect ps output, process IDs, parent relationships, wait channels, open files, and kernel logs to identify the condition. SIGKILL cannot immediately remove a task stuck in uninterruptible kernel sleep because the task must return from the kernel wait. Investigate the underlying storage, device, or kernel operation rather than repeatedly sending signals. Resolve the underlying fault and confirm process and service recovery."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q26. How would you troubleshoot Linux network performance problems involving packet drops, retransmissions, MTU mismatch, and connection timeouts?",
        "answer": "Collect evidence from both endpoints and the network path. Use ip -s link, ethtool statistics, ss -s, ss -ti, ping with appropriate packet sizes, tracepath, and tcpdump to inspect interface errors, drops, retransmissions, and path behavior. Check MTU consistency across interfaces, tunnels, virtual networks, and firewalls because fragmentation or blocked path-MTU discovery can create intermittent failures. Review routing tables, policy routing, firewall rules, conntrack capacity, DNS, and NIC driver logs. Distinguish packet loss from application timeouts and server-side resource exhaustion. Capture traffic carefully and avoid exposing sensitive payloads. Apply the smallest targeted network change, then test throughput, latency, retransmissions, and application connectivity in both directions."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q27. Explain Linux file descriptor exhaustion and how it can cause application failures even when CPU and memory appear healthy.",
        "answer": "A file descriptor is a process-level reference to an open file, socket, pipe, or other resource. Applications can fail with errors such as Too many open files when they exceed their per-process or system-wide limits. Inspect ulimit -n, /proc/PID/limits, /proc/sys/fs/file-nr, and the number and types of descriptors held under /proc/PID/fd. Use lsof or application-specific diagnostics to identify leaked sockets, files, or pipes. Review service manager LimitNOFILE settings because systemd-managed services may not inherit an interactive shell's limits. Increasing the limit can provide temporary headroom but does not fix a descriptor leak. Correct application lifecycle handling, tune appropriate limits, and monitor descriptor usage over time."
