      {
        "question": "Q29. Explain Linux PAM authentication flow and how to troubleshoot login failures caused by PAM configuration changes.",
        "answer": "Pluggable Authentication Modules provide a configurable authentication and account-management framework used by services such as SSH, login, and sudo. PAM configuration defines module stacks and control flags for authentication, account checks, password changes, and session setup. A malformed rule or incorrect module order can lock out users or prevent sessions from being created. Inspect the service-specific PAM configuration, included common configuration files, authentication logs, and the exact failure stage. Check account expiry, access controls, directory service connectivity, and module dependencies. Preserve an existing privileged session while testing changes and maintain a tested recovery path through console or out-of-band access. Validate both successful and intentionally rejected logins before closing the recovery session."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q30. How would you diagnose Linux boot failures involving initramfs, GRUB, filesystem UUID changes, or missing root devices?",
        "answer": "Boot failures can occur before the root filesystem is mounted, so inspect the exact boot stage and error message first. GRUB configuration identifies the kernel and initramfs, while initramfs contains early-boot drivers and scripts needed to locate and mount the root filesystem. A changed disk UUID, missing storage driver, incorrect root parameter, or damaged filesystem can prevent boot. Use rescue media or the distribution's recovery environment to inspect disks, blkid output, mount configuration, GRUB entries, and initramfs contents. Back up configuration before modifying bootloader files or regenerating the initramfs. Follow distribution-specific recovery commands and verify the correct boot device. Reboot into the repaired system and confirm that normal and fallback boot entries behave as expected."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q31. Explain Linux capabilities, seccomp, SELinux, and AppArmor and how they contribute to layered host security.",
        "answer": "Linux capabilities divide traditional root privileges into smaller permission units that can be granted selectively to processes. Seccomp filters restrict the system calls a process may make, while SELinux and AppArmor provide mandatory access-control policies governing which resources a process can access. These controls complement traditional user and group permissions rather than replacing them. When an application receives Permission denied, inspect ownership and mode bits as well as audit logs, SELinux labels and denials, AppArmor profiles, capabilities, and seccomp restrictions. Do not disable an entire security framework as a routine fix. Identify the specific denied operation and create the narrowest justified policy adjustment. Validate that the application works while unrelated access remains blocked."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q32. How would you investigate Linux time synchronization drift and its impact on distributed applications and TLS authentication?",
        "answer": "Accurate system time is important for log correlation, distributed transactions, Kerberos authentication, certificates, and scheduled jobs. Check timedatectl status, chronyc tracking and sources, or the active time synchronization service to determine synchronization state and offset. Review upstream time sources, network access, virtual machine clock behavior, and whether multiple time services are competing. Large clock corrections can disrupt applications that assume time moves smoothly, while drift can make certificate validity and authentication checks fail. Avoid manually changing production clocks without assessing application and cluster impact. Correct the time source or service configuration, verify synchronization across related systems, and monitor offset and synchronization status over time."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q33. Explain Linux filesystem journaling, inode exhaustion, and how to troubleshoot a filesystem that reports available disk space incorrectly.",
        "answer": "Filesystem capacity includes both data blocks and metadata such as inodes. A filesystem can have free data space but no free inodes, preventing new files from being created. Use df -h to inspect block capacity and df -i to inspect inode usage. Compare du output with filesystem usage and look for deleted files that remain open through running processes, using lsof where available. Journaling helps maintain filesystem consistency after crashes but does not replace backups or guarantee application-level data integrity. Inspect mount options, filesystem errors, quotas, reserved blocks, and container volume boundaries. Do not remove unknown files from system directories to reclaim space. Identify the responsible workload, clean up safely, and verify both inode and block availability."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q34. How would you perform a Linux kernel upgrade safely on a fleet of production servers?",
        "answer": "A production kernel upgrade requires compatibility checks for hardware, drivers, security agents, storage modules, boot configuration, and application dependencies. Review distribution support policies, kernel release notes, package signatures, and required reboot behavior. Test the candidate kernel on representative systems and confirm that monitoring, networking, storage, and critical services operate correctly. Maintain a known-good kernel entry and verify that bootloader configuration supports recovery. Roll out in controlled batches with capacity available to absorb node unavailability. Monitor boot success, kernel logs, service health, and performance after each batch. Define rollback criteria and ensure console or out-of-band access is available if a server cannot boot normally."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q35. Design a production Linux server operations model covering observability, patching, security, capacity, and disaster recovery.",
