      {
        "question": "Q22. How do you manage Argo CD repository credentials securely across multiple teams and Git providers?",
        "answer": "Use Argo CD repository credential templates or supported credential integrations rather than embedding tokens in Application manifests. Store credentials in Kubernetes Secrets with the required Argo CD labels, restrict Secret access through RBAC, and use narrowly scoped deploy keys or access tokens with read-only repository permissions wherever possible. For larger installations, integrate an external secrets manager or workload identity mechanism so credentials can be rotated without manual edits to every Application. Separate credentials by trust boundary, such as production and non-production repositories, and avoid sharing a powerful organization-wide token across unrelated teams. Confirm that repository-server pods can access the credential source and Git endpoint, inspect repository connection status, and review logs for authentication failures without printing secret values. Establish rotation ownership, expiry alerts, revocation procedures, and an audit trail for credential changes."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q23. What is the purpose of Argo CD ApplicationSet generators, and how would you prevent accidental deployment to unintended clusters?",
        "answer": "ApplicationSet generators create Applications dynamically from sources such as Git directories, cluster registrations, lists, or cluster metadata. They are useful when many clusters or environments follow a consistent deployment model, but an overly broad selector can create Applications in clusters that were never intended to receive a workload. Use explicit cluster labels, constrained generator selectors, environment allowlists, and destination namespace restrictions. Review the generated Application set before enabling automated synchronization, and keep production clusters distinguishable through controlled metadata rather than informal naming conventions. Test changes to generator templates in a non-production Argo CD instance and inspect the resulting Application destinations. Apply RBAC and project destination restrictions as additional safeguards. Alert on unexpected Application creation, cluster targeting changes, and generator output growth so a metadata mistake does not silently expand deployment scope."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q24. How would you implement progressive delivery with Argo CD while keeping rollout decisions safe?",
        "answer": "Argo CD provides GitOps reconciliation, while progressive delivery is commonly implemented through a rollout controller such as Argo Rollouts or a service-mesh and traffic-management integration. Define a rollout strategy with controlled canary steps, traffic weights, pause intervals, analysis templates, and explicit success or failure thresholds. Analysis should use production-relevant signals such as request error rate, latency, saturation, and business-level indicators, with a reliable baseline and sufficient observation windows. Keep the desired rollout configuration in Git and ensure the rollout controller owns the progressive rollout mechanics rather than creating conflicting manual scaling changes. Validate metric queries and failure thresholds before production use, define what happens when analysis is unavailable, and provide a tested abort or rollback procedure. Monitor both Argo CD Application health and rollout-specific status because a synced Application does not necessarily mean the new release has completed safely."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q25. How do you troubleshoot an Argo CD Application that remains OutOfSync even though the deployed workload appears correct?",
        "answer": "Start by examining the Application diff and identify the exact resource fields that differ between Git and the live cluster. Common causes include API-server defaulting, mutating admission webhooks, controllers updating status or metadata, different rendered chart values, and fields managed by another controller. Check whether the diff concerns desired configuration or server-generated fields, and compare the rendered manifests with the live object using the same target revision and destination cluster. Use narrowly scoped ignoreDifferences rules only when the field is legitimately managed outside GitOps; do not suppress broad differences to make the status green. If server-side diff or server-side apply is enabled, verify compatibility with the Kubernetes API and field ownership. Reconcile after correcting the source of drift, then confirm that the diff is resolved without hiding meaningful configuration changes. Record the reason for each ignore rule and periodically review whether it remains necessary."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q26. How would you isolate performance bottlenecks in a large Argo CD installation managing thousands of resources?",
        "answer": "Break the investigation into API-server access, repo-server manifest generation, application-controller reconciliation, Redis usage, and network dependencies. Inspect component CPU, memory, restart counts, work queues, reconciliation duration, repository fetch latency, and Kubernetes API request errors. Determine whether the bottleneck affects all Applications or only Applications sharing a repository, cluster, or unusually large manifest set. Review repo-server logs for slow rendering, plugin execution, Git operations, or Helm dependency downloads; inspect controller logs and metrics for queue pressure and API throttling. Consider supported controller sharding, repo-server scaling, cache tuning, and reconciliation concurrency changes, but change one dimension at a time and measure the result. Reduce unnecessary generated resources and avoid excessive refresh intervals. Validate capacity under realistic load, maintain resource requests and limits, and monitor reconciliation latency against an operational SLO."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q27. What controls would you use to enforce production deployment approvals in an Argo CD GitOps workflow?",
        "answer": "Treat the Git repository and its change-management process as the approval boundary. Protect production branches with mandatory reviews, CODEOWNERS, status checks, signed commits where required, and restricted merge permissions. Separate production promotion from ordinary development changes through environment-specific overlays or release references, and ensure only approved identities can modify those references. Argo CD Projects should restrict which repositories, destinations, and resource kinds an Application may use. For sensitive environments, disable unattended automated synchronization or require an explicit promotion action after the approved change reaches the production source. Audit Git history, Argo CD events, RBAC changes, and sync operations together. Test emergency access and rollback procedures so approval controls do not prevent incident recovery. Avoid relying only on a UI button as an approval mechanism because repository write permissions and API access can bypass informal process expectations."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q28. How do Argo CD sync waves and resource hooks help coordinate complex application deployments?",
        "answer": "Sync waves order resources during a synchronization so prerequisites can be created before dependent resources. Assign wave annotations to resources that require sequencing, such as namespaces and configuration before workloads, or database migration jobs before application deployments. Resource hooks can execute lifecycle actions such as pre-sync validation, sync-time migration, and post-sync verification. Design hooks to be idempotent, bounded by timeouts, observable, and safe to retry because failed or repeated sync operations can execute them again depending on hook policy. Ensure hook cleanup policies do not remove evidence needed for incident investigation. Do not treat wave ordering as a substitute for application readiness or dependency health; configure appropriate health checks and verify that prerequisite services are genuinely usable. Test failure behavior at each wave in a staging environment and document how operators recover from a partially completed synchronization."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q29. How would you secure Argo CD Projects when multiple application teams share one Argo CD control plane?",
