      {
        "question": "Q33. How would you manage Argo CD configuration drift when multiple administrators can modify the control plane?",
        "answer": "Move supported Argo CD configuration, RBAC policies, project definitions, and installation settings into a reviewed, version-controlled deployment process. Restrict direct edits to ConfigMaps, Secrets, and installation resources through Kubernetes RBAC, and use a dedicated platform ownership model for control-plane changes. Periodically compare live configuration with the approved source and alert on unexpected changes. Record changes to repository credentials, cluster registrations, trusted certificates, SSO configuration, and policy settings because these can affect every managed Application. Use admission controls or policy checks to prevent unauthorized modifications where appropriate. During an incident, capture the current configuration before changing it, identify who or what modified it, and restore the approved state through the controlled deployment path. Validate authentication, repository access, sync behavior, and project restrictions after configuration reconciliation."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q34. What is the role of Argo CD resource tracking, and how can tracking problems affect production operations?",
        "answer": "Resource tracking allows Argo CD to associate live Kubernetes resources with the Applications that manage them. Depending on the configured tracking method, association may use labels, annotations, or another supported mechanism. Tracking conflicts can occur when multiple Applications attempt to manage the same resource, when another controller changes tracking metadata, or when resource names and namespaces are reused. Such conflicts can lead to unexpected ownership, pruning decisions, or resources appearing in the wrong Application inventory. Investigate the Application resource tree, tracking metadata on the live object, and the rendered desired manifest. Establish clear ownership boundaries so one resource has one authoritative GitOps owner. Avoid manually copying Argo CD tracking metadata between objects. Before changing tracking configuration, assess the impact on existing resources and test migration behavior because ownership changes can affect pruning and reconciliation."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q35. How would you build an operational SLO and alerting strategy for Argo CD in a critical production platform?",
        "answer": "Define service indicators around the outcomes that matter to application teams: repository refresh success, reconciliation latency, sync failure rate, Application health transition time, and control-plane availability. Set SLO targets based on deployment criticality and realistic operational expectations rather than simply alerting on every transient error. Monitor the API server, application controller, repo-server, Redis, Git connectivity, and cluster connectivity as separate dependencies. Alert on sustained queue growth, repeated repository failures, unusual sync failure spikes, controller restarts, and Applications remaining degraded beyond an agreed threshold. Include labels for team, environment, project, and cluster so responders can identify ownership quickly. Link alerts to runbooks that explain evidence collection, safe remediation, and escalation. Review alert noise and SLO performance regularly, and test alert delivery during planned exercises."
      }
