      {
        "question": "Q1. How does Prometheus achieve high availability, and what limitations should engineers understand when running multiple Prometheus replicas?",
        "answer": "Prometheus high availability is commonly implemented by running two or more independent Prometheus servers with identical scrape configurations. Each replica collects the same targets and evaluates its own rules, so one server can continue serving queries and alerts when another fails. However, the replicas do not automatically share their local TSDB data or coordinate alert evaluations. External labels should identify the cluster and replica, and a compatible query layer such as Thanos or another deduplicating backend can merge replicated series. Engineers must also monitor scrape divergence, replica health, rule consistency, and downstream deduplication behavior."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q2. What is Prometheus metric cardinality, why can it become a production problem, and how do you control it?",
        "answer": "Cardinality is the number of distinct time series created by metric names and their label combinations. A metric containing labels such as user ID, request ID, session ID, or unrestricted URL paths can create millions of unique series. This increases memory consumption, ingestion cost, index size, query latency, and the risk of an out-of-memory failure. Engineers should inspect high-cardinality metrics, remove unbounded labels, normalize route names, and aggregate data before ingestion where appropriate. Track series growth, review instrumentation changes, and enforce metric-label standards during application development."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q3. Explain Prometheus federation and when you would use it instead of remote write or a long-term storage architecture.",
        "answer": "Federation allows one Prometheus server to scrape selected time series from another Prometheus server through its federation endpoint. It is useful when a global monitoring server needs aggregated metrics from multiple clusters or regions without collecting every raw application metric. The federated server should select only required metrics using appropriate match rules. Federation is not a replacement for remote write or durable long-term storage because it transfers selected samples through scraping and does not automatically provide complete historical retention. Use it for hierarchical monitoring and carefully design metric selection, scrape intervals, and network capacity."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q4. What happens in the Prometheus remote write pipeline, and how would you troubleshoot remote write failures?",
        "answer": "Prometheus remote write sends samples from its local storage pipeline to an external receiver using queued shards and HTTP requests. The WAL provides the source data for samples that must be transmitted, while queue configuration controls concurrent delivery and buffering. Failures can result from DNS problems, TLS errors, authentication failures, receiver throttling, network interruptions, or an overloaded destination. Inspect Prometheus logs, remote-write pending samples, failed requests, dropped samples, and receiver-side ingestion metrics. Correct the underlying problem before increasing queues, because excessive buffering can consume memory and delay recovery."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q5. How do Prometheus recording rules improve performance, and what risks arise from poorly designed recording rules?",
        "answer": "Recording rules periodically evaluate PromQL expressions and store their results as new time series. They are useful for expensive aggregations that are repeatedly requested by dashboards or alert rules, such as service-level request rates and error ratios. A good rule reduces repeated query work and provides predictable dashboard response times. Poorly designed rules can multiply cardinality, create duplicate or misleading metrics, and increase evaluation load. Validate expressions against representative data, use clear naming conventions, document label semantics, monitor rule evaluation duration, and avoid recording every possible aggregation combination."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q6. Explain the lifecycle of a Prometheus alert rule, including pending, firing, and resolved states.",
        "answer": "A Prometheus alert rule evaluates a PromQL expression at configured evaluation intervals. When the expression first returns a matching result, the alert enters the pending state if a for duration is configured. If the condition remains true for that duration, it becomes firing and can be sent to Alertmanager. When the expression stops returning the alerting series, the alert eventually becomes inactive or resolved according to the rule and notification flow. Engineers should choose the for duration based on expected transient behavior, use labels and annotations consistently, and test missing-data cases so short spikes do not create unnecessary pages."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q7. Describe the Prometheus relabeling pipeline and explain the difference between target relabeling and metric relabeling.",
        "answer": "Target relabeling is applied to discovered target labels before Prometheus decides which targets to scrape. It can rewrite addresses, add environment labels, keep selected targets, or drop unwanted targets. Metric relabeling is applied to individual samples after scraping and before ingestion, so it can remove or transform particular metrics and labels. Target relabeling is generally more efficient when a complete target should be excluded, while metric relabeling is useful for filtering selected samples. Incorrect regular expressions or action ordering can silently remove monitoring data, so validate relabel rules with representative discovery output and ingestion metrics."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. How would you investigate Kubernetes service discovery failures in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "Begin by checking the Prometheus target page and service-discovery page to determine whether Kubernetes objects are discovered and whether targets are dropped by relabeling. Verify the Kubernetes API endpoint, service account token, namespace permissions, Role or ClusterRole rules, and corresponding bindings. Confirm that the intended Services, Pods, EndpointSlices, and annotations or monitor resources match the discovery configuration. Review Prometheus logs for API authorization, timeout, and decoding errors. Finally, validate that network policies and pod ports allow scraping and that the discovered endpoint exposes the expected metrics path."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. A Prometheus target is DOWN even though the application is healthy. How would you isolate the cause?",
