      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. Explain how Prometheus TSDB blocks, compaction, and retention work in a production environment.",
        "answer": "Prometheus stores samples in a time-series database using a write-ahead log and immutable block files. Recent samples are initially handled through the head block and WAL, while background compaction creates larger blocks for efficient querying and storage. Retention policies remove data based on configured time or size limits, so disk capacity must be planned for active data, compaction overhead, and temporary files. Monitor disk utilization, block health, compaction duration, and WAL growth. Do not manually delete active TSDB files while Prometheus is running, because doing so can corrupt the database or remove required recovery information."
