      {
        "question": "Q32. What is the purpose of Prometheus exemplars, and how can they improve distributed tracing investigations?",
        "answer": "Exemplars attach trace-related identifiers to selected metric observations so an operator can move from an aggregated metric spike to an individual trace that provides request-level context. For example, a latency histogram can expose exemplar trace IDs that link to a tracing backend such as Tempo or Jaeger. Configure instrumentation and the Prometheus-compatible ingestion path to preserve exemplars, and configure Grafana data source integration so users can follow the trace link. Ensure trace IDs are valid and that the tracing backend retains the corresponding trace data long enough for investigation. Exemplars should not be used as high-cardinality metric labels because that would create a separate series for each trace. Test the complete metric-to-trace workflow with a real instrumented request and verify access controls across both systems."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q33. How would you design a safe migration from a single Prometheus instance to a horizontally scalable monitoring architecture?",
        "answer": "Start by documenting the current scrape configuration, rules, labels, dashboards, retention, and alerting dependencies. Decide whether the scaling requirement is caused by ingestion volume, query concurrency, retention, or failure-domain isolation because each problem may require a different architecture. Introduce the new architecture alongside the existing instance and migrate a controlled subset of targets or metrics first. Preserve stable labels and query semantics where possible, and validate recording rules, alert evaluations, dashboard results, and notification behavior. Avoid scraping the same targets into two alerting paths without a deduplication plan. Compare sample ingestion, query latency, data completeness, and resource consumption during the transition. Keep a rollback path until the new system has operated successfully under representative production load and recovery tests."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q34. How do you use promtool to validate Prometheus configuration and alerting rules before production deployment?",
        "answer": "Use promtool check config to validate Prometheus configuration syntax and referenced rule files, and use promtool check rules to detect invalid rule definitions and PromQL expressions. For important alerting logic, create rule test files with sample time series, evaluation timestamps, expected alert states, and expected output labels. Run these checks in CI whenever scrape configuration, recording rules, or alerting rules change. Include representative edge cases such as missing series, zero traffic, delayed samples, and threshold boundaries. Validation should also cover configuration generated by Helm or other templating systems, because the rendered output may differ from the source values. Promote only the validated artifact and retain the test results with the release. After deployment, confirm that Prometheus loaded the expected configuration and that rule groups are evaluating successfully."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q35. How would you build a production Prometheus operational strategy covering reliability, capacity, and incident response?",
        "answer": "Define operational objectives for scrape availability, rule evaluation reliability, query latency, data freshness, and monitoring-system availability. Monitor Prometheus itself using independent telemetry where practical so a complete monitoring failure does not hide its own symptoms. Establish capacity thresholds for active series, ingestion rate, memory, disk, query load, and rule evaluation duration. Use version-controlled configuration, automated validation, controlled releases, and tested backups or reconstruction procedures. Create runbooks for target failures, cardinality spikes, storage exhaustion, rule failures, and Alertmanager connectivity issues. Ensure alert notifications have clear ownership, severity, evidence, and escalation paths. Exercise node loss, disk pressure, network isolation, and central monitoring outages. Review incidents and capacity trends regularly, and make sure the monitoring platform remains useful during the failures it is intended to detect."
      }
    ]
