      {
        "question": "Q4. How would you troubleshoot a Grafana dashboard that loads slowly or times out?",
        "answer": "Inspect browser developer tools and Grafana server logs to distinguish slow dashboard rendering from slow data-source queries or network failures. Review dashboard refresh intervals, panel count, time range, repeated queries, transformations, and template-variable dependencies. Test the underlying query directly in Explore and inspect data-source response time and timeout limits. Reduce unnecessary panels, use recording rules or pre-aggregated data for expensive metrics, and avoid loading excessive historical ranges by default. Also monitor Grafana CPU, memory, database latency, and concurrent users because the application itself can become the bottleneck."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q5. How do Grafana dashboard variables work, and what risks arise from poorly designed variable queries?",
        "answer": "Dashboard variables dynamically provide values that can be reused in panel queries, titles, links, and other dashboard settings. Variables can depend on data-source queries or on other variables, enabling environment, cluster, namespace, and service selection. Poorly designed variables can issue expensive queries whenever a dashboard loads, create cascading refresh delays, or return unbounded values that make panels slow. Use focused label filters, chained variables where appropriate, sensible defaults, and an All option that does not expand into a huge query. Validate variable behavior when data is empty or when users select multiple values."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q6. When should Grafana transformations be used, and why should they not replace all data-source query logic?",
        "answer": "Grafana transformations manipulate returned data frames to join, filter, rename, calculate, or organize fields for visualization. They are useful when a panel needs presentation-level changes or when combining limited result sets that are not practical to express in one query. However, transformations happen after data has been retrieved, so they do not necessarily reduce backend query cost or network transfer. Complex calculations are often better implemented in PromQL, SQL, or the source system where they can use indexes and aggregation efficiently. Keep transformation chains understandable and test their behavior when query results are missing or change shape."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q7. Explain Grafana unified alerting architecture and how alert evaluation differs from dashboard visualization.",
        "answer": "Unified alerting evaluates alert rules independently of whether a dashboard is open in a browser. Rules query their configured data sources on a schedule, apply conditions and expressions, and move through states such as Normal, Pending, Alerting, and Recovering according to the rule configuration. Notification policies then route alert instances to contact points using labels and grouping rules. This separation means a panel can render successfully while an alert rule fails due to evaluation permissions, query differences, or missing data. Test alert rules directly, inspect evaluation history, and verify notification delivery rather than relying only on dashboard appearance."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. How do Grafana notification policies and contact points work together in a multi-team environment?",
        "answer": "Contact points define where notifications are delivered, such as email, chat platforms, incident-management systems, or webhooks. Notification policies determine which contact point receives an alert based on labels and can apply grouping, timing, and child-policy routing. In a multi-team environment, use consistent labels such as team, service, environment, and severity so ownership is predictable. Configure grouping and repeat intervals to reduce notification storms while preserving critical incident visibility. Test delivery credentials, escalation behavior, grouping keys, and fallback routes, and document who owns each policy."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. How should engineers investigate Grafana alerts that show No Data or Error instead of a normal alert state?",
        "answer": "No Data means the alert evaluation did not receive the expected series or values, while Error indicates that the evaluation encountered a problem such as a failed query, timeout, permission issue, or invalid expression. Inspect the alert rule query, evaluation history, data-source health, query inspector output, and Grafana logs. Confirm whether the metric disappeared because the workload stopped reporting, labels changed, or the time range is incorrect. Configure No Data and Error handling deliberately according to business risk, because treating missing telemetry as healthy can hide outages while paging on harmless gaps creates noise."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. What controls should be used to manage Grafana plugins safely in production?",
        "answer": "Plugins extend Grafana functionality but introduce additional code, compatibility, and supply-chain risk. Install plugins only from trusted sources, review plugin permissions and maintenance status, pin approved versions, and test upgrades in a staging environment. Prefer signed plugins and enforce the organization policy for unsigned plugin installation rather than disabling signature checks broadly. Monitor plugin-related startup errors and resource use, and remove plugins that are no longer required. Include plugin versions in deployment configuration so replicas remain consistent and rollback can restore a known-good plugin set."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q11. How would you optimize a Grafana dashboard that contains hundreds of panels and many concurrent viewers?",
        "answer": "Reduce the number of simultaneously rendered panels and avoid repeating expensive queries across many visualizations. Set reasonable default time ranges and refresh intervals, use dashboard variables to narrow the selected environment or service, and use recording rules or pre-aggregated datasets for commonly requested calculations. Inspect query inspector timings to identify slow panels and review the data-source query limits. Consider splitting one oversized dashboard into focused operational views and use library panels when common visualizations must be reused. Validate the improvements under realistic concurrent load instead of judging performance from a single browser session."
