      {
        "question": "Q12. How do you design Grafana dashboards for Loki logs without creating excessive label cardinality?",
        "answer": "For Loki, labels should represent a small set of stable dimensions such as cluster, namespace, application, and environment. High-cardinality values such as request IDs, user IDs, full URLs, and timestamps should remain in the log body or structured metadata rather than becoming indexed labels. Grafana dashboards should use selective LogQL label matchers before applying content filters so queries scan less data. Provide useful variables for service and namespace selection, and use consistent log fields for correlation. Monitor query performance and ingestion behavior because an unsafe label strategy can increase storage cost and make log queries difficult to operate."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q13. A Grafana panel reports a data-source timeout. How would you identify whether the issue is in Grafana or the backend?",
        "answer": "Use the panel query inspector to capture the actual request, query parameters, execution duration, and returned error. Test the same query directly against the backend using an authorized method from the Grafana network location. If the backend is slow independently, inspect its workload, query plan, storage latency, and resource saturation. If direct requests succeed but Grafana times out, review Grafana proxy settings, data-source timeout configuration, request limits, and network intermediaries. Increase timeouts only after confirming that the query is legitimately expensive and that longer requests will not overload the service."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q14. Explain Grafana RBAC and how you would separate access for platform, application, and business teams.",
