      },
      {
        "question": "Q30. How would you design Grafana dashboards for incident response rather than only executive reporting?",
        "answer": "An incident-response dashboard should help responders identify impact, scope, symptoms, and likely dependencies quickly. Start with service-level indicators such as request rate, error rate, latency, saturation, and availability, then provide drill-down views by cluster, namespace, service, pod, and instance. Include deployment markers, relevant logs, traces, and links to runbooks so engineers can correlate symptoms with recent changes. Use clear units, meaningful thresholds, consistent time ranges, and labels that identify service ownership. Avoid decorative panels that do not support a diagnostic decision. Ensure dashboards remain useful during partial outages by avoiding dependence on a single fragile data source. Test them during game days and real incident reviews, and refine the layout based on how responders actually investigate production failures."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q31. How would you manage Grafana dashboard versioning and prevent accidental loss of important dashboard changes?",
        "answer": "Use version-controlled dashboard definitions and a reviewed promotion workflow for dashboards that are operationally important. Assign stable UIDs, retain meaningful dashboard versions, and document ownership so teams know where changes should be made. If provisioning is used, treat the repository as the source of truth and establish how UI edits are handled to avoid changes being overwritten. Back up dashboard definitions and Grafana configuration through an approved recovery process. Test restoration in a separate environment and verify that data source references, folder placement, permissions, and panel queries are preserved. Keep sensitive credentials out of dashboard exports. Review changes to critical dashboards before production rollout and maintain a known-good version that can be restored if a modification breaks incident-response workflows."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q32. What causes Grafana alert evaluations to fail when the data source is temporarily unavailable?",
        "answer": "An unavailable data source can cause query errors, timeouts, missing results, or evaluation states that differ from the expected healthy or firing behavior. Review Grafana alert evaluation history, rule error details, data source health, and backend logs to determine whether the problem is connectivity, authentication, query execution, or the monitoring backend itself. Configure no-data and error handling deliberately according to the operational meaning of each alert. For example, missing telemetry from a critical service may need a separate monitoring-availability alert rather than being treated as zero traffic. Avoid creating a large storm of service alerts when one shared data source is down. Test outage and recovery behavior, confirm notification routing, and document which team owns the data source dependency."
