      },
      {
        "question": "Q29. How would you manage Jira REST API integrations securely and reliably?",
        "answer": "Use a dedicated integration identity and the authentication method supported by the Jira deployment type. Grant only the project and operation permissions required by the integration. Store tokens in a secret manager or secure CI credential store, rotate them according to policy, and never commit them to source control or print them in logs. Handle pagination, rate limits, timeouts, retries, and transient server errors. Make write operations idempotent where possible so retries do not create duplicate issues or comments. Validate API responses and log correlation identifiers without exposing sensitive issue content. Monitor integration failures and periodically review the account's access."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q30. How would you improve Jira performance when issue searches and boards become slow?",
        "answer": "Establish a baseline using response times, affected operations, user locations, and time windows. Determine whether the issue is instance-wide or isolated to particular projects, filters, dashboards, or integrations. Review expensive JQL, broad filters, excessive custom fields, automation activity, application logs, database health, and infrastructure metrics where available. Check whether board filters use inefficient functions or search across unnecessary projects. For Data Center deployments, inspect node health, clustering, shared storage, and database connectivity. Apply changes in a test environment, measure their effect, and coordinate infrastructure or database tuning with the platform team. Avoid speculative changes to production configuration."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q31. How would you implement Jira change management with approvals and deployment evidence?",
        "answer": "Create a change issue type or workflow that captures the affected service, risk, implementation plan, rollback plan, testing evidence, and planned deployment window. Define approval transitions based on risk and the responsible change authority. Integrate CI/CD so that successful build and deployment identifiers can be attached to the change record. Ensure that production deployment is blocked when mandatory approvals or evidence are missing, while maintaining a controlled emergency-change process. Record who approved and executed each transition. Validate the workflow using standard, rejected, postponed, and emergency change scenarios, and report on failed changes and approval lead time."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q32. How would you plan a Jira migration or major upgrade with minimal business disruption?",
        "answer": "Inventory projects, users, groups, workflows, custom fields, issue links, attachments, apps, integrations, and reporting dependencies. Confirm the source and target product types, supported versions, migration paths, licensing, and compatibility of marketplace applications. Create a representative test migration and compare issue counts, attachments, permissions, workflows, and critical reports. Plan a change freeze or controlled delta migration, communicate downtime, and prepare a rollback strategy. Back up the source and validate the target before switching users. After cutover, test authentication, issue creation, search, boards, automation, API integrations, and notifications. Keep migration evidence and obtain business-owner sign-off."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q33. How would you implement Jira audit and compliance controls for a regulated organization?",
        "answer": "Define which activities must be auditable, including permission changes, workflow changes, administrative actions, issue updates, approvals, and integration activity. Use the audit capabilities available in the deployed Jira product and edition, and forward relevant logs to a protected centralized logging platform where supported. Restrict administrative privileges, review access periodically, and ensure that sensitive projects use appropriate issue security. Define retention and access policies for audit records, and protect logs against unauthorized modification. Establish evidence collection for change approvals and production deployments. Test that required events are captured and that auditors can reconstruct who changed what and when."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q34. How would you design Jira service management escalation for high-severity production incidents?",
        "answer": "Define severity levels using measurable customer impact, affected services, and business criticality. Configure request types, queues, SLAs, and escalation rules so that high-severity incidents reach the correct on-call responders quickly. Capture incident commander, communication channel, affected service, timeline, and mitigation details. Integrate approved alerting and monitoring systems so alerts can create or update incidents without generating uncontrolled duplicates. Configure stakeholder notifications and escalation when response or resolution targets are at risk. Test after-hours routing, responder absence, duplicate alerts, and service dependencies. Review incident metrics and post-incident feedback to improve the process."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q35. How would you establish Jira governance and reporting across multiple engineering teams?",
