        "question": "Q13. Explain Tomcat security hardening practices for production servers exposed through internal or public networks.",
        "answer": "Production Tomcat hardening includes running the process under a dedicated non-root account, restricting network exposure, enforcing TLS where appropriate, removing unused examples and default applications, and limiting administrative interfaces. Review Manager and Host Manager access, authentication, role assignments, and network restrictions. Keep Tomcat, Java, and application dependencies on supported patched versions. Protect configuration files, keystores, credentials, logs, and deployment directories with appropriate filesystem permissions. Disable unnecessary connectors and services, configure secure cookie attributes and relevant response headers, and review server information disclosure. Use vulnerability scanning and security monitoring, then validate that legitimate application behavior continues to work after each hardening change."
