      {
        "question": "Q3. A production Docker container cannot connect to another container on the same host. How do you troubleshoot?",
        "answer": "Inspect docker network ls, docker network inspect, and docker inspect for both containers. Confirm they are attached to the intended user-defined network and that the application listens on the expected interface and port. Test name resolution using the container service name and inspect whether the application binds only to 127.0.0.1 instead of 0.0.0.0. Check host firewall rules, network aliases, and port configuration. Avoid relying on container IP addresses because they can change. Correct network membership or listener configuration, validate connectivity from the calling container, and document the service discovery design."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q4. A newly deployed Docker image works in testing but fails in production with a missing configuration error. What would you check?",
        "answer": "Compare the exact image digest, entrypoint, command, environment variables, mounted configuration files, secrets, and working directory between environments. Inspect docker inspect and container logs to determine which setting is missing. Verify that production deployment tooling supplies the expected variables and mounts, and that the container user can read required files. Do not bake production secrets into the image as a shortcut. Reproduce the production configuration in a controlled environment, correct the deployment manifest or configuration source, and redeploy using an immutable image digest. Add startup validation for mandatory settings."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q5. Docker containers on a production host cannot start because the disk is full. How would you recover safely?",
        "answer": "Check df -h and df -i, then inspect Docker storage usage with docker system df and identify large container logs, images, writable layers, and unused volumes. Determine whether the issue is filesystem capacity, inode exhaustion, or a rapidly growing application log. Do not run broad prune commands blindly because they may remove resources needed for rollback or recovery. Rotate or truncate logs using the approved procedure, remove only verified unused images and containers, and expand storage if required. Validate that production data volumes remain intact. Configure log limits, image retention, and disk alerts."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q6. A production Docker deployment reports that the container is healthy, but users receive application errors. How do you investigate?",
        "answer": "Check whether the configured health check tests only process existence or actually verifies application readiness. Inspect container logs, application metrics, dependency connectivity, and external endpoint responses. A container can remain running while its database pool is exhausted or its business endpoints fail. Compare health-check results with load balancer target health and synthetic user requests. Define separate liveness and readiness checks where supported by the deployment platform. Correct the underlying dependency or application issue and verify that unhealthy instances are removed from traffic. Avoid health checks that cause restart loops during temporary dependency outages."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q7. A Docker image deployment unexpectedly introduces a critical vulnerability into production. What is your response?",
        "answer": "Identify the deployed image digest, affected containers, vulnerability details, and exposure using image scan reports and deployment records. Determine whether the vulnerable component is reachable and whether an exploit is known or observed. Coordinate containment with security and application owners, and prioritize upgrading or replacing the affected base image and dependency. Build a patched image through the trusted pipeline, scan it, test compatibility, and deploy using a controlled rollout. Preserve the original image and evidence for investigation. Add base-image pinning, automated scanning, patch SLAs, and release gates to prevent recurrence."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. A Docker container starts successfully but becomes unresponsive after several hours. How would you diagnose it?",
        "answer": "Capture application logs, container metrics, process statistics, and thread or runtime diagnostics while the container is unresponsive. Check CPU throttling, memory growth, file descriptors, connection pools, disk I/O, and network sockets. Inspect whether the application is blocked on a downstream service, deadlocked, or leaking resources. Compare healthy and unhealthy container instances and correlate the onset with traffic or scheduled jobs. Restart only after capturing useful evidence and confirming impact. Fix the resource leak or blocking dependency, then run a soak test that exceeds the previous failure interval."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. A production Docker container cannot resolve an internal service hostname. What steps do you take?",
        "answer": "Inspect the container's /etc/resolv.conf, Docker network configuration, DNS settings, and service discovery mechanism. Test resolution with getent hosts or an approved DNS utility from inside the container. Verify that the service name or network alias is correct and that both containers share the expected user-defined network. Check custom DNS server reachability, host resolver configuration, and firewall restrictions. Determine whether the failure affects all containers or only one network. Correct the DNS or network configuration and validate repeated lookups after container recreation. Avoid hardcoding changing container IP addresses."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. A Docker container cannot write uploaded files to its mounted production volume. How do you resolve it?",
        "answer": "Inspect docker inspect mounts to confirm the host path, container destination, mount mode, and whether the mount is read-only. Check host and container ownership, numeric UID and GID, filesystem permissions, disk capacity, and security controls such as SELinux where applicable. Confirm that the application runs as the expected non-root user. Avoid solving the issue with chmod 777 or running the entire container as root. Correct ownership or group access using the approved host procedure, test file creation and persistence, and validate behavior after container recreation. Document volume ownership requirements in deployment configuration."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q11. A production Docker container cannot pull an image from a private registry. What would you investigate?",
        "answer": "Check the exact registry hostname, repository path, image tag or digest, and authentication error. Verify that the deployment identity has permission to pull the image and that credentials are current and stored securely. Inspect DNS, proxy settings, TLS certificate trust, firewall rules, and outbound connectivity from the Docker host. Review registry availability and rate limits. Test authentication using the same deployment identity rather than a personal administrator account. Correct the missing permission or network dependency, then retry the pull and verify the image digest. Add registry availability monitoring and credential rotation procedures."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q12. A production Docker host experiences high CPU utilization after many containers are deployed. How do you identify the cause?",
        "answer": "Use docker stats to compare per-container CPU usage, then inspect host-level processes with top or pidstat and correlate container IDs to host PIDs. Determine whether CPU consumption is caused by application load, runaway processes, excessive logging, compression, garbage collection, or a busy retry loop. Review CPU quotas and throttling metrics to understand whether containers are competing for capacity. Check recent deployments and traffic changes. Apply a controlled resource limit or scale workload only after checking application and downstream capacity. Validate host load and request latency after remediation, and add per-container resource dashboards."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q13. A production Docker application loses data after its container is recreated. How would you prevent this?",
        "answer": "Inspect the container configuration to determine whether important data was written only to the container writable layer. Identify database files, uploads, generated reports, and other persistent state that must survive container replacement. Move persistent data to named volumes or approved external storage, and verify backup and restore procedures. Do not assume that an image layer or stopped container is a durable backup. Migrate existing data carefully, validate ownership and application paths, and test container deletion and recreation in a non-production environment. Document which paths are ephemeral and which are persistent."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q14. A Docker container fails with permission denied when executing its startup script. What is your response?",
        "answer": "Inspect the image entrypoint, command, script path, file ownership, executable bit, mount overrides, and runtime user. Check whether a bind mount replaced a script that was executable in the image with a host file that lacks execute permissions. Verify line endings and interpreter availability, and inspect security controls that may block execution. Rebuild the image with correct ownership and permissions or correct the mounted file through source control. Avoid running as root without a security assessment. Validate startup using the same user and mounts used in production."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q15. A production Docker container receives SIGTERM during deployment but does not shut down gracefully. How do you investigate?",
        "answer": "Check the container stop timeout, application signal handling, PID 1 process, entrypoint script, and child-process management. If a shell script runs the application without exec, signals may not reach the actual application process correctly. Verify that the application handles SIGTERM, stops accepting new work, completes or safely cancels in-flight requests, and closes connections. Review orchestration termination grace periods and load-balancer draining. Correct the entrypoint and shutdown logic, then test termination during active requests. Increase the grace period only when measured shutdown requirements justify it."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q16. A production Docker deployment causes containers to run with the wrong image version. What controls would you implement?",
        "answer": "Inspect deployment manifests, image tags, registry contents, CI build metadata, and the actual image digest reported by the running container. Mutable tags such as latest can point to different content over time and make rollback difficult. Publish immutable versioned tags and deploy by digest where practical. Record source commit, build ID, and artifact provenance. Ensure the deployment pipeline promotes the tested image instead of rebuilding a different image for production. Add admission or deployment validation that checks the approved digest, then verify the running workload after rollout."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q17. A production Docker host has many exited containers and unused images. How would you clean it without disrupting services?",
        "answer": "Inventory containers, images, volumes, networks, and their relationships before cleanup. Use docker ps -a and docker system df to understand what consumes storage. Identify stopped containers that are no longer required, images not referenced by active or rollback deployments, and volumes with confirmed ownership. Do not use volume-prune operations without verifying that no persistent data is needed. Schedule cleanup through change control, preserve required logs and evidence, and validate active services afterward. Implement image retention, automated cleanup of known ephemeral resources, and storage-capacity monitoring."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q18. A Dockerized production application has intermittent connection resets to an external API. How would you troubleshoot?",
        "answer": "Capture timestamps, request IDs, destination host, response codes, connection duration, and retry behavior from the application. Check DNS results, TLS handshake, proxy configuration, host networking, connection tracking, and outbound firewall rules. Determine whether resets originate from the remote API, an intermediate proxy, or the local container host. Inspect connection pool reuse, idle timeout mismatch, and excessive concurrency. Apply bounded retries with jitter only for operations safe to retry, and set appropriate connect and read timeouts. Validate with controlled traffic and monitor reset rates and end-to-end latency."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q19. A production Docker container consumes more memory than expected even though the application heap appears normal. What would you inspect?",
        "answer": "Compare container memory usage with runtime heap metrics and inspect native memory, direct buffers, thread stacks, shared libraries, page cache, and child processes. Check whether the container memory limit is configured and whether the application runtime is aware of cgroup limits. For Java applications, compare -Xmx with the container limit and include Metaspace, direct memory, and native overhead. For other runtimes, inspect allocator and process metrics. Capture diagnostics before increasing limits. Correct native leaks or runtime sizing and validate the full memory footprint under load."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q20. A Docker container reports address already in use during production startup. How do you isolate the issue?",
        "answer": "Inspect the container's port configuration and determine whether the application is trying to bind to a port already used by another process inside the container or on the host. Use ss or netstat on the relevant network namespace and inspect docker ps port mappings. Check whether multiple replicas are incorrectly binding the same host port or whether an old process remains alive. Distinguish container ports from published host ports. Correct the listener or port mapping and validate service discovery and load balancing. Avoid changing production ports without updating dependent health checks and firewall rules."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q21. A production Docker image takes several minutes to pull during deployment. How would you optimize rollout time?",
        "answer": "Inspect image size, layer composition, registry latency, host bandwidth, and image cache behavior. Identify large build dependencies, unnecessary package caches, debug tools, and duplicated files. Use multi-stage builds to keep runtime images small and order Dockerfile layers so stable dependencies can be cached. Pin base images and use a trusted registry mirror or regional registry where appropriate. Ensure parallel pulls do not overwhelm the registry or network. Measure pull and startup time before and after changes, and validate that security scanning and reproducibility remain intact."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q22. A Docker container starts with the wrong timezone and causes incorrect timestamps in production logs. How would you correct it?",
