      {
        "question": "Q2. A production hotfix was committed directly to main, but the same fix is missing from the active release branch. What would you do?",
        "answer": "Identify the exact hotfix commit with git log and confirm that it contains only the intended change. Inspect the release branch history and determine whether the commit depends on other changes from main. Use git cherry-pick <commit> on a controlled release branch when the change is independent, resolving conflicts carefully. Run targeted tests and review the resulting diff before pushing. Avoid merging all of main merely to obtain one hotfix. Verify the release artifact contains the fix and record the commit relationship for later branch synchronization."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q3. A production deployment contains code from an incorrect Git commit because the pipeline checked out the wrong branch. How do you investigate?",
        "answer": "Capture the deployed artifact version, Git SHA, branch name, Jenkins or CI checkout logs, and deployment timestamp. Inspect the pipeline trigger, branch filters, checkout configuration, default branch settings, and any shared-library logic that selects a revision. Compare the deployed SHA with the approved release commit. Stop further promotion and redeploy the verified artifact or roll back to the last known-good release. Add an explicit branch and commit verification stage before packaging, and record immutable commit metadata in every artifact."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q4. A developer accidentally commits passwords and API keys to a Git repository. What is your production incident response?",
