      },
      {
        "question": "Q5. A production Git repository becomes unavailable because its storage volume is full. How would you restore service?",
        "answer": "Confirm the repository host's filesystem usage, inode availability, Git service health, and storage alerts. Identify whether growth comes from repository objects, LFS files, backups, logs, temporary pack files, or unrelated data. Preserve repository data and take a verified backup before cleanup. Use supported repository maintenance and garbage-collection procedures rather than manually deleting object files. Check for interrupted maintenance or active pushes before running repacking operations. Restore storage headroom, verify clone, fetch, and push operations, and introduce capacity alerts and retention policies."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q6. A merge to the production branch introduces a severe regression. How do you safely revert it?",
        "answer": "Identify the exact merge commit and assess whether subsequent commits depend on its changes. Inspect the diff and affected release behavior before selecting a recovery strategy. For a normal commit, use git revert <commit>; for a merge commit, determine the correct mainline parent before using git revert -m. Create a reviewed revert commit rather than rewriting shared production history. Run regression tests and deploy the reverted revision through the standard pipeline. Verify customer-facing behavior and document the original change and corrective action."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q7. Two developers merge conflicting changes into a shared release branch and the application no longer builds. What is your approach?",
        "answer": "Inspect the merge commit, conflict resolution, build logs, and changed files. Identify whether conflict markers, incorrect combined logic, or incompatible interfaces caused the failure. Reproduce the build from a clean checkout of the exact merge SHA. Coordinate with both change owners and create a corrective commit or revert the faulty merge if recovery is urgent. Run unit, integration, and deployment validation before promotion. Improve mandatory merge-request reviews, conflict testing, and protected branch policies."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. A production Git tag points to the wrong commit and a release pipeline has already consumed it. How do you recover?",
        "answer": "Record the tag name, referenced SHA, pipeline runs, artifact digest, and environments already deployed. Determine whether the tag is annotated or lightweight and inspect the intended release commit. Do not silently move a published release tag because consumers may have fetched the original reference. Create a corrected, clearly versioned release tag or follow the organization's approved tag-repair policy. Rebuild and validate the correct artifact, then promote it through change control. Communicate the correction to downstream consumers and enforce immutable release tags."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. A Git clone fails for all CI agents after an SSH key rotation. What would you investigate?",
        "answer": "Inspect the exact SSH authentication error, repository URL, agent identity, key file permissions, ssh-agent state, and known_hosts entry. Confirm the new public key is registered with the Git server and has access to the required repository. Test connectivity from a controlled CI agent using the same service identity without printing private key material. Check whether the host key also changed during the migration. Update credentials through the approved secret store and validate clone and fetch operations. Add key-expiry and access-validation procedures."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. A large Git repository takes several hours to clone in production build agents. How would you reduce checkout time?",
        "answer": "Measure clone duration, repository size, object count, network throughput, LFS downloads, and server-side Git performance. Determine whether builds require full history, tags, submodules, or large binary assets. Use shallow clones or partial clone filters only when the build and versioning process supports them. Evaluate Git LFS or artifact storage for large binaries instead of committing generated packages. Consider repository mirrors close to CI agents. Validate that version metadata and release operations still work, then monitor checkout time and repository growth."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q11. A production Git merge request contains an accidental deletion of critical configuration files. How do you prevent it from reaching production?",
        "answer": "Review the merge request diff and identify the deleted paths and their runtime purpose. Block approval and inspect commit history to determine whether the deletion is intentional or caused by an incorrect rebase or cleanup. Restore the required files from the correct base revision and review ownership, permissions, and environment-specific content. Run configuration validation and deployment tests before merging. Add CODEOWNERS or equivalent review requirements for critical paths, and introduce automated checks that detect removal of mandatory production configuration."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q12. A Git rebase causes commits to disappear from a developer's local branch during an urgent release. How would you recover?",
        "answer": "Stop additional resets, rebases, and garbage collection on the affected clone. Inspect git reflog, branch references, and dangling commits to identify the pre-rebase commit and the missing work. Create a safety branch at the recovered SHA before making changes. Compare the recovered commits with the remote release branch and identify which changes are already present. Reapply only missing work using cherry-pick or a carefully planned rebase. Validate the resulting history and tests before pushing, then explain recovery practices and recommend temporary backup branches before complex history operations."
