        "number": 21,
        "question": "Ansible selects the wrong Python interpreter on a managed host",
        "answer": "Treat this as an Ansible automation incident: Python-based modules fail even though Python is installed, often with import or syntax errors. Begin with evidence collection: Use verbose output to see interpreter discovery and inspect the available Python paths and versions on the affected host. The main cause to confirm is this: Automatic discovery may select an older interpreter or one without the required libraries. Use a controlled remediation: Set ansible_python_interpreter to a supported existing path for the affected host group and install required dependencies through the approved image or package process. Before closing the incident, Run Ansible ping and a harmless fact-gathering task on hosts representing each operating system family."
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "question": "Ansible modules fail because Python is absent on a minimal host",
        "answer": "Treat this as an Ansible automation incident: SSH succeeds but normal modules fail before configuration begins because the target lacks Python. Begin with evidence collection: Confirm the operating system and package manager, then inspect whether the image has Python or an approved bootstrap mechanism. The main cause to confirm is this: Minimal cloud or appliance images may omit Python, which many POSIX Ansible modules require. Use a controlled remediation: Use the raw module only for a controlled bootstrap step to install the supported interpreter, or bake Python into the machine image. Before closing the incident, After bootstrap, run Ansible ping and fact gathering; document the interpreter requirement in image validation."
