      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "Linux application experiences high storage I/O wait",
        "answer": "Treat this as a Linux host incident: CPU utilization appears moderate but requests stall and the host reports elevated I/O wait. Begin with evidence collection: Use iostat -xz, pidstat -d, device metrics, kernel logs, and cloud volume performance statistics to identify the device and workload. The main cause to confirm is this: A saturated volume, high queue depth, slow storage, backup job, or noisy neighbor may be delaying I/O completion. Use a controlled remediation: Reduce unnecessary I/O, separate competing workloads, or scale the storage tier after validating the bottleneck. Do not disable durability features blindly. Before closing the incident, Measure device latency and application response time under normal load and confirm both return within service objectives."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "Linux host cannot resolve internal or external DNS names",
        "answer": "Treat this as a Linux host incident: Applications fail by hostname while direct connections to known IP addresses may still work. Begin with evidence collection: Inspect resolv.conf, systemd-resolved, getent hosts, dig, search domains, DNS server reachability, and resolver logs. The main cause to confirm is this: Incorrect resolver configuration, unreachable DNS servers, stale records, or a broken local stub resolver may cause lookup failures. Use a controlled remediation: Restore the intended DNS configuration and network path using the environment's authoritative settings. Do not replace internal DNS with an arbitrary public resolver. Before closing the incident, Resolve representative internal and external names from the application host and verify application requests succeed."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "Linux server cannot connect to a remote IP and port",
        "answer": "Treat this as a Linux host incident: A TCP connection fails even when DNS is bypassed and the destination IP is known. Begin with evidence collection: Inspect ip addr, ip route, firewall rules, security groups, network ACLs, listener state, and test the port with nc or an approved TCP probe. The main cause to confirm is this: Routing, filtering, an incorrect subnet, or a stopped destination service may block the connection. Use a controlled remediation: Correct the specific route, policy, or listener with the network owner; avoid disabling the entire host firewall. Before closing the incident, Test from the original source host and confirm bidirectional connectivity to the exact destination port."
