        "answer": "Treat this as a Linux host incident: The server hangs or drops to a recovery prompt after selecting the newly installed kernel. Begin with evidence collection: Use console access to select a previous known-good kernel and inspect boot logs, initramfs, root drivers, modules, and package transaction history. The main cause to confirm is this: The new kernel may lack a required driver or compatible module, or the initramfs and boot parameters may be incorrect. Use a controlled remediation: Restore bootability with the previous kernel and rebuild or reinstall compatible kernel components through the supported process. Before closing the incident, Boot the repaired kernel in a maintenance window and verify storage, networking, and critical services before retiring the fallback kernel."
      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "question": "Linux enters emergency mode because a mount failed",
        "answer": "Treat this as a Linux host incident: Boot stops in emergency mode and reports a failed local filesystem or mount dependency. Begin with evidence collection: Inspect journal messages, /etc/fstab, blkid UUIDs, findmnt, and availability of the referenced storage device. The main cause to confirm is this: A stale UUID, missing volume, invalid mount option, or filesystem problem may block a required boot mount. Use a controlled remediation: Restore the missing device or correct the persistent mount definition. Run filesystem repair only on an appropriately unmounted filesystem and with the approved recovery procedure. Before closing the incident, Reboot a test or recovered host and verify every required mount is present and services start normally."
